2503:
** ἰῶτα** [ῐ], τό,
_indeclinable_, (Hebrew **yôd**) **the letter** ι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 418b, Aen. Tact. 31.18, Call.Grammars cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.453d, etc.}}
**2** **line, stroke**, on a sundial, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Bull.Soc.Alex.” 4.83 (3rd c.BC).}}
**3** proverbial, of anything very small, **smallest letter, jot,** {{NT|NT.Matt.5.18.}}

2505:
** κᾰθά** [θᾰ],
_adverb_ for καθ᾽ ἅ, **according as, just as,** {{LXX+4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.90.43, 116.27, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 551, “PCair.Zen.” 188.8 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.107.10, LXX.Gen.7.9, etc.}}; ὁ κ. παρατεταγμένος σφυγμός the (**so to speak**) 'regular' pulse (a military metaphor), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.626.}}
**II** also **καθάπερ**, _Ionic dialect_ **κατάπερ**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 14, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 164, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.182, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 8, “Ec.” 61, “IG” 12.39.42, +others, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.16, etc.}}: frequently in legal instruments, ἡ πρᾶξις ἔστω κ. ἐκ δίκης {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“PEleph.” 1.12 (4th c.BC)}}, etc.: with a _participle_, like{ὡς},{ἅτε}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.54 (nisi to be read καίπερ)}}:—strengthened, **καθάπερ** εἰ (_Ionic dialect_ κατάπερ εἰ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.170)}}, **like as if, exactly as**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 22e, 59e, +others}}; **καθάπερ** ἄν (for ἐάν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.41}}; **καθάπερ** ἂν εἰ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 684c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 240b10, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.32.2, etc.:}} —μάχαιραν κ. **like** a knife, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “adversus Christianos - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 31. Cf. καθό, καθώς.}}

2506:
** καθαίρ-εσις**, εως, ἡ,
**pulling down, demolition**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.66, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.15, “IG” 22.1672.75 (4th c.BC), “PMagd.” 9.6 (3rd c.BC), etc.: _metaphorically_, τινῶν, opposed to οἰκοδομή, NT.2Cor.10.8}}; ἀναστήσωμεν τὴν κ. τοῦ λαοῦ ἡμῶν {{LXX|LXX.1Mac.3.43: in concrete sense, αἱ καθαιρέσεις}} **the débris**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 92.31.}}
**2** generally, **overthrow, subjugation**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Caesares” 320d; τῆς ἀνέτου ἐξουσίας 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.4.4; Ἰουλιανοῦ [prev. author] 3.1.1}}; **killing**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 82.}}
**3** **reduction, diminution**, opposed to πρόσθεσις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 207a23}}: in Medicine texts, **bringing down superfluous flesh, lowering, reducing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.3.1, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).368; τῶν σωμάτων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 738a31; τῶν ὄγκων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 58e.}}
**4** **eclipse** of sun or moon (with reference to the magical process of **drawing down** those bodies), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Scholia 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.533 (_plural_).}}

2507:
** καθαιρ-έω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κατ-**, _Aeolic dialect_ **κατάγρημι**, which see: _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.453, etc.}}: _future_ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2 καθελῶ “APl.” 4.334 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus):}}_aorist 2_ καθεῖλον, _infinitive_ καθελεῖν: _aorist 1_ καθεῖλα {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.19.14}}: _Ionic dialect_ _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ καταραιρημένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.172}}:—**take down**, καθείλομεν ἱστία {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.149; κὰδ δ᾽ ἀπὸ πασσαλόφι ζυγὸν ᾕρεον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.268}}; κ. ἄχθος **take** it **down**, i.e. off one's shoulders, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 10; κ. τὸ σημεῖον 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.36}}; κ. τῶν ἐκ τῆς στοᾶς ὅπλων some of them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.8; κ. εἰκόνα ἐξ ἀκροπόλεως 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 117}}; κ. τινά, from the cross, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.86.6, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.529:}} —_middle_, κατελέσθαι τὰ τόξα **take down one's** bow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.78; τοὺς ἱστούς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.61.1.}}
**2** **put down, close** the eyes of the dead, ὄσσε καθαιρήσουσι θανόντι περ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.453; ὀφθαλμοὺς καθελοῦσα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.296; χερσὶ κατ᾽ ὀφθαλμοὺς ἑλέειν 11.426.}}
**3** of sorcerers, **bring down** from the sky, σελήνην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 750, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 513a.}}
**4** κατά με πέδον γᾶς ἕλοι may earth **swallow** me! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 829 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **put down by force, destroy**, ὅτε κέν μιν μοῖρ᾽ ὀλοὴ καθέλῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.100, 19.145, compare 3.238, etc.; μὴ καθέλοι μιν αἰών 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.60; φῶτ᾽ ἄδικον καθαιρεῖ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 398 (Lyric poetry); μοῖρα τὸν φύσαντα καθεῖλε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 517, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 878 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; **kill, slay**, ταῦρον{{Refs 7th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1143, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Stesichorus Lyricus 23, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1063, “fragment” 205}}; ἐάν τις ἀποκτείνῃ.. ἐν ὁδῷ καθελών Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.53}}:—_passive_, of criminals, **to be executed**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 22.}}
**II.2** **put down, reduce**, κ. Κῦρον καὶ τὴν Περσέων δύναμιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71, etc.}}; καθαιρεθῆναι, opposed to ἀρθῆναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.8}}; especially **depose, dethrone**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.124, etc.}}; κ. τὸ λῃστικὸν ἐκ τῆς θαλάσσης **remove** it **utterly** from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.4, compare “POxy.” 1408.23 (3rd c.AD)}}; κ. ὕβριν τινός {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.27, LXX.Zech.9.6; ὄλβον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 646.4; ὑπερηφάνους Aristeas 263:—}}_passive_, καθῃρημένος τὴν αἴσθησιν **bereft** of sense, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 38}}; καθαιρεῖσθαι τῆς μεγαλειότητος [Ἀρτέμιδος] {{NT|NT.Act.19.27.}}
**II.3** **raze to the ground, demolish**, πόλεις {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.58, +others, LXX.Isa.14.17; τείχη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 244c}}; τῶν τειχῶν a part of the walls, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.13}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.39, etc.; καθῃρέθη.. Οἰχαλία δορί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 478.}}
**II.4** **cancel, rescind**, τὸ Μεγαρέων ψήφισμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140, compare 139, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 29; ἔργον κ. λόγῳ 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 140.}}
**II.5** as law-term, **condemn**, ἡ καθαιροῦσα ψῆφος a verdict of **guilty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.37}}: with _infinitive_, ἐμὲ πάλος καθαιρεῖ.. λαβεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 275; so probably κατά με.. Ἀΐδας ἕλοι πατρὶ ξυνθανεῖν [prev. author] “OC” 1689 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 862}}; simply, **decide**, ὅ τι ἂν αἱ πλείους ψῆφοι καθαιρῶσι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.36, 39}}; in book-keeping, ἃν καθαιρῶσιν αἱ ψῆφοι whatever the counters (or accounts) **prove**, probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.227.}}
**II.6** **reduce**, τῶν αὐξανομένων καὶ καθαιρουμένων γραμμῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 237b9; τοῦ ἀποστήματος πεφυκότος ἐπὶ πολὺ καθαιρεῖν τὰ μεγέθη 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 9}}; of mild caustics, τὰ ὑπερσαρκέοντα καθαιρεῖ (probably to be read instead of καθαίρει) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἑλκῶν” 14, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.756; τὸ σῶμα κ. διαίταις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 53}}: Rhet., **minimize**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1376a34.}}
**III** **overpower, seize**, κὰδ δέ μιν ὕπνος ᾕρει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.372}}; κ. τινά **overtake**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.16}}; κ. τινὰ ἐν ἀφροσύνῃ **catch** in the act of folly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 383 (_anapaest meter_): with _genitive_ partis, κ. τῶν ὤτων}} **seize by**.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.133}}:—_passive_, κ. ὑπό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.29.}}
**IV** **fetch down as a reward** or **prize**, καθαιρεῖν ἀγῶνας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 8}}: _metaphorically_, **achieve**, ἀγώνιον.. εὖχος ἔργῳ καθελών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).63:}}_future_ _infinitive_ καθαρεῖν, παστόν, μίτραν{{Refs|epigram in “Berl.Sitzb.” 1894.908 (Asia Minor)}}:—_middle_, φόνῳ καθαιρεῖσθ᾽, οὐ λόγῳ, τὰ πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 749}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.50.}}
**V** less frequently like the simple αἱρεῖν, **take and carry off**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.41, compare 5.36 (_passive_). Cf. καθαίρω.}}

2508:
** κᾰθαίρω**,
_future_ κᾰθᾰρῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 18.6, probably in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 735b, etc.}}: _aorist 1_ ἐκάθηρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.152, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.104, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.47, “IG” 22.1672.47 (ἀνα-), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.119, etc.}}; ἐκάθᾱρα is found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.37, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.35, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.” (“Sp.”) 11}}, and commonly in later Gr., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 16.7, “BCH” 6.23 (Delos, 2nd c.BC), (ἀνα-) “PPetr.” 3p.141 (3rd c.BC), (συνανα-) “IG” 11(2).163 A 56 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), compare διακαθαίρω, ἐκκαθαίρω, etc., and 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 16}}; see below _middle_: _perfect_ κεκάθαρκα (ἐκ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 752}}:— _middle_, _future_ καθαροῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 396e, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.38 (in _passive_ sense, [prev. work] 2.13, “Nat.Mul.” 13, “Mul.” 2.160)}}: _aorist_ ἐκαθηράμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 354, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.43, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 868a, “IG” 11(2).146 A 78 (Delos, 4th c.BC), 153.9, 154 A 37 ([prev. passage], 3rd c.BC)}}; later ἐκαθᾱράμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work]146 A 80 (4th c.BC), “Inscription Délos” 290.79, +others (3rd c.BC), etc., (ἀνα-) “IG” 22.1668.8(4th c.BC)}}: _passive_, _future_ καθαρθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 7.26.64, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.222}}: _aorist_ ἐκαθάρθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.43, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.104, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.2, etc. (_aorist 2_ ἀποκαθαρῇ}} is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Cynegeticus” 27.1)}}: _perfect_ κεκάθαρμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c, etc.}}: (καθαρός):
**I** **cleanse**, of things, καθήρατε δὲ κρητῆρας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.152; τραπέζας ὕδατι.. καθαίρειν 22.439; καθήραντες χρόα καλὸν ὕδατι 24.44; κ. οἰκίαν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator [same place], 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus}} [prev. cited]; of wounds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἑλκῶν” 6, +others (compare καθαιρέω 11.6): with _genitive_, ἵππον αὐχμηρᾶς τριχός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 475; κ. σῖτον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 18.6}}; γῆν **clear** of weeds,{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 20.11, compare “PLille” 5.24 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; χρυσόν **purify, refine**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 303d}}: _metaphorically_, **purge, clear** a land of monsters and robbers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1012 (hexameters), 1061, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 7; κ.λῃστηρίων τὴν ἐπαρχίαν [prev. author] “Mar.” 6: c._accusative_ _cognate_, καθαρμὸν κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 735b}}:—_passive_, τὴν νηδὺν ἀνασχισθεῖσαν καὶ καθαρθεῖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.71.}}
**I.2** in religious sense, **purify**, [δέπας] ἐκάθηρε θεείῳ by fumigating with sulphur, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.228}}; κ. τινὰ φόνου **purify** him **from** blood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.44, compare “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.160 (from Cyrene)}}; Δῆλον κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.64, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8}}; στόλον κ., Latin _classem lustrare_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.96: _absolutely_, “IG” 5(1).1390.68 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}:— _middle_, **purify oneself, get purified**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.73; οἱ φιλοσοφίᾳ καθηράμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114c, compare “Phdr.” 243a, “Cra.” 396e; καθαίρεσθαι καθαρμούς [prev. author] “Lg.” 868e}}; καθήρασθαι στόμα **keep one's** tongue **pure**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 354}}:—_passive_, κεκαθαρμένος καὶ τετελεσμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c.}}
**I.3** in Medicine texts, **purge, evacuate**, either by purgatives or emetics, κ. κάτω ἢ ἄνω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.64 (_passive_),compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.11.11, etc.}}:—_middle_, κατὰ κύστιν ἐκαθήρατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.15:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 5.2}}, etc.; also of menstruation, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Superf.” 33}}; of the after-birth, τὰ λοχεῖα κ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mul.” 1.78}}; καθαίρων, ὁ, name for ἶρις, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.1.}}
**I.4** **prune** a tree,i.e.**clear** it of superfluous wood, {{NT|NT.John.15.2.}}
**I.5** **sift, winnow** grain, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 373.10 (2nd c.AD).}}
**I.6** _metaphorically_, ={μαστιγόω}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.119.}}
**II** of the thing removed by purification, **purge away, wash off**, λύματα πάντα κάθηρεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.171; ἐπεὶ πλῦνάν τε κάθηράν τε ῥύπα πάντα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.93}}; **clear away**, τὰ λῃστικά {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.52: _metaphorically_, φόνον κ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 74 (Lyric poetry)}}; also perhaps, **clear up, explain** an action, τὴν σύστασιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 66G., compare 73G.}}
**III** with _double_ _accusative_, αἷμα κάθηρον.. Σαρπηδόνα **cleanse** him **of** blood, **wash** the blood **off** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.667:}} —_passive_, καθαίρομαι γῆρας I **am purged of** old age, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 45; ὁ καθαρθεὶς τὸν φόνον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.43.}}

2509:
Included with: ** κᾰθά** [θᾰ],
_adverb_ for καθ᾽ ἅ, **according as, just as,** {{LXX+4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.90.43, 116.27, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 551, “PCair.Zen.” 188.8 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.107.10, LXX.Gen.7.9, etc.}}; ὁ κ. παρατεταγμένος σφυγμός the (**so to speak**) 'regular' pulse (a military metaphor), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.626.}}
**II** also **καθάπερ**, _Ionic dialect_ **κατάπερ**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 14, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 164, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.182, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 8, “Ec.” 61, “IG” 12.39.42, +others, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.16, etc.}}: frequently in legal instruments, ἡ πρᾶξις ἔστω κ. ἐκ δίκης {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“PEleph.” 1.12 (4th c.BC)}}, etc.: with a _participle_, like{ὡς},{ἅτε}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.54 (nisi to be read καίπερ)}}:—strengthened, **καθάπερ** εἰ (_Ionic dialect_ κατάπερ εἰ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.170)}}, **like as if, exactly as**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 22e, 59e, +others}}; **καθάπερ** ἄν (for ἐάν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.41}}; **καθάπερ** ἂν εἰ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 684c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 240b10, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.32.2, etc.:}} —μάχαιραν κ. **like** a knife, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “adversus Christianos - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 31. Cf. καθό, καθώς.}}

2510:
** καθάπτω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κατ-**, **fasten** or **fix on, put upon**, καθῆψεν ὤμοις.. ἀμφίβληστρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1051; κ. τι ἀμφί τινι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1006; τι ἐπί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.9; τι εἴς τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.6.3; τι ἔκ τινος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.647e}}; ἄγκυραν καθάψας **having made** it **fast**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 213.10; τὰ ὀστέα καθάπτει τὰ νεῦρα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Spiritu” 483b31}}:—_middle_, κισσὸν ἐπὶ κρατὶ καθάπτεσθαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 3.4}}:— _passive_, βρόχῳ καθημμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1222, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Adon.” 11.}}
**2** **equip** by fastening or hanging on, in _middle_, σκευῇ σῶμ᾽ ἐμὸν καθάψομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 202, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 9.19 (1st c.BC: Archias):}} —_passive_, νεβρίνῃ καθημμένος δορᾷ **with** a fawn-skin **slung round him**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 219; καθημμένοι νεβρίδας 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.71.}}
**3** _intransitive_, **attach itself**, εἴς τι, πρός τι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 514b30, 515a3; later = 11.5}}, **fasten upon**, τῆς χειρός τινος {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Act.28.3, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.164.}}
**II** used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in}}_middle_, καθάπτεσθαί τινα ἐπέεσσι, in good or bad sense, as, σὺ τόν γ᾽ ἐπέεσσι καθάπτεσθαι μαλακοῖσι **do** thou **accost** him.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.582}}; μαλακοῖσι καθαπτόμενος ἐπέεσσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.70; μειλιχίοις ἐπέεσσι κ. 24.393}}; but also ἀντιβίοις ἐπέεσσι καθαπτόμενος **assailing.. **, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|18.415, 20.323; χαλεποῖσι κ. ἐπέεσσι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 332}}: without a qualifying _adjective_, **accost, assail**, ἐπέεσσι καθάπτετο θοῦρον Ἄρηα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.127, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.240; without ἐπέεσσι, γέροντα καθαπτόμενος προσέειπεν 2.39, compare 20.22, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.421.}}
**II.2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, with _genitive_, **upbraid**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.69, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 52a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.4: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.82.}}
**II.3** in military sense, **attack**, καθαψάμενοι τῆς οὐραγίας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.19.14.}}
**II.4** **appeal** to, θεῶν.. καταπτόμενος **appealing** to them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.68; Δημαρήτου καὶ ἄλλων μαρτύρων [prev. author] 8.65.}}
**II.5** **lay hold of**, τυραννίδος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 32.3; βρέφεος χείρεσσι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.65}}; τῆς θαλάσσης **take to** the sea, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.23}}: _active_, καθάπτων τοῦ τραχήλου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.20.10 (compare 1.3).}}
**II.6** **to be sensitive in respect of**, ψόφου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.16.}}

2511:
** κᾰθαρ-ίζω**,
_future_ **-ιῶ**{{NT|NT.Heb.9.14}}:—**cleanse**, θυσιαστήριον {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.29.36, cf. NT.Matt.23.25, NT.Act.10.15}}; **sift** grain, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PStrassb.” 2.11 (3rd c.AD)}}; **prune away**, περισσὰ βλαστήματα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.131r192 (1st c.AD)}}; **clear** ground of weeds, etc., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLips.” 111.12 (4th c.AD)}}; **keep** a precinct **clear**, ἀπό τινων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.37 (Andania, 1st c.BC):}} —in _middle_, _future_ -ιοῦμαι, of the menses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 43.}}
**II** of persons, **purify**, ἀπὸ ἁμαρτίας {{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.38.10; ἀπὸ παντὸς μολυσμοῦ NT.2Cor.7.1; τὴν συνείδησιν ἀπὸ νεκρῶν ἔργων NT.Heb.9.14}}; **cleanse** from leprosy, {{NT|NT.Matt.8.2 (and in _passive_, of the disease, [prev. work] 3):—}}_passive_, -ιζέστω ἀπὸ γυναικός κτλ. {{Refs|“IG” 22.1366.4, compare 1365.}}

2513:
** κᾰθᾰρ-ός**, ά, όν,
_Doric dialect_ **καθαρός** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.103, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 32c. 1}}, _Aeolic dialect_ **κόθ-** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 7.3}}; compare ἀνακαθαίρω, κάθαρσις:
**1** **physically clean, spotless** (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”), εἵματα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.61, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 12, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 35, 562, etc.}}; of persons, **cleanly**, κ. περὶ ἐσθῆτα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Virtutibus et Vitiis” 1250b28, compare “Rh.” 1416a23 (nisi to be read καθάριος).}}
**2** **clear of admixture, clear, pure**, especially of water, Βορυσθένης ῥέει καθαρὸς παρὰ θολεροῖσι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.53; κ. ὕδατα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 209 (_anapaest meter_); ὕδωρ κ. ζῶν LXX.Num.5.17; δρόσοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 96 (_anapaest meter_); κ. καὶ διαφανῆ ὑδάτια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229b; οὖρον 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.3; διαχώρημα [prev. author] “Coac.” 640}}; κ. φάος, φέγγος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.14, 9.90; πνεῦμα κ. οὐρανοῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 867; κ. ἄρτος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.40}}; of **white** bread, Wilcken {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 30i17 (3rd-2nd c.BC), LXX.Jdt.10.5, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.482, 19.137}}; ἄλευρον κ. {{Refs|Diocl.fragment 139}}; χρυσίον, ἀργύριον -ώτατον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.166, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.36, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.190, etc.; σῖτος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 18.8; σῖτος κ. ἀπὸ πάντων “PHib.” 1.84 (a). 6 (4th-3rd c.BC): frequently of grain}}**winnowed**, πυρὸς κ. ἄδολος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“POxy.” 1124.11 (1st c.AD),compare “PTeb.” 93.36 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; of metals, etc., σίδηρος {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 4481.13 (5th c.AD)}}, etc.; ἀρωμάτων, καθαρῶν, λαχάνων, uncertain meaning in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.429.6 (4th c.AD)}}; ἄκρατος καὶ κ. νοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.30; χρόαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Sensu” 440a5; φωναί [prev. author] “Aud.” 801b28}}; of feelings, **unmixed**, μῖσος τῆς ἀλλοτρίας φύσεως {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 245d, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 89}}; **serene**, φρήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1120 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** **clear of objects, free**, ἐν καθαρῷ (i.e. τόπῳ) in an **open** space, ἐν κ., ὅθι δὴ νεκύων διεφαίνετο χῶρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.491; ἐν κ., ὅθι κύματ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἠϊόνος κλύζεσκον 23.61, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.535 (_superlative_)}}; πάξαις Ἄλτιν ἐν κ. in **a clearing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10 (11). 45}}; ἐν κ. βῆναι to leave the way **clear**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1575 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐν τῷ κ. οἰκεῖν live in **the clear sunshine**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 520d}}; διὰ καθαροῦ ῥέειν, of a river whose course is **clear and open**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.202: with}} _substantive_, κελεύθῳ ἐν κ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.23}}; χῶρος κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.132; ἐν κ. λειμῶνι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 26.5}}; ἐν ἡλίῳ κ. in the **open** sun, opposed to σκιά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239c}}; ὥς σφι τὸ ἐμποδὼν ἐγεγόνεε κ. was **cleared away**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.183}}; κ. ποιεῖσθαι τὰς ἀρκυστασίας set up the nets **in open ground**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.6}}; frequently of land, **free** from weeds, etc., παραδώσω τὸν κλῆρον κ. ἀπὸ θρύου καλάμου ἀγρώστεως κτλ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 105.59 (2nd c.BC)}}; παραδώσω τὰς ἀρούρας κ. ὡς ἔλαβον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 1018.25 (3rd c.AD): with _genitive_, γλῶσσα καθαρὴ τῶν σημηΐων}} **clear** of the marks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38}}; καθαρὸν τῶν προβόλων, of a fort, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.21.7}}; of documents, **free from mistakes,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 1277.13 (3rd c.AD)}}; χειρόγραφον κ. ἀπὸ ἐπιγραφῆς καὶ ἀλείφαδος **free** from interlineation and erasure, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.178.13 (2nd c.AD).}}
**3.b** _metaphorically_, **free, clear** of debt, liability, etc., κ. ἀπὸ δημοσίων καὶ παντὸς εἴδους {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 197.14 (1st c.AD); κ. ἀπό τε ὀφειλῆς καὶ ὑποθήκης καὶ παντὸς διεγγυήματος [prev. work] 112.11 (1st c.AD)}}; γῆ κ. ἀπὸ γεωργίας βασιλικῆς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 633 (2nd c.AD)}}; καθαρὰ ποιῆσαι to give a **discharge,** {{Refs|PAvrom. 1 A 22}}; in moral sense, **free from pollution**, καθαρῷ θανάτῳ an **honourable** death, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.462; θάνατον οὐ κ., τὸν δι᾽ ἀγχόνης 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.491; ψυχαὶ ἀρηΐφατοι καθαρώτεραι ἢ ἐνὶ νούσοις 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 136}}; frequently **free from guilt** or **defilement, pure**, χεῖρες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 313 (_anapaest meter_); καθαρὸς χεῖρας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.35, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.95; κ. παρέχειν τινὰ κατὰ τὸ σῶμα καὶ κατὰ τὴν ψυχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 405b}}; ἔρχομαι ἐκ κοθαρῶν κοθαρά Orph{{Refs|“fragment” 32c.1, +others}}; of ceremonial **purity**, καθαρὰ καὶ ἁγνή εἰμι ἀπό τε τῶν ἄλλων τῶν οὐ καθαρευόντων καὶ ἀπ᾽ ἀνδρὸς συνουσίας Jusj. cited in {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.78, compare “UPZ” 78.28 (2nd c.BC), LXX.Num.8.7, +others; ἀπὸ τάφου καὶ ἐκφορᾶς καθαροί “SIG” 982.9 (2nd c.BC)}}; especially of persons **purified** after pollution, ἱκέτης προσῆλθες κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 474, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 548, etc.}}; also of things, βωμοί, θύματα, δόμος, μέλαθρα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 654 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1163, 1231 (troch.), 693}}: with _genitive_, **clear of** or **from.. **, κ. ἐγκλημάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.11}}; ἀδικίας, κακῶν, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 496d, “Cra.” 404a; ὁ τῶν κακῶν κ. τόπος [prev. author] “Tht.” 177a; κ. τὰς χεῖρας φόνου [prev. author] “Lg.” 864e; Κόρινθον.. ἀποδεῖξαι τῶν μιαιφόνων καθαράν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.6; κ. εἰμι ἀπὸ τοῦ αἵματος πάντων NT.Act.20.26, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.24; κ. ἀπὸ ὅρκου LXX.Gen.24.8}}; **ceremonially pure**, of food, ὄσπριον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37}}; of victims, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Gen.7.2, +others, “PGen.” 32.9 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; κ. ἡμέραι, opposed to ἀποφράδες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 800d.}}
**3.c** in _active_ sense, **purifying, cleansing**, λέβης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.26; θέειον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.96.}}
**4** of birth, **pure, genuine**, σπέρμα θεοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.15}}; πόλις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 673}}; τῶν Ἀθηναίων ὅπερ ἐστράτευε καθαρὸν ἐξῆλθε, i.e. were **citizens of pure blood**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.8}}; οἱ τῷ γένει μὴ κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 13.5}}; κ. ἀστοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 506}}; καθαρόν a **real, genuine** saying, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1015}}; κ. Τίμων **a** Timon **pure and simple**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Av.” 1549; κ. δοῦλος 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 9 (glossed by ἀπηκριβωμένος, “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 105)}}; ζημία κ., of a person, Alciphro 3.21.
**5** of language, **pure**, ὀνόματα, λέξις, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 1, 3; διάλεκτος [prev. author] “Dem.” 5}}; so of writers, [Λυσίας] κ. τὴν ἑρμηνείαν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lys.” 2; [Ξενοφῶν] κ. τοῖς ὀνόμασι [prev. author] “Pomp.” 4}}; also, **clear, simple**, σεμνὸς καὶ κ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.77a.}}
**5.b** Grammars, **preceded by a vowel, pure**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 635.10, 639.5, Hdn.Gr. 2.930, +others}}; **containing a 'pure' syllable**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 928.}}
**6** **without blemish, sound**, ὁ κ. στρατός, τὸ κ. τοῦ στρατοῦ, the **sound portion** of the army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.211, 4.135; see above 4.}}
**7** **clear, exact**, ἂν κ. ὦσιν αἱ ψῆφοι if the accounts are **exactly balanced**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.227 (sed compare καθαιρέω 11.5).}}
**II** _adverb_ **purely**, ἁγνῶς καὶ καθαρῶς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 121, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 337}}: _comparative_ **-ωτέρως**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.44.}}
**II.2** of birth, κ. γεγονέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147; αἱ κ. Ἑλληνίδες 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.112, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh. Pr.” 24.}}
**II.3** **with clean hands, honestly**, σὺν δίκῃ.. καὶ κ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 198}}; δικαίως καὶ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.62; κ. τε καὶ μετρίως τὸν βίον διεξελθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 108c.}}
**II.4** **clearly, plainly**, λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 631, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 35 (_anapaest meter_); λέξις κ. καὶ ἀκριβῶς ἔχουσα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.4; κ. γνῶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1045, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 66e; εἴσεσθαι [prev. passage]; καθαρώτατα ἀποδεῖξαι [prev. author] “Cra.” 426b.}}
**II.5** of language, **purely, correctly**, -ώτερον διαλέγεσθαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1116e, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Im.” 15.}}
**II.6** **entirely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 591; κ. τις ὢν ἀόργητος 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.71 W.; κ. ἐς ἐφήβους τελεῖν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 36.25, compare “Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.4.34.9: _superlative_ -ώτατα}} **in its purest form**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 66.}}

2514:
** κᾰθᾰρ-ότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**purity** of αἰθήρ as compared with ἀήρ, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 111b: _metaphorically_, [ἡ σοφία] χωρεῖ διὰ πάντων διὰ τὴν κ. LXX.Wis.7.24}}; ἡ τῶν εἰδῶν κ. {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 308; ἄμικτος καὶ ἀσύγχυτος κ. [prev. passage]}}
**2** **cleanliness**, of a town, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 778c.}}
**3** **clearness**, ὀφθαλμῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 213.}}
**4** moral **purity**, ψυχῆς {{Refs|Aristeas 234.}}
**5** **honesty**, ἡ περὶ τὰ χρήματα κ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.25.9}}; ἐπιείκεια καὶ κ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 67.6 (4th c.AD)}}; πίστις καὶ κ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Michel” 545.18 (Phrygia, 2nd c.BC).}}
**6** **purity, lucidity**, of literary style, {{Refs|Scholia Hermog.in Rhetores Graeci 7.81W.}}
**7** as a title, **Rectitude, Holiness,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|POxy. 2110.16 (4th c.AD).}}

2515:
** καθέδρ-α**, ἡ,
**seat**, κ. τοῦ λαγῶ a hare's **seat** or **form**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.4}}; **chair**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.25.1, “CPR” 22.8 (2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.3.7}}; opposed to κλίνη, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.714e}}; of rowers' **seats**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.21.2}}; κ. λοιμῶν, πρεσβυτέρων, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.1.1, 106(107).32.}}
**2** **sitting part, posteriors**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 47, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.184, “PRyl.” 63.10 (3rd c.AD).}}
**3** **base** of a column, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.46.}}
**II** **sitting posture**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 6b11, “PA” 689b21, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lassitudine” 5, 7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.45c, etc.}}
**II.2** **sitting idle, inaction**, ἐν τῇ καθέδρᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.18; κ. καὶ σχολή 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 28.}}
**II.3** **session**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 11.}}
**III** **chair** of a teacher, ἐπὶ τῆς Μωυσέως κ. ἐκάθισαν {{NT|NT.Matt.23.2}}; **professorial chair**, ἐπὶ τῆς κ. σοφιστής {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 845 (Eleusis, 3rd c.AD).}}
**IV** imperial **throne**, τὸν ἐπὶ τῇ κ. τοῦ Αὐτοκράτορος, the Emperor's representative, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BSA” 27.234 (Sparta, 2nd c.AD).}}

2516:
** καθέζομαι**
(see. below), _imperfect_ ἐκαθεζόμην in Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.9, “Cyr.” 5.3.25}} (but frequently as _aorist_ 2, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.44, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.110, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272e)}}; in Poets, καθεζόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.417, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 6, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1139}}: _future_ καθεδοῦμαι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ra.” 200, “Av.” 727 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.111, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 146a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.15; later καθεδήσομαι 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.72, καθεσθήσομαι LXX.Lev.12.5}}: _aorist_ καθεσθείς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.644.5 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.3.4, 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 3.2, but see 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 11 (καθίζομαι, _passive_ of καθίζω}}, which supplies the _transitive_ sense, is more common in _present_ and _imperfect_, but we have κατ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἕζεαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.378, καθεζόμεσθα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 33, καθέζονται 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.37, etc.)}}:—**sit down, take one's seat**, ἀγορήνδε καθεζώμεσθα κιόντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.372; εἰνὶ θύρῃσι καθέζετο 9.417, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.126, etc.}}; κατ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἕζευ ἐπὶ θρόνου{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 522; κατ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἕζετ᾽ ἐπὶ.. λίθοισιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.406}}; καθεζομένη πρόχνυ (see. πρόχνυ); so κ. ἐν.. εὐνατηρίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 918; ἐπὶ ζυγοῖς ἀρχῆς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 75; ἐς θρόνον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 231}}; **preside**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator [same place], 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.73; ἐνθαδί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 200}}; οὐ λαχόντες προεδρεύειν, ἀλλ᾽ ἐκ παρασκευῆς καθεζόμενοι **taking their seats**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.3: in Medicine texts, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.3.}}
**2** **sit down in, occupy**, a country, **encamp**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.18, 7.77}}; **settle**, εἰς χώραν {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“OGI” 201.13 (Nubia, 6th c.AD).}}
**II** **remain seated**, in various senses:
**II.1** **sit still**, with collateral notion of inaction, τίφθ᾽ οὕτως κατ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἕζεαι ἶσος ἀναύδ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.378, compare 6.295.}}
**II.2** **sit** as suppliants, ἱκέται καθεζόμεσθα βώμιοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heracl.” [same place]; πρὸς τὰ ἱερὰ ἱκετῶν καθεζομένων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1139, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.107.}}
**II.3** **sit** for one's portrait, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Plotini” 1.}}
**II.4** of a teacher, πρὸς ὑμᾶς ἐκαθεζόμην διδάσκων {{NT|NT.Matt.26.55.}}

2518:
** καθεύδω**,
so also in _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.95 codices:}}_imperfect_ καθεῦδον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(καθηῦδον) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.611, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 495, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217d, +others}}; ἐκάθευδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.13,23, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.11}}: _future_ καθευδήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 419, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.30, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἐκαθεύδησα (not in _Attic dialect_), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 6}}; _infinitive_ καθευδῆσαι {{Refs|Hp “Int.” 12}}:— **lie down to sleep, sleep**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.611, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.402, etc.}}; opposed to ἀγρυπνέω, ἐγρήγορα, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 471, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 71c, etc.; καλὸς νέκυς, οἷα καθεύδων 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.71; κ. μάτην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 881}}; νυκτὸς κ. **to sleep** by night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251e}}; κ. τὰς νύκτας **to sleep** all one's nights, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 4}}; μαλακῶς, σκληρῶς κ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 187.6, 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 16.2}}; of male and female, ἵνα τώ γε καθεύδετον ἐν φιλότητι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.313; κ. μετά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 219d}}: generally, **pass the night**, τὴν βουλὴν εἰς ἀκρόπολιν ἰέναι κἀκεῖ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.45}}; κ. ἐπὶ ξύλου **roost**, of a fowl, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1431}}; ἐκ τοῦ καθεύδοντος from **a sleeping state**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 72b.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **lie asleep, lie idle**, Χερί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1357, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.20, “An.” 1.3.11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.303}}; κ. τὸν βίον **to be asleep** all one's life, **sleep away** one's life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 404a}}; opposed to ἐνεργεῖν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1157b8}}; opposed to προσέχειν τοῖς πράγμασι, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 15.}}
**II.2** of things, **lie still, be at rest**, ἐλπίδες οὔπω κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 634; καθεύδειν ἐᾶν ἐν τῇ γῇ κατακείμενα τείχη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 778d: τοὺς νόμους ἐᾶν κ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 30.}}
**II.3** of the **sleep** of death, καθεύδοντες ἐν τάφῳ {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.87(88).6, cf. LXX.Dan.12.2, NT.1Thes.5.10.}}

2519:
** καθηγ-ητής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**guide**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Numenius Apamensis Platonicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.313d.}}
**2** **teacher, professor**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.43 W., +others, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 3, NT.Matt.23.10, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.70e, 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 5.6, “OGI” 408 (Theb. Aegypt., 2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 930.6 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}, etc.:— also **καθηγ-ητήρ**, ῆρος, ὁ, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.300}}, _Doric dialect_ **καθᾱγ-**, κελεύθου {{Refs|“IG” 12(1).44 (from Rhodes)}}:—_feminine_ **καθηγ-ήτειρα** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 33P., 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 76.6.}}

2520:
** καθήκω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κατήκω**, (see. ἥκω) **come** or **go down**, especially to fight, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 455 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **come down to, reach to**, ἐς θάλασσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.22, 130}}; ἐπὶ θάλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.32, 5.49, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.27; πρὸς τὸν Μηλιακὸν κόλπον [prev. author] 3.96}}; κέρκος.. εἰς λεπτὸν καθήκουσα **tapering** away, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 503a20: _metaphorically_, of descent, ὃ [γένος] εἰς αὐτὸν κ. 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.11.8.}}
**3** **come** **in due course** to any one, καθῆκεν ἐς ἡμᾶς ὁ λόγος the turn of speaking **came** to us, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.25; παρὰ τετάρτην ἡμέραν ἑκάστῃ σημαίᾳ καθήκειν τὴν λειτουργίαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.33.9, compare “PCair.Zen.” 218.24 (3rd c.BC)}}; τῆς βολῆς καθηκούσης ἐς αὐτόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 2; ἐν τῷ ξυμποσίῳ.. ἐπὶ τὸν Θεσμόπολιν καθῆκε τὸ σκῶμμα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 34.}}
**4** of Time, ὁπότε καθήκοι ὁ Χρόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.7.2; ὅταν οἱ Χρόνοι καθήκωσι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 591a8; πρότερον ἢ τὴν ὥραν καθήκειν “PRev.Laws” 41.14 (3rd c.BC)}}; in _participle_, τοῦ καθήκοντος Χρόνου the **normal** time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 75, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.35, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.126}}; αἱ κ. ἡμέραι the **regular, proper** days, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.80; ἐν τῇ κ. ὥρᾳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 568a17; ἐν τοῖς κ. καιροῖς [prev. work] 573a30}}; of events, ἑορτῆς εἰς τὰς ἡμέρας ἐκείνας καθηκούσης as the festival **fell** on those days, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 18, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.7.1}}; ἐκκλησίαν ποιῆσαι, ὅταν ἐκ τῶν νόμων καθήκῃ when it **is** legally **due**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.185}}; ἡ κ. σύνοδος or ἐκκλησία, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.14.1, 1.15.8, etc.}}
**II** **to be meet, fit, proper**, τοῖς κ. [νομίμοις] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1325a13}}; τὰς ἐσθῆτας τὰς κ. ἀεὶ ταῖς περιθεταῖς **suiting** them, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.78.3}}; ὁ καθήκων ἐκ τῶν νομίμων ἀριθμός **a quorum**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.30; also καθῆκόν ἐστιν αὐτὸν ἐπαινεῖσθαι “Inscription Prien.” 114.32 (1st c.BC).}}
**II.2** _impersonal_, καθήκει μοι **it belongs** to me, **is** my **duty**, with _infinitive_, οἷς καθήκει ἁθροίζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.7, compare “Cyr.” 8.1.4, etc.: in later writers}}_imperfect_ καθῆκε in _present_ sense, **it is meet, proper**, οὐδ᾽ ἅψασθαι καθῆκέ τινων{{NT|Aristeas 149; οὐ κ. αὐτὸν ζῆν NT.Act.22.22: frequently in}}_participle_, τὰ καθήκοντα **one's due** or **duty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.5; τὰ κατήκοντα Σπαρτιήτῃσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.104}}; ποιεῖν τὸ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 575: especially in Stoic philosophical (from signification 1.3 _accusative_ to 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.108, compare κατά 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.3), περὶ τοῦ κ.}}, title of work by Zeno,{{Refs|compare “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 1.55, etc.: frequently in _plural_, [prev. work] 3.30}}; μὴ κ. un**becoming,** {{NT|NT.Rom.1.28.}}
**II.3** τὰ κατήκοντα the **present crisis**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97, 5.49; τὰ κ. πρήγματα [prev. author] 8.19, 40, 102.}}
**II.3.b** τὰ καθήκοντα the **payments due**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 42.15 (2nd c.BC); τὴν -ουσαν ἡμῖν δίδοσθαι σύνταξιν τῶν δεόντων [prev. work] 6.}}
**II.4** _adverb_ _present_ _participle_ καθηκόντως **fittingly, properly**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.53U., “OGI” 90.28(Rosetta, 2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.9.6, variant in 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.93; πρός τι 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.43}}; **consistently with duty,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Stoic. 3.188, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.448e}}; **appropriately**, with _dative_, τῷ τόπῳ Aristeas 81; κ. ἔχειν πρός τι {{Refs|[prev. author] 87.}}
**III** τὸ καθῆκον the **precise proportion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 46.}}

2521:
** κάθημαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κάτ-**, _2nd pers._ _singular_ κάθησαι (_Ionic dialect_ κάτ- {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.6, probably in 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Sosibii Victoria” vi4, κάθῃ 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 115, NT.Act.23.3, uncertain reading in “Comica Adespota” 1203, (προ-) 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 13.171a}} codices; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ κάθηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 597, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.70, “SIG” 987.26 (Chios, 4th c.BC)}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ κατέαται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.86; _imperative_ κάθησο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.191, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 627; κάθου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 620, 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 13, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 1017, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 224}}; κάθουσο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Scholia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.42}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ καθήσθω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 916}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ καθήσθωσαν {{Refs|“IG” 9(2).1109.38 (Thess.)}}; _subjunctive_ καθῶμαι, κάθῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 277, καθῆται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 754; _optative_ καθοίμην [prev. author] “Ra.” 919, probably in [prev. author] “Lys.” 149}}; _infinitive_ καθῆσθα; _participle_ καθήμενος: _imperfect_ ἐκαθήμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 152, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.31, etc., ἐκάθητο “hymnus ad Bacchum” 14, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 510, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.6, ἐκάθησθε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 638, ἐκάθηντο, _Ionic dialect_ ἐκατέατο variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.144, 8.73}}; also without syllable augment καθῆστο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.569, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1102, “Ph.” 1467, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328c, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.19, καθῆτο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.169,217}}; _Ionic dialect_ κατῆστο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.46, καθῆσθε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.21 (with variants), καθῆντο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 302, variant in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.58}}; _Epic dialect_ καθήατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.76}}; _Ionic dialect_ κατέατο [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.144, 8.73, 9.90 (variant{καθ-})}}]: the later _future_ καθήσομαι {{LXX+NT+5th c.BC+|LXX.Lev.8.35, NT.Luke.22.30 is corrupt in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 960}}:—**to be seated, sit**, αὐτός τε κάθησο καὶ ἄλλους ἵδρυε λαούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.191; κάθησ᾽ ἑδραία 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 266}}: frequently in _participle_, πέτρῃ ἔπι προβλῆτι καθήμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.407}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀκτῆς κλαῖε κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.82; κ. οἶος ἐν Ἴδῃ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.207; ἐν ἀγῶνι κ. 23.448}}; κλαῖον δ᾽ ἐν λεχέεσσι κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.497}}; θύρῃσι κ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. 17.530; ἐπὶ ταῖσι θύραις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 466}}; αὐτόθεν ἐκ δίφροιο κ. even from his seat **as he sat there**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.420; καθήμεθ᾽ ἄκρων ἐκ πάγων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 411}}; ἐκ μέσου κατῆστο **sate** aloof, remained neutral, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.83, compare 4.118, 8.73}}; ἐν θρόνῳ κ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.149}}; θρόνῳ κ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 315; κ. πρὸς τάφῳ [prev. author] “Hel.” 1084; πρὸς τὸ πῦρ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 773; ἐπὶ δίφρου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328c; ἐπὶ τῶν ἵππων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.54; ἐπὶ τοῦ ἅρματος NT.Act.8.28; ἐς τοὐργαστήριον 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.27: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἕδραν κ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 55}}: with _accusative_ loci, **sit on**, ὀφρύην {{Refs|[prev. work] 394.}}
**2** especially of courts, councils, assemblies, etc., **sit**: οἱ καθήμενοι **the judges, the court**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.139, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.3, etc.; δικαστὰς οὐχ ὁρῶ καθημένους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 208}}; ὑμεῖς οἱ καθήμενοι you **who sit as judges**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.85; οὐκ ἐπὶ τούτῳ κ. ὁ δικαστής 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35c; κ. ὑπὲρ τῶν νόμων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.25}}; of the βουλή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.43; βουλῆς περὶ τούτων καθημένης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.116}}; of an assembly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.10.5}}; οἱ κ. **the spectators in a theatre**, {{Refs|Hegesippus Epigrammaticus 1.29.}}
**3** **sit still, sit quiet**, ὕψι περ ἐν νεφέεσσι καθημένω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.264}}; σφοῖσιν ἐνὶ μεγάροισι καθήατο (for ἐκάθηντο) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.76; ἐν πένθεϊ μεγάλῳ κατῆστο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.46}}; μετὰ κόπον κ. **rest** after labour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 479.3}}: and, in bad sense, **sit doing nothing, lie idle**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.403, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134}}; of an army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.124}}; of a boat's crew, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 107.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; οὐδὲν ποιοῦντες ἐνθάδε καθήμεθα, μέλλοντες ἀεί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.17, compare 2.23, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 142.20, etc.}}; also, of an army, **to have its quarters, be encamped**, περὶ τὰς Ἀχαρνάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.20, compare 101; ἐχθρῶν ὑπ᾽ αὐτοῖς τείχεσιν καθημένων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 752.}}
**4** **reside** in a place, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Neh.11.6; λαὸς καθήμενος ἐν σκοτίᾳ NT.Matt.4.16}}; **settle**, εἰς Σινώπην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 9p.43H.}}
**5** **lead a sedentary, obscure life**, ἐν σκότῳ καθήμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.83; ἔσω καθημένη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 919}}; αἱ βαναυσικαὶ [τέχναι] ἀναγκάζουσι καθῆσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 4.2}}; **to be engaged** or **employed**, especially in a sedentary business, ἐπ᾽ αὐτῷ τούτῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.86}}; κ. ἐπὶ τῇ τραπέζῃ, of bankers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.42, compare 45.33; ἐπ᾽ ἐργαστηρίου [prev. author] 59.67; ἐπὶ τοῦ.. ἰατρείου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.40}}; καθῆσθαι ἐν πόλει, opposed to ζῆν ἐν Χωρίῳ, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 11p.59H.}}
**6** **sit** as a suppliant, ἐν Δελφοῖσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.63, cf. Oracle texts [prev. work] 7.140.}}
**7** of districts and countries, **lie**, Χωρία ὁμοίως καθήμενα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.8.7.}}
**7.b** **to be low-lying**, τὰ λεῖα καὶ καθήμενα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.1, compare “NA” 16.12}}; πεδίον κ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 14.17}}; πόπανον.. κ. δωδεκόμφαλον probably **flat** in the middle, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1367.}}
**8** of a statue, **to be placed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1315b21.}}
**9** of things, **to be set** or **placed**, λαγῴοις ἐπ᾽ ἀμύλῳ καθημένοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 32, compare 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 108.17; τὸ πηδάλιον κ. πλάγιον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 851a4, compare [prev. work] 13.}}

2523:
** καθίζω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κατ-**, _imperfect_ κάθιζον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.426, +others; in Prose ἐκάθιζον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.6, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.13}}: _future_ καθέσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 12.11 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; καθίσω (_intransitive_) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus Comedy texts 5}}; _Ionic dialect_ κατίσω (_transitive_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.190}}; _Attic dialect_ also καθιῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2. 1.4, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.25, 39.11, “IG” 22.778.13 (3rd c.BC)}}; _Doric dialect_ καθιξῶ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 10.16}}: _aorist 1_ καθεῖσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.389, +others}}_subjunctive_ καθέσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.104}}; _infinitive_ καθέσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 22.46 aB*21, 25 (5th-4th c.BC)}}; _poetry_ κάθεσσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.42}} codices; this _aorist_ καθεῖσα has Ms. authority in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 31 (ἐγκαθ-, _middle_), “Ph.” 1188, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.88, 4.79, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.82, but we also find _Epic dialect_ κάθῐσα, _Ionic dialect_ κάτ- (for which κάθεσα, κάτεσον, etc., should perhaps be restored), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.280 (variant{κάθεσαν}), +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.89, 2.126, καθῖσα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 911, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.66 (to be read καθεῖσα), later ἐκάθῐσα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.23, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 544, etc., compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.89}}]; also _Epic dialect_ _participle_ καθίσσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.488}}; _Doric dialect_ καθίξας {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.12}}_subjunctive_ καθίξῃ{{Refs|[prev. work] 51}}; late _participle_ καθιζήσας, _subjunctive_ -ζήσῃ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 54.30, 37.27}}: _perfect_ κεκάθῐκα {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.115, NT.Heb.12.2, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 323.23}}:—_middle_, _imperfect_ ἐκαθιζόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 824, κὰδ.. ἵζ- 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.50}}: _future_ καθιζήσομαι {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229a, “Euthd.” 278b, (προσ-) 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.167, later καθίσομαι NT.Matt.19.28, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.583f}} **-ιοῦμαι** {{LXX|LXX.Mal.3.3, +others}}: _aorist 1_ καθεσσάμην {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 111; also ἐκαθισάμην “SIG” 975.6 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, (ἐπ-, παρ-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.130 codices, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.14}}; _Epic dialect_ ἐκαθισσάμην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 233, καθισσάμην 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.278, 1219}}:—_passive_, _aorist 1_ _participle_ καθιζηθείς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 63.5:}}
**I** causal, **make to sit down, seat**, ἄλλους μὲν κάθισον Τρῶας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.68; μή με κάθιζ᾽ 6.360; σ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἐμοῖσιν ἐγὼ γούνεσσι καθίσσας 9.488; κὰδ δ᾽ εἷσ᾽ ἐν θαλάμῳ 3.382; τὴν μὲν.. καθεῖσεν ἐπὶ θρόνου 18.389; κατίσαι τινὰ ἐπ᾽ οἰκήματος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121}}.; καθιεῖν τινα εἰς τὸν θρόνον, i.e. to make him king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.4; ἐπὶ θρόνον 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.22 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**I.2** **set, place**, τὸν μὲν.. καθεῖσεν ἐπ᾽ ἠϊόεντι Σκαμάνδρῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.36; κὰδ δ᾽ ἐν Ἀθήνῃς εἷσεν 2.549; Κρόνον.. Ζεὺς γαίης νέρθε καθεῖσε 14.204; καθίζειν τινὰ εἰς δόμον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1541}}; κ. στρατόν **encamp** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Heracl.” 664, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.90; κ. τὸ στράτευμα ἐς Χωρίον ἐπιτήδειον [prev. author] 6.66; σύλλογον εἰς Χωρίον κ., Χωρὶς μὲν τοὺς ὁπλίτας, Χωρὶς δὲ τοὺς ἱππέας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 755e.}}
**I.2.b** **post** watchers, guards, etc., σκοπὸς ὅν ῥα καθεῖσεν Αἴγισθος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.524}}; κατίσαι φυλάκους **set** guards, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.89, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.14; ἄλλους κάτισον ἀγαγὼν κατὰ τὰς.. πύλας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.155; κ. ἐνέδραν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 19}}: rarely of things, τι ἐπὶ τηγάνοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 127.}}
**I.3** **set up**, ἀνδριάντα κάθεσσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.42}} codices:—_middle_, καθέσσασθαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 111, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1219.}}
**I.4** **cause** an assembly, court, etc., **to take their seats, convene**, ἀγορὰς ἠμὲν λύει ἠδὲ καθίζει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.69}}; ὅταν καθέσωσιν ἀγῶνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.104; κ. τὸ δικαστήριον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 305, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.11, “IG” 22.778.13; νομοθέτας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.25, probably in [prev. author] 3.10}}; but κ. τινὶ δικαστήν **appoint** a judge to try a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 874a; ἐάν τε Χιλίους ἐάν θ᾽ ὁποσουσοῦν ἡ πόλις καθίσῃ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.223}}; **constitute, establish**, δικαστήρια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 298e; βουλὴν ἐπίσκοπον πάντων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 19.}}
**I.5** **put into a certain condition**, especially in the phrase κλαίοντά τινα κ **set** him aweeping, κλάοντα καθέσω ς᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus [same place], compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 535e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.15; but [prev. work] 14 κλαίειν τινὰ κ}}. **to make** him weep: for [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.51, see at {ἀκράτιστος}.}}]
**I.6** **marry**, γυναῖκας ἀλλοτρίας {{LXX|LXX.Neh.13.27, compare 23.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **take one's seat, sit**, _absolutely_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.394, etc.}}; μετ᾽ ἀθανάτοισι, ἐν θρόνοισι καθίζειν,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.50, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.422}}; ἐν [θώκοισι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.181}}; ἐπὶ τοῖς ἐργαστηρίοις or τῶν -ίων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.9, 7.15; ἐπὶ σκίμποδα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 254; ἐπὶ δένδρου 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 614a34 (but κ. ἐπὶ κώπην}}, of rowers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 197)}}; of suppliants, κ. ἐπὶ τὸν βωμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.24; εἰς γόνυ 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.115: in Poets also with _accusative_, κ. τρίποδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 366, “El.” 980}}; βωμόν, ὀμφαλόν, ἱερά, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 48, “Ion” 6, 1317.}}
**II.2** **sit, recline at meals**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.2.}}
**II.3** **sit as judge**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97, 5.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 659b, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.382}}; **hold a session**, of the πρόεδροι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.89, compare “Hermes” 17.5 (from Delos).}}
**II.4** **reside**, μετά τινος {{LXX|LXX.Ruth.2.23 (3.1)}}; ἐν πόλει{{LXX|LXX.Neh.11.1.}}
**II.5** **settle, sink down**, ἐπὶ τὰ ἰσχία καθίσαι τὼ ἵππω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254c; καθίσας ὁ φελλὸς ἀνοίξει τὸν κρουνόν 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “(Spiritalia) Pneumatica” 1.20.}}
**II.6** of ships, **run aground, be stranded**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.39.3, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.3.4.}}
**III** _middle_ in _intransitive_ sense, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.50 (in tmesi), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.3, etc.; εἰς τὸν αὐτὸν θᾶκον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 516e}}; ἐὰν δὲ καθίζεσθαι κελεύσῃ if he order them **to take their seats** (among the spectators in the theatre), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.56 (nisi to be read καθέζεσθαι, as also [prev. work]162, both readings are found [prev. work]119)}}; καθίζεσθαι ἢ κατακλινῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228e.}}
**III.2** of birds, **settle, alight**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 614b23.}}
**III.3** **leave goods purchased** in a market, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 975.6 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}.--_Attic dialect_ in this _signification_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

2524:
** καθίημι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κατ-**, _future_ καθήσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 555 (Lyric poetry):}}_aorist 1_ καθῆκα, _Epic dialect_ καθέηκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.642}}: _2nd pers._ _dual_ _aorist 2_ κάθετον {{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 487}}: _perfect_ καθεῖκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysippus Comicus 1, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.46: (see. ἵημι)}}:—**let fall, drop, send down**, κὰδ δὲ [κεραυνὸν].. ἧκε Χαμᾶζε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.134; κατὰ δ᾽ ὑψόθεν ἧκεν ἐέρσας 11.53}}; οἶνον λαυκανίης καθέηκα I **have sent** the wine **down** my throat, {{Refs|24.642}}; καθίετε ἵππους ἐν δίνῃσι **sink** them in the stream, as an offering to the river-god, {{Refs|21.132}}; [ἱστία] ἐς νῆας κάθεμεν we **let** them **down, lowered** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.72; λαῖφος καθήσειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eu.”}} [same place]; σχοίνῳ σπυρίδα κ. **let** it **down** by a cord, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.16}}; σῶμα πύργων κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1011}}; κοντὸν ἐς [τὴν λίμνην] κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.195; ἐμαυτὸν εἰς ἅλα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1614}}; ὅπλα εἰς ἅλἀ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1375; καθεῖσαν δέλεάρ μοι φρενῶν [prev. author] “IT” 1181 (so _metaphorically_ τοῦτον τὸν λόγον καθεῖκε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.46); κ. τι ἐς πῶμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1034; νάρθηκ᾽ ἐς πέδον [prev. author] “Ba.” 706}}; κ. σπονδάς **pour** them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “IA” 60}}; τὸν κλῆρον ἐς μέσον καθείς, of putting lots into a helmet or urn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1285; ἄγκυραν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.36; τὰ δίκτυα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 533b18}}; κατιεμένην καταπειρητηρίην, of a sounding-line, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.28: _absolutely_, καθιέναι}} **reach by sounding, sound**, οὐδεὶς καθεὶς ἐδυνήθη πέρας εὑρεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 351a13: in Medicine texts, [αὐλίσκον]}} **pass** a catheter, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “de renum et vesicae affectionibus” 7.11}}; οἵαν πρόφασιν καθῆκε (παρὰ προσδοκίαν for οἷον ἄγκιστρον) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 174}}; λόγους συμβατηρίους κ. **make** offers of peace, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 41.47}}; κ. πεῖραν **make** an attempt, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 2.13, “NA” 1.57}}; εἰς ὤμους κ. κόμας **let** one's hair **flow loose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 695, compare “IT” 52}}; κ. πώγωνα **let** one's beard **grow long**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 100, compare “Th.” 841, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.23.21}} (_passive_, τὰς τρίχας καθειμέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 27; πώγωνα καθειμένος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 10; τὸ γένειον αὐτῷ καθεῖτο 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 11.10)}}; [αἱ ὄϊες] μείζω τὰ οὔθατα καθιᾶσιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 596a24}} (_passive_, of a mare's udder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.2)}}; also τείχη καθεῖναι ἐς θάλασσαν **carry** them **down** to the sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.52}} (_passive_, καθεῖτο τείχη {{Refs|4.103)}}; καθῆκε τὰ σκέλη **let down** his legs, of one who had been lying, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c}}; κατ᾽ ἀμφοῖν ἄμφω (i.e. τὰ σκέλη) καθέντος, of a wrestler, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.143}}; κ. δόρατα **let down** one's pike, **bring** it **to the rest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.25}}; κ. τὰς κώπας **let down** the oars, so as to stop the ship's way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.91}}; rarely of striking, δι᾽ ὀμφαλοῦ καθῆκεν ἔγχος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1413; καθῆκε ξύλον παιδὸς ἐς κάρα [prev. author] “HF” 993}}; κ. πρὸς γαῖαν γόνυ to kneel down, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 561; ἐς δὲ γῆν γόνυ καμάτῳ καθεῖσαν [prev. author] “IT” 333}}; κ. τινὰ ἐς ὕπνον **let** him **fall** asleep, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “HF” 1006; εἰς κίνδυνον ἐμαυτόν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.27}}; [πώλους] ἐς λειμώνων Χλόην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 423}}; of a general, κ. στρατόπεδα εἰς.. **let** them **march** into.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.70.11; εἰς τὸ πεδίον τὴν δύναμιν [prev. author] 3.92.7}}; κ. ἐπί τινας τόπους ἐνέδρας **lay** an ambush, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.63.9}}:—_passive_, **stretch down seawards**, ὄρεα μέχρι πρὸς τὴν θάλατταν καθειμένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 118a; ἕως γῆς τοῦ πρηστῆρος καθιεμένου 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.47U., compare p.51}} U.; τὸ καθειμένον τῆς φωνῆς **low tone** of voice, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 5.2.3.}}
**2** **send down into the arena, enter for racing**, ἅρματα, ζεύγη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.34}}; of plays, **produce**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus cited in Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 552 (_passive_)}}; διδασκαλίαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cimon” 8}}; so ἔδοξε τοῖς πρυτάνεσι.. γνώμας καθεῖναι (Comedy texts for προθεῖναι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 397}}; κατὰ τὴν ἀγορὰν λογοποιοὺς κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.15}}: frequently in later Greek in a general sense, **set in motion, employ**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 7.4; κ. ἔς τινας ὑποψίας 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.38}}; φίλους καὶ ῥήτορας κ. **employ** them, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 7, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.42}}:—_passive_, **to be put in motion**, ἡ στρατηλασίη κατίετο ἐς πᾶσαν τὴν Ἑλλάδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.138.}}
**3** **allow to return** from exile, φυγάδας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.20.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **swoop down** like a wind, λαμπρὸς καὶ μέγας καθιείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 430}}; of rivers, **run down**, ἑκατέρωσε μέχρι τοῦ μέσου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 112e}}; κ. εἰς γόνυ **sink** on the knee, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 45}}; κ. εἰς ἀγῶνα, Latin _descendere in arenam_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.616d, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 6}}; κ. ἐς Ῥόδον **arrive** there, variant for{κατῆγεν}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 5.17.2.}}

2525:
** καθίστημι**,
**A** in causal sense:—_active_, in _present_, _imperfect_, _future_, and _perfect_ καθέστᾰκα {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 28, LXX.Jer.1.10, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 54, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 32.11, etc.; once καθέστηκα “PHib.” 1.82i14 (3rd c.BC)}}: _pluperfect_ **-εστάκει**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Demetrius Astrologus Sceps. cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.697d}}:—also in _middle_, _future_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.5.1)}}, _aorist_ 1, more rarely _present_ (below {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.2)}}:—**set down**, κρητῆρα καθίστα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.202}}; νῆα κατάστησον **bring** it **to land**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.185}}; κ. δίφρους **place, station** them, before starting for the race, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 710}}; ποῖ [δεῖ] καθιστάναι πόδ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 184; κ. τινὰ εἰς τὸ φανερόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.22}}; **set up, erect**, of stones, {{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 94, 95 H.:}} —_middle_, [λαῖφος] κατεστήσαντο βοεῦσι **steadied** it, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 407.}}
**A.2** **bring down** to a place, τούς μ᾽ ἐκέλευσα Πύλονδε καταστῆσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.274}}: generally, **bring**, κ. τινὰ ἐς Νάξον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.64, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.78}}; especially **bring back**, πάλιν αὐτὸν κ. ἐς τὸ τεῖχος σῶν καὶ ὑγιᾶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.34; κ. τοὺς Ἕλληνας εἰς Ἰωνίαν πάλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.13}}; without πάλιν, **replace, restore**, ἐς φῶς σὸν κ. βίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 362}}; ἃς (i.e. τὰς κόρας) οὐδ᾽ ὁ Μελάμπους.. καταστήσειεν ἄν **cure** their squint, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 112.5}}; ἰκτεριῶντας κ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.1}}; τὸ σῶμα **restore** the general health, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.133}}:—_middle_, κατεστήσαντο (variant for{κατεκτήσαντο}) εὐδαιμονίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.62}}:—_passive_, οὐκ ἂν ἀντὶ πόνων Χάρις καθίσταιτο **would be returned**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86.}}
**A.3** **bring before** a ruler or magistrate, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.209, “PRyl.” 65.10 (1st c.BC)}}, etc.; τινὰ ἐπί τινα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 202.6 (3rd c.BC), “POxy.” 281.24 (1st c.AD).}}
**A.II** **set in order, array**, of soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.10}}; **set** as guards, προφυλακάς {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.2.1, etc.}}
**A.II.2** **ordain, appoint**, κατέστησε τύραννον εἶναι παῖδα τὸν ἑωυτοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.94, compare 25}}: usually without the _infinitive_, κ. τινὰ ὕπαρχον {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.105}}; ἄλλον [ἄρχοντα] ἀντὶ αὐτοῦ {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.12, etc.; βασιλέα ἐπί τινας LXX.1Ki.8.5, +others; τινὰ ἐς μοναρχίαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 352; ἐπὶ τὰς ἀρχάς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.132; τινὰ τύραννον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1672; κ. ἐγγυητάς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1064}}; δικαστάς, ἐπιμελητάς, νομοθέτας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 917, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.9, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.10 (sed to be read καθίσατε,compare καθίζω 1.4)}}; of games, etc., γυμνικοὺς ἀγῶνας κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.1}}: rarely with _infinitive_, οἱ καθιστάντες μουσικῇ.. παιδεύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 410b}}:—so in _passive_, κυβερνᾶν κατασταθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.7.3}}: _aorist_ _middle_, **appoint for oneself**, τύραννον καταστησάμενοι παρὰ. σφίσι αὐτοῖσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.á; ἄρχοντας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.39, etc.}}
**A.II.2.b** especially of laws, constitutions, ceremonies, etc., **establish**, νόμους, τελετάς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 892, “Ba.” 21, etc.}}; πολιτείαν, δημοκρατίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 7.1, Decrees [prev. work] 29.3; ὀλιγαρχίαν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.42}}; also, **set in order, arrange**, πολιτείαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 590e}}:—also in _middle_, τοῦτο βουλευτήριον φρούρημα γῆς καθίσταμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 706; τὴν Ἱππίου καθίσταμαι τυραννίδα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 502; καθίστατο τὰ περὶ τὴν Μυτιλήνην ᾗ αὐτῷ ἐδόκει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.35; πόλεις ἐπὶ τὸ ὠφέλιμον [prev. author] 1.76; [Εὔβοιαν] ὁμολογίᾳ [prev. work] 114}}; πρὸς ἐμὲ τὸ πρᾶγμα καταστήσασθαι **settle** it with me, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.90.}}
**A.II.3** **bring into a certain state**, τινὰ ἐς ἀπόνοιαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.82; ἐς ἀπορίαν [prev. author] 7.75; εἰς ἀνάγκην 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.3; εἰς αἰσχύνην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 230d; εἰς ἐρημίαν φίλων [prev. author] “Phdr.” 232d; εἰς ἀγῶνα [prev. author] “Ap.” 24c; τινὰ εἰς ἀσφάλειαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.123}}; τίνας εἰς ἀγῶνα καθέστακ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 28, compare 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 2; κ. τινὰ ἐν ἀγῶνι καὶ κινδύνῳ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.61; τὴν πόλιν ἐν πολέμῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 242a; τοὺς φίλους ἐν ἀκινδύνῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.28}}; κ. ἑαυτὸν ἐς κρίσιν **present** himself for trial, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.131, compare 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 6}}; κ. τινὰ εἰς τοὺς ἀρχικούς **reckon** him as one of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.9.}}
**A.II.4** with _double_ _accusative_, **make, render so and so**, ψευδῆ γ᾽ ἐμαυτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 657; ἡ ἐπιθυμία κ. τινὰ ἀμνήμονα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.7}}; τὸ πιστὸν ὑμᾶς ἀπιστοτέρους κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.68}}; κ. τι φανερόν, σαφές, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.42, 1.32}}; τινὸς ἐπίπονον τὸν βίον κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.17: with}}_participle_, κλαίοντα καθιστάναι τινά **bring** one to tears, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 635: rarely with}}_infinitive_, κ. τινὰ φεύγειν **make** him fly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 283, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Charid.” 8}}:—_passive_, ἀνάγκη τὴν ναυμαχίαν πεζομαχίαν καθίστασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89.}}
**A.II.5** _middle_, **get for oneself**, τὴν ζόην καταστήσασθαι ἀπ᾽ ἔργων ἀνοσιωτάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.105.}}
**A.II.6** **make**, in periphrases, πάννυχοι.. διάπλοον καθίστασαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 382:}} —_middle_, κρυφαῖον ἔκπλουν οὐδαμῇ καθίστατο{{Refs|[prev. work] 385.}}
**B** _intransitive_ in _aorist_ 2, _perfect_ καθέστηκα, and _pluperfect_ of _active_ (also _future_ καθεστήξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.37, 102), and all tenses of}}_middle_ (except _aorist_ 1) and _passive_: _perfect_ καθέσταμαι{{LXX+2nd c.BC+|in later Greek, “IG” 22.1006.24 (2nd c.BC), LXX.Num.3.32, etc.}}:—**to be set, set oneself down, settle**, ἐς [Αἴγιναν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.131, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.75}}; [ὀδύναι] καθίσταντο ἐς ὑπογάστριον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.97}}; of joints, ἐξίσταται ἀνωδύνως καὶ κ. goes out of joint and **in again**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Art.” 8}}; κ. ἐς Ῥήγιον **to make** R. **a base** of operations, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.86}}; simply, **to be come to** a place, ὅποι καθέσταμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 23.}}
**B.b** **come before** another, **stand in** his **presence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.135; λέξον καταστάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 295}} (unless it be taken in _signification_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4), compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.152; κ. ἐς ὄψιν τινός [prev. author] 7.29; καταστάντες ἐπὶ τοὺς ἄρχοντας ἔλεγον [prev. author] 3.46, compare 156; καταστὰς ἐπὶ τὸ πλῆθος ἔλεγε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.84.}}
**B.2** **to be set** as guard, ὑπό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.59, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 356, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.19, etc.}}; **to be appointed**, δεσπότης.. καθέστηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 142; στρατηλάτης νέος καταστάς [prev. author] “Supp.” 1216}}; κ. Χορηγὸς εἰς Θαργήλια, στρατηγός, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.35, etc.; οἱ πρόβουλοι καθεστᾶσιν ἐπὶ τοῖς βουλευταῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1299b37}}; δικτάτωρ.. καθε[στάμενος τὸ τέταρτον], = Latin _dictator designatus quartum_, of Caesar, {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).35b7 (Mytil.).}}
**B.3** **deposit a sediment**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.2, 7.}}
**B.4** also, **stand** or **become quiet** or **calm**, of water, ὅταν ἡ λίμνη καταστῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 865, compare “PHolm.” 16.3}}; θάλασσα γαληνὴ καὶ κ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.31.10}}; πνεῦμα λεῖον καὶ καθεστηκός calm and **settled**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1003}}; ὁ θόρυβος κατέστη **subsided**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80}}; of laughter, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.4}}; of a swelling, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 7; ἕως τὰ πράγματα κατασταίη 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.25}}; also of persons, καταστάς **composedly**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 295 (but see above 1b)}}; [ἡ ψυχὴ] καθίσταται καὶ ἠρεμίζεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 248a2}}; ὁρῶμεν [τοὺς ἐνθουσιαστικοὺς].. καθισταμένους {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1342a10; καθεστηκυίας τῆς διανοίας 1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 4.13}}; καθεστῶτι προσώπῳ with **composed, calm** countenance, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 17; μαίνεσθαι καὶ ἔξω τοῦ καθεστηκότος εἶναι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 5}}; τίς ἂν καθεστηκὼς φήσαι; what person **of mature judgement** would say.. ? {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.15}}; ἡ καθεστηκυῖα ἡλικία **middle** age, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.36}}; ἡλικία μέση καὶ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 316c}}; οἱ καθεστηκότες **those of middle age**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.13}}: also, with metaphor from wine, **mellow**, of persons, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 45.8.}}
**B.5** **come into a certain state, become**, and in _perfect_ and _pluperfect_, **to have become, be,** ἀντὶ φίλου πολέμιόν τινι κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87; οἱ μὲν ὀφθαλμῶν ἰητροὶ κατεστέασι, οἱ δὲ κεφαλῆς [prev. author] 2.84; ἔμφρων καθίσταται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 306; τῶν ἄνωθεν ὑπόπτων καθεστώτων 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 13; ἐς μάχην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.45; ἐς πόλεμον ὑμῖν καὶ μάχην κ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1168; ἐς πάλην καθίσταται δορὸς τὸ πρᾶγμα [prev. author] “Heracl.” 159; ἐς τὴν ἴησιν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.12}}; ἐς τὸ αὐτό they **recover,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Coac.” 160 (later _absolutely_, καταστῆναι καὶ μηδενὸς ἔτι φαρμάκου δεηθῆναι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ λεπτυνούσης διαίτης - CMG” 1); ἐς τοὺς κινδύνους 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.1; ἐς φόβον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.12, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.81}}; ἐς δέος, λύπην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.108, 7.75; ἐς φυγήν [prev. author] 2.81; ἐς ἔχθραν τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.67}}; εἰς ὁμόνοιαν, εἰς πολλὴν ἀθυμίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.18, 12.3}}; καταστῆναι ἐς συνήθειάν τινος τὴν πόλιν ποιεῖν make the city **become** accustomed to it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.165; ἀντιστασιώτης κατεστήκεε}}**had been,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.92, compare 9.37; ἐν δείματι μεγάλῳ κατέστασαν [prev. author] 7.138; καταστάντων σφι εὖ τῶν πρηγμάτων [prev. work] 132}}; τίνι τρόπῳ καθέστατ; in what case **are ye?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 10}}; φονέα με φησὶ.. καθεστάναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 703; ἄπαρνος δ᾽ οὐδενὸς καθίστατο [prev. author] “Ant.” 435; κρυπτὸς καταστάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1064; οἱ ἐν τούτῳ τῆς ἡλικίας καθεστῶτες ἐν ᾧ.. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.1}}; ἐν οἵῳ τρόπῳ [ἡ τῶν Ἀθηναίων ἀρχὴ] κατέστη how it **came into being,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.97, compare 96}}; ἀρξάμενος εὐθὺς καθισταμένου (i.e. τοῦ πολέμου) from its first **commencement,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1.}}
**B.6** **to be established** or **instituted, prevail,** καί σφι μαντήϊον Διὸς κατέστηκε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29; ἄγραι.. πολλαὶ κατεστᾶσι [prev. work] 70, compare 1.200}}; ὅδε σφι νόμος κατεστήκεε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 197; βροτοῖσιν ὃς καθέστηκεν νόμος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 91}}: with _infinitive_, θεὸν Ἀμφιάραον πρώτοις Ὠρωπίοις κατέστη νομίζειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.34.2}}: _perfect_ _participle_, **existing, established, prevailing,** τὸν νῦν κατεστεῶτα κόσμον{{Refs|Hdt 1.65; ἦν κατεστηκὸς οὐδὲν φόρου πέρι [prev. author] 3.89}}; τοὺς κατεστεῶτας τριηκοσίους the **regular**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|300, [prev. author] 7.205; οἱ καθεστῶτες νόμοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1113, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1400}}; τὰ καθεστῶτα the **present state of life,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1160}}; also, **existing laws, usages,** τὰ τότε κ., τά ποτε κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 798b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.56; ἐπὶ τοῖσι κατεστεῶσι ἔνεμε τὴν πόλιν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59.}}
**B.7** of purchases, **cost,** πλέον ἢ ὅσου ἐμοὶ κατέστησαν more than **they stood** me **in,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.11, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.349a.}}
**B.8** **stand against, oppose,** πρός τινα uncertain reading in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.18.5}}:—_passive_, Τιτήνεσσι κατέσταθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 674.}}
**C** _aorist 1_ _middle_ and sometimes _present_ _middle_ are used in _transitive_ sense, see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.2f}}

2526:
** καθό**,
_adverb_ for καθ᾽ ὅ (which should perhaps be written), **in so far as, according as,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 34.5, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1022a14, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 31.16, NT.2Cor.8.12, etc.; κ. μεγέθει καὶ κ. ποιότητι}}**in respect of..,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.91 W.}}
**II** **wherefore,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 267d, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.51b.}}

2527:
** καθόλου**,
_adverb_ **on the whole, in general,** ={καθ᾽ ὅλου} (as it should perhaps be written), Epist.Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.77}}; κ. γράφειν, opposed to κατὰ μέρος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.32.8; κ. εἰπεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 156a13, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.397c, etc.}}; οἱ κ. λόγοι **general** statements, opposed to οἱ ἐπὶ μέρους, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107a30}} (but in Roman times, accounts **kept by the central government**, = Latin _summae rationes_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 715.3 (from Alexandria), 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 79.21, etc.)}}; τοῦτο γάρ ἐστι κ. μᾶλλον too **general**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1265a31, compare “GA” 748a8}}; ἡ τῶν κ. πραγμάτων σύνταξις **general** history, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.4.2}}; τὸ κ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.77, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.569f; τὸ κ. τῆς μοχθηρίας, opposed to τὸ πρὸς ἡμᾶς, [prev. work]468e; οὐδ᾽ οὗτος ἀποφαίνει κ. τὸ καταλειφθέν}} the **whole** amount left, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.43}}; ἐν τῷ κ. **in general, speaking generally**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.30e, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.8.8, +others}}
**2** in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, of terms, τὸ κ}}. **general**, opposed to τὸ καθ᾽ ἕκαστον (singular), λέγω δὲ κ. μὲν ὃ ἐπὶ πλειόνων πέφυκε κατηγορεῖσθαι, καθ᾽ ἕκαστον δὲ ὃ μή {{Refs|“Int.” 17a39, compare “Metaphorical” 1023b29}}; opposed to τὸ κατὰ μέρος, {{Refs|“Rh.” 1357b1, +others}}; hence, τὰ κ. **universal** truths, ἡ ποίησις μᾶλλον τὰ κ., ἡ δ᾽ ἱστορία τὰ καθ᾽ ἕ. λέγει [{{Refs|“Po.” 1451b7; ={γνῶμαι}, [prev. work] 1450b12}}]; especially **commensurate** predicate, ὃ ἂν κατὰ παντός τε ὑπάρχῃ καὶ καθ᾽ αὑτὸ καὶ ᾗ αὐτό {{Refs|“APo.” 73b26}}; as _adjective_, of propositions, λόγος κ. a **universal** statement, opposed to ἐν μέρει, κατὰ μέρος (particular), ἀδιόριστος (infinite), {{Refs|“APr.” 24a17}} {κάθομα}; of inference, ἡ κ. ἀπόδειξις **universal** proof, opposed to κατὰ μέρος, {{Refs|“APo.” 85a13}}; hence, as predicate, κ. εἰσὶν [αἱ ἀρχαί] {{Refs|“Metaphorical” 1003a7}}; as _adverb_, κ. ἀποφαίνεσθαι ἐπὶ τοῦ κ. {{Refs|“Int.” 17b5, +others}}
**3** **completely, entirely**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.20.2}}; οὐδὲ κ. μακρὸν πλοῖον no warships **at all**,{{LXX+5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 13, cf. LXX.Dan.3.50, +others; μηδὲ τέχνην εἶναι τὸ κ. τοῦ πείθειν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.327S. (Written κατὰ ὅλου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77a.)}}

2528:
** καθοπλ-ίζω**,
**equip, arm fully**, τῇ πανοπλία {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.154, cf. Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.116}}, Aristeas 14:—_middle_, **arm oneself fully**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 122, 160, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.62.7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 9, etc.}}; παντοπλίας κ. **arm oneself in.. **, {{LXX|LXX.4Mac.3.12}}:—_passive_, **to be so armed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.11; καθωπλισμένοι εἰς τὰ Μακεδονικά 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.27}}; θυμιατηρίῳ καθωπλισμένος **furnished with.. **, {{LXX|LXX.4Mac.7.11: _metaphorically_, καλοκἀγαθίᾳ [prev. work] 11.22.}}
**II** **array, set in order**: _metaphorically_, τὸ μὴ καλὸν καθοπλίσασα δύο φέρειν so **ordering** that which is not well as to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1087 (Lyric poetry, Scholia καταπολεμήσασα τὸ αἰσχρὸν καὶ νικήσασα).}}

2529:
** καθορ-άω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κατ-**, _imperfect_ καθεώρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.10}}, _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ κατώρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.208}}: _perfect_ καθεώρακα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 905b}}: _future_ κατόψομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.17}}: _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _perfect_ κατῶπται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432b}}: _aorist 1_ κατώφθην {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 46b}}: for _aorist_ _active_, see at {κατεῖδον}:— **look down**, ἐξ Ἴδης καθορῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.337; ἐπί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.44}}:— _middle_, ἐπὶ Θρῃκῶν καθορώμενος αἶαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.4.}}
**II** _transitive_, **look down upon**, ὅσους θνητοὺς ἠέλιος καθορᾷ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 14, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 168, 616, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.10; ὑψόθεν τὸν τῶν κάτω βίον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 216c, etc.: _metaphorically_, φρένα Δίαν κ., ὄψιν ἄβυσσον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1058 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_middle_, Τροίην κατὰ πᾶσαν ὁρᾶται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.291.}}
**II.2** **have within view, see distinctly, descry**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.208, 9.59, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 326, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 516a, etc.}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.20, 112, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 38d, etc.}}
**II.3** **behold, observe, perceive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.49, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 910.5 (_anapaest meter_)}}; καθορᾶν τι ἔν τινι **to observe** something therein, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 905b, compare “Grg.” 457c; τι ἐν τῇ ζητήσει [prev. author] “R.” 368e}}; ἵν᾽ ἃ πανουργεῖς μὴ καθορᾷ σου that he **may** not **observe** thy knavish tricks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 803}}; also κ. τὰς τρίχας εἰ.. **to look and see** whether.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38.}}
**II.4** **explore**, τὰ ἄλλα {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.17, compare 123.}}
**II.5** **regard, reverence**, τὸ τοῦ θεοῦ κράτος {{LXX|LXX.3Mac.3.11.}}

2531:
** καθώς**,
_adverb_ ={καθά}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.82 codices, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 891b34, “IG” 5(2).344.20 (4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 3rd c.BC), Wilcken “Chr.” 11 A 53 (2nd c.BC), “IG” 22.1030.22 (1st c.BC), +others}}; **even as,** {{NT|NT.John.15.12.}}
**2** **how**, ὑπομιμνῄσκειν κ... Aristeas 263,{{NT|cf. NT.Act.15.12.}}
**II** of Time, **as, when**,{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 7.17, LXX.2Mac.1.31, Aristeas 310. (Condemned by 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 397, 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 212.)}}

2532:
** καί**,
_conjunction_, copulative, joining words and sentences,
**A** **and**; also _adverb_, **even, also, just**, frequently expressing emphatic assertion or assent, corresponding as positive to the negative οὐ (μή) or οὐδέ (μηδέ). copulative, **and**,
**A.I** joining words or sentences to those preceding, ἦ, καὶ κυανέῃσιν ἐπ᾽ ὀφρύσινεῦσε Κρονίων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.528, etc.}}: repeated with two or more Nouns, αἱ δὲ ἔλαφοι κ. δορκάδες κ. οἱ ἄγριοι οἶες κ. οἱ ὄνοι οἱ ἄγριοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.7}}; joining only the last pair, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.1 (p.168.5 Z.), 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Analytica Priora commentaria - pars” 239.30, etc., see reading in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1451a20}}; ὁ ὄχλος πλείων κ. πλείων ἐπέρρει more **and** more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.39}}; to add epithets after πολύς, πολλὰ κ. ἐσθλά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.330; πολλὰ κ. μεγάλα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.1, etc.}}
**A.I.2** to addalimiting or defining expression, πρὸς μακρὸν ὄρος κ. Κύνθιον ὄχθον to the mountain **and specially** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 17, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 63 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1277 (_anapaest meter_)}} (sometimes in reverse order, πρὸς δῶμα Διὸς κ. μακρὸν Ὄλυμπον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.398)}}; to add by way of climax, θεῶν.. κ. Ποσειδῶνος all the gods, **and above all.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 750, etc.; ἐχθροὶ κ. ἔχθιστοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.68; τινὲς κ. συχνοί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 455c}}; frequently ἄλλοι τε καί.., ἄλλως τε καί.., see at {ἄλλος} {{Refs|11.6, ἄλλως 1.3}}; ὀλίγου τινὸς ἄξια κ. οὐδενός little **or** nothing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ap.” 23a: joined with the _demonstrative_ _pronoun_ οὗτος (which see), εἶναι.. δούλοισι, κ. τούτοισι ὡς δρηπέτῃσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.11, compare 1.147}}; κ. ταῦτα **and** this **too.. **, γελᾶν ἀναπείθειν, κ. ταῦθ᾽ οὕτω πολέμιον ὄντα τῷ γέλωτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.16, etc.}}
**A.II** at the beginning of a sentence,
**A.II.1** in appeals or requests, καί μοι δὸς τὴν Χεῖρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.75}}; καί μοι λέγε.., καί μοι ἀπόκριναι.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3a, “Grg.” 462b}}; frequently in Oratt., καί μοι λέγε.. τὸ ψήφισμα, καί μοι ἀνάγνωθι.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.105, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.8, etc.}}
**A.II.2** in questions, to introduce an objection or express surprise, κ. τίς τόδ᾽ ἐξίκοιτ᾽ ἂν ἀγγέλων τάχο; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 280}}; κ. πῶς..; **pray** how..? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1348}}; κ. δὴ τί..; **but** then what..? {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hel.” 101}}; κ. ποῖον..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 462}}; κ. τίς εἶδε πώποτε βοῦς κριβανίτα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 86}}; κἄπειτ᾽ ἔκανε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1398 (_anapaest meter_)}}; κ. τίς πώποτε Χαριζόμενος ἑτέρῳ τοῦτο εἰργάσατ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.57, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 163d, +others}}
**A.II.3** ={καίτοι}, **and yet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1245, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 509.}}
**A.II.4** at the beginning of a speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 36a.}}
**A.III** after words implying sameness or like ness, **as**, γνώμῃσι ἐχρέωντο ὁμοίῃσι κ. σύ they had the same opinion **as** you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.50, compare 84}}; ἴσον or ἴσα κ..., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 611, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 994}}; ἐν ἴσῳ (i.e. ἐστὶ) κ. εἰ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60, etc.}}
**A.III.2** after words implying comparison or opposition, αἱ δαπάναι οὐχ ὁμοίως κ. πρίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.28; πᾶν τοὐναντίον ἔχει νῦν τε κ. ὅτε.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 967a.}}
**A.III.3** to express simultaneity, ἦν ἦμαρ δεύτερον.., κἀγὼ κατηγόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 355, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.50}}; παρέρχονταί τε μέσαι νύκτες κ. ψύχεται [τὸ ὕδωρ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.181, compare 3.108}}; [οἱ Λακεδαιμόνιοι] οὐκ ἔφθασαν τὴν ἀρχὴν κατασχόντες κ. Θηβαίοις εὐθὺς ἐπεβούλευσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.98.}}
**A.IV** joining an affirmative clause with a _negative_, ἀλλ᾽ ὥς τι δράσων εἷρπε κοὐ θανούμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 160, etc.}}
**A.V** καί.., καί.. correlative, **not only.., but also.. **, κ. ἀεὶ κ. νῦν, κ. τότε κ. νῦν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 523a, “Phlb.” 60b; κ. κατὰ γῆν κ. κατὰ θάλατταν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.7.}}
**A.VI** by anacoluthon, ὣς φαμένη κ. κερδοσύνῃ ἡγήσατ᾽ Ἀθήνη, for ὣς ἔφη κ..., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.247}}; ἔρχεται δὲ αὐτή τε.. κ. τὸν υἱὸν ἔχουσα, for κ. ὁ υἱός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.1; ἄλλας τε κατηγεόμενοί σφι ὁδούς, κ. τέλος ἐγίνοντο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.104; τοιοῦτος ὤν, κᾆτ᾽ ἀνὴρ ἔδοξεν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 392, compare “Nu.” 624.}}
**B** **even, also, just**,
**B.1** τάχα κεν κ. ἀναίτιον αἰτιόῳτο **even** the innocent, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.654, compare 4.161, etc.}}; δόμεναι κ. μεῖζον ἄεθλον an **even** greater prize, {{Refs|23.551, compare 10.556, 5.362: with numerals, κ. πέντε}}**full** five,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.833; γενομένης κ. δὶς ἐκκλησίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.44, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.44, 60, 68, +others (but ἐτῶν δύο κ. τριῶν}} two **or** three, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.82, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 4.4).}}
**B.2** **also**, κ. ἐγώ I **also**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.40}}; κ. αὐτοί they **also**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.44, etc.}}; Ἀγίας καὶ Σωκράτης κ. τούτω ἀπεθανέτην **likewise** died, {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.6.30}}; in adding surnames, etc., Ὦχος ὁ κ. Δαρειαῖος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Ctesias Historicus “fragment” 29.49 (sed Photii est): 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus Papyri have _nominative_ ὃς κ., _genitive_ τοῦ κ. etc., Πανίσκος ὃς κ. Πετεμῖνις “PLond.” 2.219 (b) 2 (2nd c.BC); _dative_ τῷ κ. [prev. work](a) v2, “PRein.” 26.5 (2nd c.BC)}}; _nominative_ ὁ κ. first in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PTeb.” 110.1 (1st c.BC)}}, frequently later, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 22.25 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; Ἰούδας ὁ κ. Μακκαβαῖος {{NT+8th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.6.4; Σαῦλος ὁ κ. Παῦλος NT.Act.13.9: with ἄλλος, λαβέτω δὲ κ. ἄλλος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.152}}; εἴπερ τι κ. ἄλλο, ὥς τις κ. ἄλλος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.2, “An.” 1.3.15, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 356: frequently in antithetic phrases, οὐ μόνον.., ἀλλὰ καὶ..}}, not only.., but **also.. **, see at {μόνος}; οὐδὲν μᾶλλον.. ἢ οὐ καὶ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.94, +others}}
**B.2.b** frequently used both in the _antecedent_ and _relative_ clause, where we put **also** in the _antecedent_ only, εἰ μὲν κ. σὺ εἶ τῶν ἀνθρώπων ὧνπερ κ. ἐγώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 458a, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.476, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.21.}}
**B.3** frequently in apodosi, after temporal Conjs., ἀλλ᾽ ὅτε δή ῥα.., κ. τότε δή.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.494, compare 8.69, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.112}}; also after εἰ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.897: in Prose, ὡς δὲ ἔδοξεν, κ. ἐχώρουν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.93}}: as a Hebraism, κ. ἐγένετο.. κ... {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.24.30, +others, NT.Luke.1.59, etc.}}
**B.4** with Advs., to give emphasis, κ. κάρτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.125}}; κ. λίην **full** surely, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.408, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.46; κ. μᾶλλον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.470, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 386}}; κ. πάλαι, κ. πάνυ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1252, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 154e}}; κ. μάλα, κ. σφόδρα, in answers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1326, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 191e.}}
**B.5** with words expressing a minimum, **even so much as, were it but, just**, ἱέμενος κ. καπνὸν ἀποθρῴσκοντα νοῆσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.58}}; οἷς ἡδὺ κ. λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 528}}; τίς δὲ κ. προσβλέψετα; who will **so much as** look at you? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1192, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 614, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28b, 35b.}}
**B.6** **just**, τοῦτ᾽ αὐτὸ κ. νοσοῦμεν 'tis **just** that that ails me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 906, compare “Ba.” 616, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 490, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 892, “Ra.” 73, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 456a, “Tht.” 166d}}: frequently with a _relative_, τὸ κ. κλαίουσα τέτηκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.176; διὸ δὴ καὶ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.128, etc.: also in interrogations (usually to be rendered by emphasis in intonation), ποίου Χρόνου δὲ καὶ πεπόρθηται πόλις}}; and **how long ago** was the city sacked? {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 278}}; ποῦ καί σφε θάπτε; **where** is he burying her? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 834, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1290, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1289, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 6b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.46, etc.}}
**B.7** **even, just**, implying assent, ἔπειτά με κ. λίποι αἰών thereafter let life **e'en** leave me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.685, compare 17.647, 21.274, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.224.}}
**B.8** κ. εἰ **even if**, of a whole condition represented as an extreme case, opposed to εἰ κ. **although, notwithstanding that**, of a condition represented as immaterial even if fulfilled,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.347, 5.351, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.292, 16.98 with 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.410, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.312, 8.139, etc.}}; εἰ κ. ἠπιστάμην if I **had** been able, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 108d, compare “Lg.” 663d. (This remark does not apply to cases where εἰ and καί}} each exert their force separtely, as εἴ περ ἀδειής τ᾽ ἐστί, καὶ εἰ.. **and if.. ** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.117, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.78, etc.)}}
**B.9** before a Participle, to represent either καὶ εἰ.., or εἰ καί.., **although, albeit**, Ἕκτορα κ. μεμαῶτα μάχης σχήσεσθαι ὀΐω, for ἢν κ. μεμάῃ, **how much soever** he rage, **although** he rage, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.655; τί σὺ ταῦτα, κ. ἐσθλὸς ἐών, ἀγορεύει; (for εἰ κ. ἐσθλὸς εἶ) 16.627, compare 13.787, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.343, etc.; κ. τύραννος ὢν ὅμως 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 851.}}
**C** Position: καί **and**, is by Poets sometimes put after another word, ἔγνωκα, τοῖσδε κοὐδὲν ἀντειπεῖν ἔχω, for καὶ τοῖσδε οὐδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 51, compare 3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus 51.7, etc.}}
**C.2** καί **also**, sometimes goes between a _preposition_ and its case, ἐν κ. θαλάσσᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.28.}}
**C.3** very seldom at the end of a verse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 312, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1193.}}
**D** crasis: with ᾰ, as κἄν, κἀγαθοί, etc.; with ε, as κἀγώ, κἄπειτα, etc., _Doric dialect_ κἠγώ, κἤπειτα, etc.; with η, as Χἠ, Χἠμέρη, Χἠμεῖς, etc.; with ῐ in Χἰκετεύετε, Χἰλαρ; with ο, as Χὠ, Χὤστις, etc.; with υ in Χὐμεῖς, Χὐποχείριον, etc.; with ω in the _pronoun_ ᾧ, Χ; with αι, as κᾀσχρῶ; with αυ, as καὐτό; with ει, as κεἰ, κεἰς (but also κἀς), κᾆτ; with εὐ-, as κεὐγένεια, κεὐσταλή; with οι in Χοἰ (Χᾠ {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 816.34)}}; with ου in Χοὖτος, κοὐ, κοὐδέ, and the like.

2537:
** καινός**, ή, όν,
**new, fresh**, ἔργα οὔτ᾽ ὦν κ. οὔτε παλαιά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.26; κ. ὁμιλία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 406}}; κ. λόγους φέρειν to bring **news**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 659}}; τίδ᾽ ἐστὶ κ.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 722, compare “Ph.” 52; τὰ κ. τοῖς πάλαι τεκμαίρεται [prev. author] “OT” 916; θυτῆρα καινῷ καινὸν ἐν πεπλώματι [prev. author] “Tr.” 613}}; ἢ βούλεσθε περιιόντες πυνθάνεσθαι, “λέγεταί τι κ.;” {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.10; γένοιτ᾽ ἄν τι -ότερον ἤ.. [prev. passage]; ἐκ καινῆς (i.e. ἀρχῆς)}} **anew, afresh**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.92, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.1.11, “Jahresh.” 23 “Beibl.” 91 (Pamphyl., 1st c.AD)}}, etc. (also ἐκ καινοῦ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“CPR” 244.14 (3rd c.AD))}}; especially of **new** dramas, τραγῳδῶν γιγνομένων καινῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.34}}; briefly τραγῳδοῖς κ. at the representation of the **new** tragedies, Docum. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.54; τραγῳδῶν τῇ κ. [ἐπιδείξει] [prev. work]55; καινῇ κωμῳδῶν, τραγῳδῶν, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2759iii (from Aphrodisias)}}; but κ. κωμῳδία, τραγῳδία, of a **new style** of drama, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 7.1773 (Thebes, 2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **newly-made**, κύλικες, τριήρης, ὀθόνια, οἶνος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1026.26 (Cos, 4th-3rd c.BC), “IG” 22.1623.289, “PLond.” 2.402v12 (2nd c.BC), “Ostr.” 1142.4 (3rd c.AD).}}
**3** _adverb_ -νῶς **newly, afresh**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 240.4.}}
**II** **newly-invented, novel**, καινότεραι τέχναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 116; κ. προσφέρειν σοφά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 298; ἔνθα τι κ. ἐλέχθη 3rd c.BC: Philoxenus Epigrammaticus 3.23; οὐκ ἀείδω τὰ παλαιά, καινὰ γὰρ ἀμὰ κρείσσω 5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Fragmenta” 21}}; κ. θεοί **strange** gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3b; κ. δαιμόνια [prev. author] “Ap.” 24c; κ. τινες σοφισταί [prev. author] “Euthd.” 271b; κ. καὶ ἄτοπα ὀνόματα [prev. author] “R.” 405d}}; καινὰ ἐπιμηχανᾶσθαι **innovations**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.16}}; οὐδὲν -ότερον εἰσέφερε τῶν ἄλλων he introduced **as little of anything new as** others, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.1.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 115b; πεπόνθαμεν -ότατον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.26}}; τὸ κ. τοῦ πολέμου probably _falsa lectio_ for{κενόν} (see. κενός), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.30}}; οὐ καινόν nothing **to be surprised at**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 17}}; τὸ -ότατον **what is strangest**, parenthetically, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 22, +others; εἰ Χρὴ -ότατα μᾶλλον ἢ κακουργότατα εἰπεῖν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.2.}} _adverb_, μὴ σὺ -νῶς μοι λάλει **in new, strange style**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 144, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 267b}}: _comparative_ -οτέρως, νοῆσαι περί τινος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 308b31}}; **without precedent**, -νῶς κατακριθῆναι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 669.46,49 (Egypt, 1st c.AD).}}
**III** κ. ἄνθρωπος, = Latin _novus homo_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 1}}; πράγματακ., = **res novae**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cic.” 14, compare 2.212c.}}

2538:
** καινότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**newness, freshness**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 13; αἱ τῶν δερμάτων -τητες 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 18.}}
**2** **novelty**, λόγου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38; τῶν εὑρημένων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.2; Χρὴ γὰρ εἰς ὄχλον φέρειν.. ὅσ᾽ ἄν τις καινότητ᾽ ἔχειν δοκῇ 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 54.6}}; ἡ ἐν τοῖς σχηματισμοῖς κ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 2.3: _plural_, καινότητες}} **novelties**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.41; αἱ κ. καὶ αἱ ὑπερβολαὶ τῶν τιμῶν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 44.3.}}

2539:
** καίπερ**,
in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always with a word between (except καί περ πολλὰ παθόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.224)}}; but one word in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus and Prose, and usually in Trag.}}
**I** **even**, καὶ αὐτοί περ πονεώμεθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.70.}}
**II** **although, albeit**, usually with _participle_, καὶ αὐτῇ περ νοεούσῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.577; καὶ ἀχνύμενός περ ἑταίρου 8.125; καὶ πρίν περ θυμῷ μεμαώς 5.135; καὶ κήδεά περ πεπαθυίῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.555}}: so in later Poets, κ. ἀχνύμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).4, compare “N.” 6.6; καὶ θοῦρός περ ὤν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 199.2; κ. αὐθάδη φρονῶν [prev. author] “Pr.” 907; κ. οὐ στέργων ὅμως [prev. author] “Th.” 712; κ. οὐ δύσοργος ὤν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 377: preceded by ὅμως, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 495d: the}}_participle_ must frequently be supplied, καὶ θεός περ [ὤν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1203}}; γιγνώσκω σαφῶς, κ. σκοτεινὸς [ὤν], τήν γε σὴν αὐδὴν ὅμως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1326}}; also εἰ μέμονάς γε, καὶ ὀψέ περ [ἐρυόμενος],.. ἐρύεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.247; ἐπιμνησαίμεθα Χάρμης, καὶ πρὸς δαίμονά περ [μαχούμενοι] 17.104}}; λέγεις ἀληθῆ, κ. ἐκ μακροῦ Χρόνου [λέγων] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1141}}; ἀλλ᾽ ἔστιν ὧν δεῖ, κ. οὐ πολλῶν ἄπο, ={καίπερ οὐ πολλῶν ὄντων}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 647: with finite Verbs only as uncertain reading, κ. ἔχει (to be read καἴπερ) 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.36}}; κ. (to be read καίτοι) ἐκεῖνό γε ᾤμην τι εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 219c.}}

2540:
** καιρός**, ὁ,
**due measure, proportion, fitness** (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), καιρὸς δ᾽ ἐπὶ πᾶσιν ἄριστος (which became a proverbial) 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 694, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 401; κ. παντὸς ἔχει κορυφάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.78; κ. Χάριτος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 787 (_anapaest meter_) (compare ὑποκάμπτω 11)}}; εἰ ὁ κ. ἦν σαφής the **distinction**, the **point**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 386}}; ἡ ἀπορία ἔχει τινὰ κ. has some **point** or **importance**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1043b25}}; καιροῦ πέρα beyond **measure, unduly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 507; μείζων τοῦ κ. γαστήρ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.19; καιροῦ μεῖζον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 626}} codices; προσωτέρω or πορρωτέρω τοῦ κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.34, “HG” 7.5.13}}; ὀξύτερα τοῦ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 307b; νωθεστέρα τοῦ κ. [prev. work] 310e}}; ὑπερβάλλων τῇ φιλοτιμίᾳ τὸν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 8, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 44.}}
**II** of Place, **vital part** of the body {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (καίριος 1), ἐς καιρὸν τυπείς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1120.}}
**III** more frequently of Time, **exact** or **critical time, season, opportunity**, Χρόνου κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1292}}: usually alone, κ. [ἐστιν] ἐν ᾧ Χρόνος οὐ πολὺς κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Praec.” 1, cf. Chrysipp. et 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in Daremberg}} **Notices etextr. des MSS. médicaux**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1p.200; κ. ὀξύς 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.1}}; κ. πρὸς ἀνθρώπων βραχὺ μέτρον ἔχει 'time and tide wait for no man', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.286}}; κ. ὄλβου, ={καίριος ὄλβος}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “N.” 7.58}}; δηλοῦν, ὅ τι περ δύναται κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 576 codices (sed to be read δύνασαι)}}; τίνα κ. τοῦ παρόντος βελτίω ζητεῖτ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.16}}; κ. δόσιος **for** giving, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 20}}; κ. τοῦ ποτισμοῦ, τῆς τρύγης, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“BGU” 1003.12 (3rd c.BC), “PStrassb.” 1.8 (V 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator); τὰ ἐκ τοῦ κ. προγινόμενα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.32.3}}; καιρὸν παριέναι to let **the time** go by, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.27 (so in _plural_, τοὺς κ. παριέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 374c; τοὺς κ. ὑφαιρεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.66); κ. τῶν πραγμάτων τοῖς ἐναντίοις καθυφιέναι καὶ προδοῦναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.6}}; καιροῦ (τοῦ κ.) τυχεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 593, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 687a, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 281; καιρὸν εἰληφέναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.6 (but καιρὸς ἐλάμβανε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34; compare καιροῦ διδόντος 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 45.7); καιροῦ λαβέσθαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 13; καιρὸν ἁρπάσαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 15; κ. τηρεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1382b11; καιρῷ Χρήσασθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 7}}; καιρῷ Χειμῶνος ξυλλαβέσθαι co-operate with the **occurrence** of a storm, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 709c}}; ἔχει κ. τι it happens **in season**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.42, etc.}}; κ. ἔχειν τοῦ εὖ οἰκεῖν to be **the chief cause** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 421a; ὑμέας καιρός ἐστι προβοηθῆσαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 523, etc.; νῦν κ. ἔρδειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1368: sometimes with _Article_, ἀλλ᾽ ἔσθ᾽ ὁ κ... ξένους.. τυγχάνειν τὰ πρόσφορα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 710; ὁ κ. ἐστι μὴ μέλλειν ἔτι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 661, compare “Pl.” 255.}}
**III.b** adverbial phrases, ἐς καιρόν **in season**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.144, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 744, etc.; ἐς κ. ἐπείγεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.139}}; ἐς αὐτὸν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1168}}; εἰς δέοντα κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 294; ἐν καιρῷ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 381, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.59, etc.; ἐν κ. τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44a; ἐπὶ καιροῦ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.258, 20.90, etc.; κατὰ καιρόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.22; ὥς οἱ κατὰ κ. ἦν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30 (but also οἱ κατὰ κ. ἡγεμόνες}} in office at **the time**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 15.10 (2nd c.AD), etc.)}}; παρὰ τῷ ἐντυχόντι αἰεὶ καὶ λόγου καὶ ἔργου κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.43; πρὸς καιρόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 38, “Tr.” 59, etc.; σὺν καιρῷ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.38.7: without Preps., καιρῷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1516}}; καιρόν, _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 34, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 495.9 (in _comparative_ form καιρότερον, 5th c.BC: Achaeus Tragicus 49)}}; κ. γὰρ οὐδὲν ἦλθες {{Refs|E “Hel.” 479}}; opposed to ἀπὸ καιροῦ **out of season**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187e; ἄνευ καιροῦ [prev. author] “Ep.” 339d; παρὰ καιρόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.24, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 8c o (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 277a}}; πρὸ καιροῦ **prematurely**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 365 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἐπὶ καιροῦ also means **on the spur of the moment**, ἐπὶ κ. λέγειν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 8, compare “Art.” 5; ἐξενεγκεῖν πόλεμον [prev. author] “Ant.” 6.}}
**III.2** **season**, πᾶσιν καιροῖς at all **seasons** of the year, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|“IG” 14.1018, cf. LXX.Gen.1.14, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.13, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 3.11}}; κ. ἔτους, later Gr. for _Attic dialect_ ὥρα ἔτους, _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 424}}; **time of day**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.14.}}
**III.2.b** **critical times, periodic states**, καιροὶ σωμάτων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1335a41.}}
**III.3** generally, **time, period**, κατὰ τὸν κ. τοῦτον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 27.1.7; κατ᾽ ἐκεῖνο καιροῦ 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Conon Historicus 3, +others: more frequently in _plural_, κατὰ τούτους τοὺς κ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 23.2, +others, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.39.1}}; τὰ κατὰ καιρούς **chronological sequence** of events, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.33.5}}; ἐν τοῖς πάλαι, ἐντοῖς μεταξὺ κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.28,363 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III.4** in _plural_, οἱ καιροί **the times**, i. e. **the state of affairs**, frequently in bad sense, ἐν τοῖς μεγίστοις κ. at the most **critical times**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.33, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.44; περιστάντων τῇ πόλει κ. δυσκόλων “IG” 22.682.33, etc.}}: also in _singular_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.44, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.9}}; ὁ ἔσχατος κ. extreme **danger**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 29.27.12, etc.; καιρῷ δουλεύειν “Anthologia Graeca” 9.441 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus).}}
**IV** **advantage, profit**, τινος **of** or **from** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.54, “P.” 1.57}}; εἴ τοι ἐς κ. ἔσται ταῦτα τελεόμενα to his **advantage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.206}}; ἐπὶ σῷ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 151 (Lyric poetry)}}; τίνα κ. με διδάσκει; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1060 (Lyric poetry)}}; τί σοι καιρὸς.. καταλείβει; what **avails** it..? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 131 (Lyric poetry)}}; τίνος εἵνεκα καιρο; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.182}}; οὗ κ. εἴη where it was **convenient** or **advantageous**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.54; ᾗ κ. ἦν [prev. work] 90}}; Χωρίον μετὰ μεγίστων κ. οἰκειοῦταί τε καὶ πολεμοῦται with the greatest **odds**, the most **critical results**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.36.}}
**V** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **seven,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 44.}}

2543:
** καί τοι**,
**and indeed, and further**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} with one or more words between, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.426, +others; καὶ σύ τοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 344}}; **καὶ** τἆλλά τοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.10: once in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus as one word, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.267.}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} usually, **and yet**, to mark an objection introduced by the speaker himself, frequently in Rhetorical questions, καίτοι τί φημ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 101}}; κ. τί φων; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1132, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.99, etc.}}: without a question, κ. φύγοιμ᾽ ἄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 480; κ. καὶ τοῦτο.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.12, 18.122: strengthened, καίτοι γ᾽ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 611, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 953.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.3, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.274, etc.: mostly separated, καίτοι.. γε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 77, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 43, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.12.7, etc. (καίτοι}} is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 849)}}; so καίτοι περ variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.53.}}
**III** with a participle, much like{καίπερ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 511d, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.8.13, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.22 W., 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 3}}: once in the _Attic dialect_ Oratt., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.34; also καίτοι γε διαχλευάζων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 364b.}}

2544:
Related to: ** καί τοι**,
**and indeed, and further**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} with one or more words between, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.426, +others; καὶ σύ τοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 344}}; **καὶ** τἆλλά τοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.10: once in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus as one word, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.267.}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} usually, **and yet**, to mark an objection introduced by the speaker himself, frequently in Rhetorical questions, καίτοι τί φημ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 101}}; κ. τί φων; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1132, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.99, etc.}}: without a question, κ. φύγοιμ᾽ ἄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 480; κ. καὶ τοῦτο.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.12, 18.122: strengthened, καίτοι γ᾽ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 611, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 953.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.3, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.274, etc.: mostly separated, καίτοι.. γε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 77, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 43, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.12.7, etc. (καίτοι}} is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 849)}}; so καίτοι περ variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.53.}}
**III** with a participle, much like{καίπερ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 511d, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.8.13, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.22 W., 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 3}}: once in the _Attic dialect_ Oratt., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.34; also καίτοι γε διαχλευάζων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 364b.}}

2545:
** καίω**,
_Attic dialect_ **κάω** [ᾱ], _imperfect_ ἔκαιον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.553, _Attic dialect_ ἔκᾱον}}, _Epic dialect_ καῖον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.343}}: _future_ καύσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.21, (ἐπι-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 186.4, (κατα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1218; also καύσομαι [prev. author] “Pl.” 1054}}: _aorist 1_ ἔκαυσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pax” 1088, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.80 (bis), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 456b, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔκηα (certain _active_ and _middle_ forms have κει- in codices of {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, see below), ἔκηα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.40, +others}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔκηε (ν) {{Refs|22.170, 24.34, +others}}; unaugm. κῆεν {{Refs|21.349}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔκηαν (variant{ἔκειαν}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.336; _imperative_ κεῖον 21.176}} codices; _1st pers._ _plural_ _subjunctive_ κείομεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.333}} (κατα-), {{Refs|377, 396 (better attested than κήομεν); _optative_ κήαι, κήαιεν, 21.336, 24.38}}; _infinitive_ κῆαι [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.97 (variant{κεῖαι}), κατα-κῆαι 10.533, 11.46, κακκῆαι [prev. work] 74 (variant{κακκεῖαι})}}]; _participle_ κείαντες {{Refs|9.231, 13.26}}, _Attic dialect_ κέαντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 849, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 757, (ἐκ-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 97, ἐκκέας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1133 (Lyric poetry), ἐγκέαντι “IG” 12.374.96,261:}}_perfect_ κέκαυκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(κατα-, προσ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.37, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 124.3:}} —_middle_, _aorist 1_ ἐκαυσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(ἀν-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.202, 8.19}}; _Epic dialect_ κείαντο, κειάμενοι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.88, 234; κειάμενος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.2, 23.51}}:—_passive_, _future_ καυθήσομαι {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 107, (κατα-, ἐκ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1505, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 362a; late κᾰήσομαι NT.1Cor.3.15}}: _aorist 1_ ἐκαύθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.4, “Int.” 28, (κατ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.19, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.74}}; _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialectaorist 2_ ἐκάην [ᾰ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.212 (κατ-), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.13, (δί-) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 40, (κατ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.180}}; _infinitive_ καήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.210, καῆναι 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 9.8}}: _perfect_ κέκαυμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 457, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.34, etc., κέκαυσμαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 28}}; _infinitive_ κεκαῦσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 343a9. (From κᾰϝ-yw.)}}
**I** **kindle**, πυρὰ πολλά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.77; πῦρ κείαντες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.231; πῦρ κῆαι 15.97, etc.:}} —_middle_, πῦρ κείαντο **they lighted them** a fire, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.88, compare 234, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.2}}:—_passive_, **to be lighted, burn**, πυραὶ νεκύων καίοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.52; θεείου καιομένοιο 8.135; καιομένοιο πυρός 19.376, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1372, etc.; φῶς πυρὸς καόμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 514b}}; αἱ φλόγες αἱ καιόμεναι.. περὶ τὸν οὐρανόν the meteors **which blaze**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 341b2}}; of ore, **to be smelted**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 552b10.}}
**II** **set on fire, burn**, μηρία, ὀστέα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.553, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 557; νεκρούς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.343; δένδρεα [prev. work] 338}}:—_passive_, νηυσὶν καιομένῃσιν {{Refs|9.602.}}
**II.2** **make hot**, of the sun, ἀνθρώπους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.104: _absolutely_, [prev. passage], 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 413b}}; [Χείμαρρος] ἠελίῳ κεκαυμένος **smelted,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 9.277 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.3** of extreme cold, ἡ Χιὼν καίει τῶν κυνῶν τὰς ῥῖνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 8.2, compare 6.26 (_passive_)}}; κάειν λέγεται.. τὸ ψυχρόν, οὐχ ὡς τὸ θερμόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 382b8.}}
**II.4** _passive_, of fever-heat, τὰ ἐντὸς ἐκάετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49}}: _metaphorically_, of passion, especially of love, **to be on fire**, ἐν φρασὶ καιομένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.219; κάομαι τὴν καρδίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 9; ἔρως.. ὕβρει καόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 783a}}; καίεσθαί τινος (i.e. ἔρωτι) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Hermesianax Elegiacus 7.37, 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 4.6, compare 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 14.2}}; also καομένη Ἑλλάς Greece **being in a fever** of excitement, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 33.7.}}
**II.5** **suffer from inflammation**, ἐκαύθη ἔσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.20, compare 4.}}
**III** **burn and destroy** (in war), τέμνειν καὶ κ., κ. καὶ πορθεῖν, **waste with fire** and sword, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.2.15, 6.5.27.}}
**IV** of surgeons, **cauterize**, ὤμους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 11}}:—in _passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aph.” 6.60: _absolutely_, τέμνειν καὶ κάειν}} **to use** knife and **cautery**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480c, 521e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.18, etc.: rarely reversed, κέαντες ἢ τεμόντες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 849.}}
**V** **burn** or **bake** pottery, κανθάρους uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 15.}}

2546:
Included with: ** ἐκεῖ**
(not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ **κῆ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 51}}: _Doric dialect_ **τηνεῖ** (which see):— _adverb_ **there, in that place,** opposed to ἐνθάδε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.83; οἱ ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 685, etc.}}; τἀκεῖ **what is** or **happens there, events there,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 578.5, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90; redundant, οὗ ἦν ἐ. LXX.1Ki.9.10.}}
**2** frequently as euphemistic for ἐν Ἅιδου, **in another world,** κἀκεῖ δικάζει τἀμπλακήματα Ζεὺς ἄλλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 230, compare “Ch.” 359 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 76; εὐδαιμονοίτην, ἀλλ᾽ἐ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1073; εὔκολος μὲν ἐνθάδ᾽, εὔκολος δ᾽ ἐκεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 82, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 64a, +others; in full, ἐκεῖ δ᾽ ἐν Ἅιδου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 418}}; οἱ ἐ. euphemistic for **the dead,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 355 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 776, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 427b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.61.}}
**3** Philos., **in the intelligible world,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.2.7, 2.4.5, etc.}}
**II** with Verbs of motion, for ἐκεῖσε, **thither,** ἐ. πλέομεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.147}}; ἐ. ἀπικέσθαι variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.108; ὁδοῦ τῆς ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1019; οἱ ἐ. καταπεφευγότες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.71, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.101.10; βλέψον δὲ κἀκεῖ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 103.}}
**III** rarely, of Time, **then,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 395 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.38.}}

2547:
Related to: ** ἐκεῖθεν**,
_poetry_ **κεῖθεν** (the only form used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, also in Trag. where metre requires)}}: _Aeolic dialect_ **κήνοθεν** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 86}}: _Doric dialect_ **τηνῶθεν** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 754}}; **τηνῶθε** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.10}}:— _adverb_ **from that place, thence,** opposed to ἐκεῖσε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 490, etc.}}; of a person, τἀκεῖθεν εἰ ποθούμεθα **on his part,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 632; ὁ ἐ. ἄγγελος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 619b; τὸ σκῆπτρον ἐ. παραλαβόντες 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.181b.}}
**2** ={ἐκεῖ}, οἱ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.62: with _genitive_, τοὐκεῖθεν ἄλσους}} **on yon side of** the grove, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 505; ἕζοντο τὸ κεῖθεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1411 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** by attraction for ἐκεῖσε, βῆναι κεῖθεν ὅθενπερ ἥκει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1227 codices (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **thence, from that fact,** γνοίη δ᾽ ἄν τις ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.224, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.48, etc.}}
**III** of Time, **thenceforward,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.234; ἐ. ἤδη 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 54.25.}}

2548:
Related to: ** ἐκεῖνος**, ἐκείνη, ἐκεῖνο,
also **κεῖνος** (regular in _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_ (as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 37.3 (Teos, 5th c.BC), though 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus prefers ἐκεῖνος), and Lyric poetry, in Trag. κεῖνος}} only where the metre requires,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 230, 792, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 220 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.; but not in _Attic dialect_ Prose, and in Comedy texts only in mock Trag. passages): _Aeolic dialect_ **κῆνος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.1}}: _Doric dialect_ **τῆνος** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.4, etc.}}: in Comedy texts, strengthened **ἐκεινοσί** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 277 (probably), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1196, etc.}}; **ἐκεινοσίν** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24}}: (ἐκεῖ):—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_ **the person there, that person** or **thing,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}: generally with reference to what has gone immediately before, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 106c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.9, etc.}}; but when οὗτος and ἐκεῖνος refer to two things before mentioned, ἐκεῖνος, properly belongs to **the more remote,** in time, place, or thought, οὗτος to **the nearer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 271b, etc.: but ἐκεῖνος}} sometimes = **the latter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.13, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1325a7, etc.: ἐκεῖνος is frequently the predicate to οὗτος or ὅδε, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος τὸν σὺ ζητέεις 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622}}; ἆρ᾽ οὗτός ἐστ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ὅν..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 240, etc.}}: also joined as if one _pronoun_, τοῦτ᾽ ἐκεῖνο..δέρκομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1115, etc.}}; κατ᾽ ἐκεῖνο καιροῦ at **that point** of time, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 32, etc.; ἐς ἐ. τοῦ χρόνου 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 46.49}}; ἀλλ᾽ ἐκεῖνο, **à propos,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 8.}}
**2** to denote wellknown persons, etc., κεῖνος μέγας θεός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.90; ἐκεῖνος ἡνίκ᾽ ἦν Θουκυδίδης 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 708; καίτοι φασὶν Ἰφικράτην ποτ᾽ ἐκεῖνον.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.62; ὦ παῖ 'κείνου τἀνδρός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 36d.}}
**2.b** ἐκεῖνα **the ideal world,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 250a.}}
**3** for things, of which one cannot remember or must not mention the name, ={ὁ δεῖνα}, **so-and-so,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 195.}}
**3.b** in formulae, τεθνάτω καὶ οἱ παῖδες οἱ ἐξ ἐκείνου {{Refs|“IG” 12.10.33.}}
**4** with simple _demonstrative_ force, Ἶρος ἐκεῖνος ἧσται Irus sits **there,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.239}}; νῆες ἐκεῖναι ἐπιπλέουσιν **there** are ships sailing up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.51.}}
**5** in indirect speech where properly the _reflexive_ _pronoun_ αὑτοῦ would stand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.14, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.22, etc.}}
**6** after a Relat. in apodosi almost _pleonastic_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.19 (assuming variant).}}
**7** in _Aeolic dialect_ and _Attic dialect_ the _substantive_ with ἐκεῖνος properly has the _Article_ (κῆνος ὤνηρ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 25.6), and ἐκεῖνος}} may precede or follow the _substantive_, ἐκείνῃ τῇ ἡμέρᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 57a}}; τὴν στρατείαν ἐ., τὸν ἄνδρ᾽ ἐ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 649: in Poets the _Article_ is frequently omitted, ἤματι κείνῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.37, etc.}}; but when this is the case in Prose, ἐκεῖνος follows the _substantive_, ἡμέρας ἐκείνης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59, etc.}}
**II** _adverb_ ἐκείνως **in that case,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.77, 3.46}}; **in that way,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 27; ζῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 516d, etc.}}: _Ionic dialect_ **κείνως** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120.}}
**III** _dative_ _feminine_ ἐκείνῃ as _adverb_,
**III.1** of Place, **at that place, in that neighbourhood,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.106, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.77, etc.}}; κείνῃ (i.e. ὁδῷ) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.111.}}
**III.2** of Manner, **in that manner,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556a, etc.}}
**IV** with Preps., ἐξ ἐκείνου from **that time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.17; ἀπ᾽ ἐκείνου 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 2.2}}; κατ᾽ ἐκεῖνα in **that region,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.17, etc.; μετ᾽ ἐκεῖνα}}**afterwards,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.81}}; compare ἐπέκεινα.

2549:
** κᾰκία**, ἡ,
(κακός) **badness** in quality, opposed to ἀρετή (excellence), {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 322, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 512 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 181e, “R.” 348c, etc.}}; κακίᾳ ἡνιόχων by their **incapacity**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 248b: _plural_, κακίαι}} **defects**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 6.}}
**2** **cowardice, faint-heartedness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.87, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556d; κ. καὶ ἀνανδρία [prev. author] “Cri.” 46a.}}
**3** **moral badness, vice**, μετ᾽ ἀρετῆς ἀλλ᾽ οὐ μετὰ κακίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.56}}; ἡ ἀρετή, ὡσαύτως δὲ.. καὶ ἡ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 72a, etc.}}; personified in the Fable of Prodicus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.26: _plural_, περὶ κακιῶν, title of treatise by Philodemus.}}
**4** Philos., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Evil”, ὕλη κακίας αἰτία 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.8.14.}}
**II** **ill-repute, dishonour**, κ. ἀντιλαβεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58.}}
**II.2** **hurt, damage** done or suffered, {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ki.6.9, LXX.1Mac.7.23, NT.Matt.6.34.}}

2550:
** κᾰκοήθ-εια**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **bad disposition, malignity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 348d, 401a, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 32}} (-ηθία), {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Rom.1.29; κ. τὸ ἐπὶ τὸ Χεῖρον ὑπολαμβάνειν ἅπαντα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1389b20: _plural_, κ. ὑπὲρ τοῦ πράγματος λεγόμεναι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.166, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.284, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 96.2.}}
**II** **bad manners** or **habits**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.16.}}
**III** in Medicine texts, **malignant character**, τῆς νόσου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 471}}: in _plural_, **malignant diseases** or **growths**, [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.92 (variant for{τὰ -ήθη}).}}]

2554:
** κᾰκοποι-έω**,
**do ill, play the knave**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 111, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 731}}; **manage one's affairs ill**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 3.11.}}
**II** _transitive_, **do mischief to, injure**, πολλὰ μὲν τὴν βασιλέως Χώραν κ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 3.5.26, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.6.10; τὰς νῆας [prev. author] 13.4.1}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 27.7.6.}}

2556:
** κᾰκός**, ή, όν,
**A** **bad**:
**A.I** of persons,
**A.I.1** of appearance, **ugly**, εἶδος μὲν ἔην κακός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.316, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.49.3.}}
**A.I.2** of birth, **ill-born, mean**, γένος ἐστὲ διοτρεφέων βασιλήων.., ἐπεὶ οὔ κε κακοὶ τοιούσδε τέκοιεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.64; Ζεὺς δ᾽ αὐτὸς νέμει ὄλβον.. ἐσθλοῖς ἠδὲ κακοῖσι 6.189; οὐ κακὸν οὐδὲ μὲν ἐσθλόν 22.415; οὐδ᾽ ἐὰν.. φανῶ τρίδουλος, ἐκφανῇ κακή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1063; κακός τ᾽ ὢν κἀκ κακῶν [prev. work] 1397.}}
**A.I.3** of courage, **craven, base**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.365, 6.489; κακοῦ τρέπεται Χρὼς ἄλλυδις ἄλλῃ (called δειλὸς ἀνήρ in the line above) 13.279}}; Ἕκτωρ σε κ. καὶ ἀνάλκιδα φήσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.153, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.375; κ. καὶ ἀνήνορα 10.301; οἵτινες.. ἐγένοντο ἄνδρες κ. ἢ ἀγαθοὶ ἐν τῇ ναυμαχίῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.14; κ. καὶ ἄθυμος [prev. author] 7.11}}; οὐδαμῶν κακίονες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 104; κακοὺς πρὸς αἰχμήν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1306; κακή τ᾽ ἐς ἀλκὴν καὶ σίδηρον εἰσορᾶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 264; οὐδενὶ ἐπιτρέψοντας κακῷ εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.31.}}
**A.I.4** **bad** of his kind, i. e. **worthless, sorry, unskilled**, ἡνίοχοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.487}}; [τοξότης] ἢ κ. ἢ ἀγαθός{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 632; νομῆες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.246}}; κ. ἀλήτης a **bad** beggar,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 578; ἰατρός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 473}}; κυβερνήτης, ναύτης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 880, “Andr.” 457; μάγειρος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 265e: with _accusative_ modi, πάντα γὰρ οὐ κακός εἰμι}} I am not **bad** in all things, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.214; κ. γνώμην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 910: also with _dative_, κακοὶ γνώμαισι [prev. author] “Aj.” 964}}: with _infinitive_, κ. μανθάνειν {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 545}}; [νῆσος] φυτεύεσθαι κακή {{Refs|“Tragica Adespota” 393}}; compare 11.
**A.I.5** in moral sense, **base, evil**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.384, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 240}}; opposed to Χρηστός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 520; ὦ κακῶν κάκιστε [prev. author] “OT” 334, “Ph.” 984; πλεῖστον κάκιστος [prev. author] “OC” 744; κ. πρός τινας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.86; εἰς φίλους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 424 codices; περὶ τὰ Χρήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Clitopho” 407c.}}
**A.I.6** **wretched**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.42.}}
**A.II** of things, **evil, pernicious**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc., as δαίμων, θάνατος, μοῖρα, αἶσα, κῆρες, νοῦσος, ἕλκος, φάρμακα, ὀδύναι, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.64, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.173, 13.602, 1.418, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.316, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.10, 2.723, 22.94, 5.766}}; Χόλος, ἔρις, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.206, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.161}}; πόλεμος, ἔπος, ἔργα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.82, 24.767, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.67, +others}}; ἦμαρ, ἄνεμος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.251, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.109}}; of omens and the like, **unlucky**, ὄρνις, ὄναρ, σῆμα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.219, 10.496, 22.30: also in Trag., κ. τύχη, δαίμων, μόρος, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 328, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 354, 369, etc.}}; of words, **abusive, foul**, κ. λόγοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 259, compare “Tr.” 461}}; κ. ποιμήν, i.e. the storm, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 657}}: Astrology texts, **unlucky**, τόποι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.12}}; κ. τύχη, name for the sixth region, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “M.” 1.}}
**B** κακόν, τό, and κακά, τά, as _substantive_, **evil, ill**, δίδου δ᾽ ἀγαθόν τε κακόν τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.63; ἀθάνατον κακόν 12.118; ἐκ μεγάλων κακῶν πεφευγέναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.65}}; so κ. ἄμαχον, ἄπρηκτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.76, “I.” 8(7).8}}; ἔκπαγλον, ἄφερτον, ἀμήχανον, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 862, 1102, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 447, etc.}}; κακὸν ἥκει τινί there's **trouble** in store for some one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 552}}; δυοῖν ἀποκρίνας κακοῖν the least of two **evils**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 640, compare “OC” 496}}; κακῶν Ἰλιάς, see at {Ἰλιά; κακόν τι ῥέξαι τινά} to do **harm** or **ill** to any one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.195, etc.; πολλὰ κάκ᾽ ἀνθρώποισιν ἐώργει 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.289}}; κακὰ φέρειν, τεύχειν τινί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.304, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 265}}; κακόν τι (or κακὰ) ποιεῖν τινα (see. δράω, ποιέω, ἐργάζομαι); κακὸν πάσχειν ὑπό τινος to suffer **evil** from one, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, etc.: in Trag. frequently repeated, κακὰ κακῶν, ={τὰ κάκιστα}, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1238 (Lyric poetry); εἴ τι πρεσβύτερον ἔτι κακοῦ κακόν [prev. author] “OT” 1365 (Lyric poetry); δεινὰ πρὸς κακοῖς κακά [prev. author] “OC” 595, compare “Ant.” 1281; δόσιν κακὰν κακῶν κακοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1041 (Lyric poetry).}}]
**B.2** κακά, τά, **evil words, reproaches**, πολλά τε καὶ κακὰ λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.61, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 571, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1244, “Ph.” 382, etc.}}
**B.3** Philos., κακόν, τό, **Evil,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Stoic. 3.18, +others, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.8.1, +others}}
**B.4** of a person, **pest, nuisance**, τουτὶ παρέξει τὸ κ. ἡμῖν πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 931}}; also, comically, ὅσον συνείλεκται κακὸν ὀρνέων what a **devil** of a lot of birds, {{Refs|[prev. work] 294.}}
**C** degrees of Comparison:
**C.1** regular _comparative_ in _Epic dialect_, κακώτερος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.275, 15.343, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 27.22, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.421, etc.}}: also in late Prose, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.62}}: irregular κακίων, ον [with ῐ], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.277, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 262, etc.}}, with ῑ in Trag., except {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 546 (_anapaest meter_); κακῑότερος “Anthologia Graeca” 12.7 (from Strato).}}
**C.2** _superlative_ κάκιστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.--Cf. also Χείρων, Χείριστος, and ἥσσων, ἥκιστος.}}
**D** _adverb_ κακῶς **ill**, ἢ εὖ ἦε κακῶς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.253, etc.}}; κακῶς ποιεῖν τινα to treat one **ill**; κακῶς ποιεῖν τι to hurt, damage a thing; κακῶς ποιεῖν τινά τι to do one any evil or harm; κ. πράσσειν to fare **ill**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 266, etc.; κάκιον ἢ πρότερον πράττειν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.11; κ. ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 58, etc.}}; of illness, {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.Matt.4.24; rarely κακῶς πάσχειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 759, 1041 (_anapaest meter_)}}; Χρῆν Κανδαύλῃ γενέσθαι κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8; κ. ὄλοισθε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1035, etc.}}; with play on two senses, ὡς κ. ἔχει ἅπας ἰατρός, ἂν κ. μηδεὶς ἔχῃ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Philemo Junior Comicus 2}}; κ. ἐρεῖν τινά, λέγειν τὴν πόλιν, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 7.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 503}}; κ. εἰδότες, ={ἀγνοοῦντες}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.32, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 33}}; κακῶς ἐκπέφευγα I have **barely** escaped, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.126}}: _comparative_ κάκιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 428, And.[same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 236a, etc.}}: _superlative_ κάκιστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1456, “Pax” 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420b, etc.}}
**D.2** _adverb_ and _adjective_ frequently coupled in Trag., _Attic dialect_, etc., κακὸν κακῶς νιν.. ἐκτρῖψαι βίον {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 248; κακὸς κακῶς ταφήσῃ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 446 (troch.); ἀπό σ᾽ ὀλῶ κακὸν κακῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 65, compare “Eq.” 189, 190, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.6, 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Persico” 1.24; κακοὺς κακῶς ἀπολέσει αὐτούς NT.Matt.21.41; κακοὺς κάκιστα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 839}}; in reversed order, ὥσπερ ἀξία κακῶς κακὴ θανεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1055}}; with intervening words, κακῶς.. ἀπόλλυσθαι κακούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1369, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 268, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 2}}. (Perh. _cognate_ with Avest. **kasu-**, _comparative_ **kasyah-**, _superlative_ **kasišta-** 'small', Lithuanian _nukašëti_ 'grow feeble, thin', [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|\{1st c.BC-1st c.AD\}: Germanicus Caesar Epigrammaticus}}] **hager.**)

2557:
** κᾰκουργ-ος**,
_Epic dialect_ **κακοεργός** (also late Prose, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.38}}; δαιμόνια κακοεργά {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Aeneas Gazaeus Rhetor “Theophrastus” p.60B.)}}, ον, (ἔργον) **doing ill, mischievous, knavish**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἀλλά με γαστὴρ ὀτρύνει κακοεργός}} **importunate**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.54; frequently later, κλῶπες κακοῦργοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.41; κ. ἀνήρ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1043; also κακουργότατος λόγος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.125; κ. μάχαιρα “Anthologia Graeca” 11.136 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus); -ότατα εἰπεῖν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.2}}. _adverb_ **-γως**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.132.}}
**2** as _substantive_, **malefactor, criminal** in the eye of the law, {{NT+5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 133, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.134, “PLille” 1.7.20 (3rd c.BC), NT.Luke.23.32, etc.; οὐδεὶς κακοεργός 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.47}}; at Athens, technically, **thief, robber**, ὁ τῶν κακούργων νόμος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.9, compare 16, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.78, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.28, 24.102.}}
**II** with _genitive_, **doing harm to**, κ. μὲν τῶν ἄλλων, ἑαυτοῦ δὲ πολὺ -ότερος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 421b}}: _absolutely_, **harmful**, κ. ἐπιθυμίαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 554c; καρτερία [prev. author] “La.” 192d; ἄγνοια -οτάτη καὶ αἰσχίστη [prev. author] Alcaeus1.118a.}}

2559:
** κᾰκόω**,
**maltreat, distress**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always of persons, κεκακωμένοι ἐν Πύλῳ ἦμεν, ἐλθὼν γάρ ῥ᾽ ἐκάκωσε βίη Ἡρακληείη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.689}}; μηδὲ.. κάκου κεκακωμένον **afflict** not **the afflicted**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.754; ἠμὲν κυδῆναι.. βροτὸν ἠδὲ κακῶσαι 16.212, compare 20.99; ὅσοι παθόντες εὖ κακοῦσί μ᾽ ἐκδίκως 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 976}}; κ. [θεὸς] δῶμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 156; κ. τοὺς ἀναιτίους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1162; τοὺς Ἀθηναίους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.32; τὸν δῆμον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.91; ἑαυτούς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 248c}}:—in _passive_, **to be in ill plight, be distressed**, κεκακωμένος ἅλμῃ **befouled** with brine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.137 (see. above): generally, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.170, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 728 (troch.), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 261, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.16, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.25; πρὸς θεῶν κακοῦται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 268; ἐκάκωτο ὑπὸ τῆς πορείας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.35; ἐκ πυρετοῖο “Anthologia Graeca” 11.382.1 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** of things, **spoil, ruin**, τὰ κοινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.82; τὸ ναυτικόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.78}}; of the air, **injure** a plant, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.11.2; τὰ κακούμενα τῆς Χώρας 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 15.1}}: Astrology texts, **render unpropitious**, Vett. Val.{{Refs|70.22 (_passive_): physically}}**injure, paralyse**, τὰς ἀρχὰς τῶν νεύρων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.690:}} —_passive_, κακοῦται πᾶν τὸ σκέλος **deteriorates**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 58.}}

2560:
Included with: ** κᾰκός**, ή, όν,
**A** **bad**:
**A.I** of persons,
**A.I.1** of appearance, **ugly**, εἶδος μὲν ἔην κακός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.316, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.49.3.}}
**A.I.2** of birth, **ill-born, mean**, γένος ἐστὲ διοτρεφέων βασιλήων.., ἐπεὶ οὔ κε κακοὶ τοιούσδε τέκοιεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.64; Ζεὺς δ᾽ αὐτὸς νέμει ὄλβον.. ἐσθλοῖς ἠδὲ κακοῖσι 6.189; οὐ κακὸν οὐδὲ μὲν ἐσθλόν 22.415; οὐδ᾽ ἐὰν.. φανῶ τρίδουλος, ἐκφανῇ κακή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1063; κακός τ᾽ ὢν κἀκ κακῶν [prev. work] 1397.}}
**A.I.3** of courage, **craven, base**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.365, 6.489; κακοῦ τρέπεται Χρὼς ἄλλυδις ἄλλῃ (called δειλὸς ἀνήρ in the line above) 13.279}}; Ἕκτωρ σε κ. καὶ ἀνάλκιδα φήσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.153, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.375; κ. καὶ ἀνήνορα 10.301; οἵτινες.. ἐγένοντο ἄνδρες κ. ἢ ἀγαθοὶ ἐν τῇ ναυμαχίῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.14; κ. καὶ ἄθυμος [prev. author] 7.11}}; οὐδαμῶν κακίονες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 104; κακοὺς πρὸς αἰχμήν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1306; κακή τ᾽ ἐς ἀλκὴν καὶ σίδηρον εἰσορᾶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 264; οὐδενὶ ἐπιτρέψοντας κακῷ εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.31.}}
**A.I.4** **bad** of his kind, i. e. **worthless, sorry, unskilled**, ἡνίοχοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.487}}; [τοξότης] ἢ κ. ἢ ἀγαθός{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 632; νομῆες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.246}}; κ. ἀλήτης a **bad** beggar,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 578; ἰατρός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 473}}; κυβερνήτης, ναύτης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 880, “Andr.” 457; μάγειρος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 265e: with _accusative_ modi, πάντα γὰρ οὐ κακός εἰμι}} I am not **bad** in all things, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.214; κ. γνώμην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 910: also with _dative_, κακοὶ γνώμαισι [prev. author] “Aj.” 964}}: with _infinitive_, κ. μανθάνειν {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 545}}; [νῆσος] φυτεύεσθαι κακή {{Refs|“Tragica Adespota” 393}}; compare 11.
**A.I.5** in moral sense, **base, evil**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.384, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 240}}; opposed to Χρηστός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 520; ὦ κακῶν κάκιστε [prev. author] “OT” 334, “Ph.” 984; πλεῖστον κάκιστος [prev. author] “OC” 744; κ. πρός τινας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.86; εἰς φίλους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 424 codices; περὶ τὰ Χρήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Clitopho” 407c.}}
**A.I.6** **wretched**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.42.}}
**A.II** of things, **evil, pernicious**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc., as δαίμων, θάνατος, μοῖρα, αἶσα, κῆρες, νοῦσος, ἕλκος, φάρμακα, ὀδύναι, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.64, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.173, 13.602, 1.418, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.316, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.10, 2.723, 22.94, 5.766}}; Χόλος, ἔρις, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.206, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.161}}; πόλεμος, ἔπος, ἔργα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.82, 24.767, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.67, +others}}; ἦμαρ, ἄνεμος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.251, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.109}}; of omens and the like, **unlucky**, ὄρνις, ὄναρ, σῆμα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.219, 10.496, 22.30: also in Trag., κ. τύχη, δαίμων, μόρος, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 328, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 354, 369, etc.}}; of words, **abusive, foul**, κ. λόγοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 259, compare “Tr.” 461}}; κ. ποιμήν, i.e. the storm, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 657}}: Astrology texts, **unlucky**, τόποι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.12}}; κ. τύχη, name for the sixth region, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “M.” 1.}}
**B** κακόν, τό, and κακά, τά, as _substantive_, **evil, ill**, δίδου δ᾽ ἀγαθόν τε κακόν τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.63; ἀθάνατον κακόν 12.118; ἐκ μεγάλων κακῶν πεφευγέναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.65}}; so κ. ἄμαχον, ἄπρηκτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.76, “I.” 8(7).8}}; ἔκπαγλον, ἄφερτον, ἀμήχανον, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 862, 1102, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 447, etc.}}; κακὸν ἥκει τινί there's **trouble** in store for some one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 552}}; δυοῖν ἀποκρίνας κακοῖν the least of two **evils**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 640, compare “OC” 496}}; κακῶν Ἰλιάς, see at {Ἰλιά; κακόν τι ῥέξαι τινά} to do **harm** or **ill** to any one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.195, etc.; πολλὰ κάκ᾽ ἀνθρώποισιν ἐώργει 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.289}}; κακὰ φέρειν, τεύχειν τινί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.304, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 265}}; κακόν τι (or κακὰ) ποιεῖν τινα (see. δράω, ποιέω, ἐργάζομαι); κακὸν πάσχειν ὑπό τινος to suffer **evil** from one, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, etc.: in Trag. frequently repeated, κακὰ κακῶν, ={τὰ κάκιστα}, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1238 (Lyric poetry); εἴ τι πρεσβύτερον ἔτι κακοῦ κακόν [prev. author] “OT” 1365 (Lyric poetry); δεινὰ πρὸς κακοῖς κακά [prev. author] “OC” 595, compare “Ant.” 1281; δόσιν κακὰν κακῶν κακοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1041 (Lyric poetry).}}]
**B.2** κακά, τά, **evil words, reproaches**, πολλά τε καὶ κακὰ λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.61, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 571, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1244, “Ph.” 382, etc.}}
**B.3** Philos., κακόν, τό, **Evil,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Stoic. 3.18, +others, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.8.1, +others}}
**B.4** of a person, **pest, nuisance**, τουτὶ παρέξει τὸ κ. ἡμῖν πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 931}}; also, comically, ὅσον συνείλεκται κακὸν ὀρνέων what a **devil** of a lot of birds, {{Refs|[prev. work] 294.}}
**C** degrees of Comparison:
**C.1** regular _comparative_ in _Epic dialect_, κακώτερος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.275, 15.343, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 27.22, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.421, etc.}}: also in late Prose, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.62}}: irregular κακίων, ον [with ῐ], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.277, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 262, etc.}}, with ῑ in Trag., except {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 546 (_anapaest meter_); κακῑότερος “Anthologia Graeca” 12.7 (from Strato).}}
**C.2** _superlative_ κάκιστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.--Cf. also Χείρων, Χείριστος, and ἥσσων, ἥκιστος.}}
**D** _adverb_ κακῶς **ill**, ἢ εὖ ἦε κακῶς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.253, etc.}}; κακῶς ποιεῖν τινα to treat one **ill**; κακῶς ποιεῖν τι to hurt, damage a thing; κακῶς ποιεῖν τινά τι to do one any evil or harm; κ. πράσσειν to fare **ill**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 266, etc.; κάκιον ἢ πρότερον πράττειν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.11; κ. ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 58, etc.}}; of illness, {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.Matt.4.24; rarely κακῶς πάσχειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 759, 1041 (_anapaest meter_)}}; Χρῆν Κανδαύλῃ γενέσθαι κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8; κ. ὄλοισθε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1035, etc.}}; with play on two senses, ὡς κ. ἔχει ἅπας ἰατρός, ἂν κ. μηδεὶς ἔχῃ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Philemo Junior Comicus 2}}; κ. ἐρεῖν τινά, λέγειν τὴν πόλιν, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 7.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 503}}; κ. εἰδότες, ={ἀγνοοῦντες}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.32, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 33}}; κακῶς ἐκπέφευγα I have **barely** escaped, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.126}}: _comparative_ κάκιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 428, And.[same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 236a, etc.}}: _superlative_ κάκιστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1456, “Pax” 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420b, etc.}}
**D.2** _adverb_ and _adjective_ frequently coupled in Trag., _Attic dialect_, etc., κακὸν κακῶς νιν.. ἐκτρῖψαι βίον {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 248; κακὸς κακῶς ταφήσῃ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 446 (troch.); ἀπό σ᾽ ὀλῶ κακὸν κακῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 65, compare “Eq.” 189, 190, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.6, 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Persico” 1.24; κακοὺς κακῶς ἀπολέσει αὐτούς NT.Matt.21.41; κακοὺς κάκιστα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 839}}; in reversed order, ὥσπερ ἀξία κακῶς κακὴ θανεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1055}}; with intervening words, κακῶς.. ἀπόλλυσθαι κακούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1369, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 268, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 2}}. (Perh. _cognate_ with Avest. **kasu-**, _comparative_ **kasyah-**, _superlative_ **kasišta-** 'small', Lithuanian _nukašëti_ 'grow feeble, thin', [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|\{1st c.BC-1st c.AD\}: Germanicus Caesar Epigrammaticus}}] **hager.**)

2561:
** κάκ-ωσις** [ᾰ], εως, ἡ,
(κᾰκόω) **ill-treatment**, τοῦ ἡγεμόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.3, compare “Stud.Pal.” 1.8.10 (V 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus)}}; **oppression**, τοῦ λαοῦ {{LXX|LXX.Exo.3.7.}}
**2** especially in Law, **ill-usage**, of persons by their natural protectors, ὁ τῆς κ. νόμος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.91, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.32, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.40, etc.; γραφὴ κακώσεως [prev. author] 58.32, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 328}}; κ. γονέων, ὀρφανῶν, ἐπικλήρου, οἴκου ὀρφανικοῦ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 56.6}}; τοκέων κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 147}}; also κ. ἐπαρχίας **misgovernment**, of the Rom. **actio repetundarum**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 4.}}
**II** **suffering, distress**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.43; πληρωμάτων [prev. author] 7.4; αἰκίαι σωμάτων καὶ κακώσεις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1386a8, compare 1385a24}}; of the effects of disease, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 17: _plural_, [prev. author] “Aër.” 19; αἱ τᾶς σαρκὸς τακομένας κακώσιες 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 102c, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 21, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.31.}}

2562:
** κᾰλᾰμ-η**, ἡ,
(see. κάλαμος) **stalk**, especially **the stalk** or **straw of corn**, _metaphorically_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, αἶψά τε φυλόπιδος πέλεται κόρος ἀνθρώποισιν, ἧς τε πλείστην μὲν καλάμην Χθονὶ Χαλκὸς ἔχευεν, ἄμητος δ᾽ ὀλίγιστος}}, i.e. when there is much **straw** and little harvest, much **slaughter** and little profit, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.222}}; κ. πυρῶν wheat-**straw**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.33; σῖτος σὺν τῇ καλάμῃ ἀποκείμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.27; καλάμαν τε καὶ ἱερὰ δράγματα.. ἀσταχύων 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 20}}; proverbial of a greedy farmer, πυροὺς ἐπὶ καλάμῃ ἀροῦν to exhaust ground by one corn-crop after another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 77: _plural_, σῖτος ἐπὶ ταῖς κ. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.13.}}
**2** **stubble**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 341b27, “PSI” 4.380.6 (3rd c.BC), NT.1Cor.3.12, etc.: _metaphorically_, of an old man, καλάμην γέ σ᾽ ὀΐομαι εἰσορόωντα γιγνώσκειν}} thou mayst still, I ween, perceive **the stubble** (i.e. **the residue**) of former strength, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.214; τὸ γῆρας καλάμη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1410b14; τὴν κ. δωρῇ, δοὺς ἑτέροις τὸ θέρος “Anthologia Graeca” 11.36 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}; Ῥήσου κ. **the remains** of Rhesus, i.e. his **corpse**, Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 6.53}}; ἀπὸ τῆς κ. τεκμαίρεσθαι to judge from **the remains**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 5.}}
**3** κολχὶς κ., ={λίνον}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 265.}}
**II** ={κάλαμος}, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 8.9.}}

2563:
** κάλᾰμος** [κᾰ], ὁ,
**reed**, used for thatching or wattling, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.101, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76; for wreaths, κ. λευκός 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1006 (_anapaest meter_); for bedding, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 16; for fuel, “PCair.Zen.” 85 (3rd c.BC)}}; various species, κ. εὐώδης, ἀρωματικός, **sweet flag, Acorus Calamus**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.8.4, 9.7.1, “Od.” 33, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.18}}; κ. αὐλητικός **pole-reed, Arundo Donax**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.11.1, 9}}; κ. εἰλετίας **marram, Ammophila arundinacea**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 13}}; κ. ἐπίγειος **bush-grass, Calamagrostis epigeios**, {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; κ. Ἰνδικός **bamboo, Bambusa arundinacea**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. passage], 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.92, “PLond.” 2.191.11 (2nd c.AD), “Geoponica” 2.6.23, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.98}}; κ. Ἰνδικὸς ὁ ἄρρην **male bamboo, Dendrocalamus strictus**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.11.13}}; κ. κύπριος, ={δόναξ}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.414}}; κ. πλόκιμος **spear-grass, Phragmites communis**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.11.1}}; κ. Χαρακίας, **Arundo Donax**, {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}
**II** applied to various uses,
**II.1** **reed-pipe, flute**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).84, “N.” 5.38, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 702 (Lyric poetry), “IT” 1126 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** **fishing-rod**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 11, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.43, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 27.9; κ. ἁλιευτικός 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 693a23.}}
**II.3** **limed** **twig** used by fowlers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 10.5, 5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 171, 296.}}
**II.4** **shaft** of an arrow, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 7.5}}; made of κ. τοξικός, or Κρητικός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.11.11.}}
**II.5** **reed-pen**, {{LXX+NT+1st c.BC+|LXX.Psa.44(45).1, NT.3John.13, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 29, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 38; κάλαμοι γραφικοί “PGrenf.” 2.38.7 (1st c.BC)}}; κ. γραφεῖς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 10.61.}}
**II.6** **measuring-rod,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Rev. 11.1, +others: hence, a definite measure, “IG” 9(1).61.50 (Daulis, 2nd c.AD)}}; = 5 πήχεις, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus}} “*Geometry texts” 4.11; = 6 2/3 πήχεις, {{Refs|[prev. work]23.13.}}
**II.7** in Medicine texts, **tube** for insufflation, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.9, 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.985; for fumigation, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 1.56; for extraction, 1st c.AD: Celsus Medicus 7.5.2}}; also, **splint**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus}}**in Hp.Fract.**{{Refs|12.282 C.}}
**II.8** ornament of female dress, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.292 (3rd c.BC: Hedylus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.9** **stake** to which vines were tied, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 369.4 (2nd c.AD), 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 3.125b, etc.}}
**III** collectively,
**III.1** **reed**, i.e. **reeds**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 359b1, “POxy.” 742.2 (1st c.BC)}}, etc.: in _plural_, **reed-beds**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.71.4.}}
**III.2** of plants, which are neither shrub nor bush (ὕλη), nor tree (δένδρον), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.1.}}
**III.3** **mat of reeds**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372b}}; **roof of reeds** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|(from Coan), 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**IV** ={καλάμη}, **stalk** of wheat, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 18.2.}}
**V** ὁ κ. τοῦ σκέλους the **shinbone**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Scholia 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.23.}}
**VI** **ticket** for obtaining corn-rations, = **tessera frumentaria,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}} (Cf. Latin _culmus_, OHG. **halm**, etc.)

2564:
** κᾰλέω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **κάλημι** (which see), _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ καλήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.125}}: _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ καλέεσκον {{Refs|6.402}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ κάλεσκε {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1514}}: _future_, _Ionic dialect_ καλέω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.383}}, _Attic dialect_ καλῶ {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.15, etc.; later καλέσω LXX.Gen.16.11, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.69, (παρα-) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.14 codices, “SIG” 656.40 (Teos, 2nd c.BC), (ἐγ-) variant in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.133, compare 23.123 codices (καλέσω in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1452 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 964, etc., is}}_aorist 1_ _subjunctive_): _aorist 1_ ἐκάλεσα, _Epic dialect_ ἐκάλεσσα, κάλεσσα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.379, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.693}} (late _Epic dialect_ ἔκλησα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 86, late Prose ἐκάλησα Ps.Callisth. 3.35)}}: _perfect_ κέκληκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 260, etc.}}:—_middle_, _Attic dialect_ _future_ καλοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1221, “Ec.” 864; in _passive_ sense, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 971, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1140, etc.}}; later καλέσομαι (ἐκ-, ἐπι-) uncertain reading in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.174, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 17}}: _aorist 1_ ἐκαλεσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.189, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 937a}}; _Epic dialect_ καλεσσάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.54, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ καλέσαντο [prev. work] 270}}:—_passive_, _future_ κεκλήσομαι {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.138, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 698 (Lyric poetry), “Pr.” 840, etc.; κληθήσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681d, LXX.Gen.48.6, variant in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 13}}: _aorist_ ἐκλήθην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 78, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1359, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 862, etc.:}}_perfect_ κέκλημαι, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ κεκλήαται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1128}}, _Ionic dialect_ κεκλέαται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.164}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _pluperfect_ κεκλήατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.195; _optative_ κεκλῄμην, κεκλῇο 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 119, κεκλῄμεθα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 253: late}}_perfect_ κεκάλεσμαι Suid. see at {κλητή}.
**I** **call, summon**, εἰς ἀγορὴν καλέσαντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.90; ἐς Ὄλυμπον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.402}}; ἀγορήνδε, θάλαμόνδε, θάνατόνδε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.4, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.348, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.693: with _accusative_ only, κεκλήατο (for -ηντο) βουλήν}} **they had been summoned** to the council, {{Refs|10.195: followed by}}_infinitive_, αὐτοὶ γὰρ κάλεον συμμητιάασθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 197; καιρὸς καλεῖ.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 466; κἄμ᾽ ὑπηρετεῖν καλεῖς [prev. author] “El.” 996}}; κ. τινὰ εἰς ἕ, ἐπὶ οἷ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.203, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.330, etc.; εἰς μαρτυρίαν κληθείς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 937a; ἐμὲ νῦν ἤδη καλεῖ ἡ εἱμαρμένη [prev. author] “Phd.” 115a}}; **demand, require**, ἡ ἡμέρα 'κείνη εὔνουν καὶ πλούσιον ἄνδρα ἐκάλει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.172:}}_aorist_ _middle_, καλέσασθαί τινα **call to oneself**, frequently in _Epic dialect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.270, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.43, etc.; φωνῇ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.161; ἀγορήνδε λαόν 1.54}}; **call** a witness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” [prev. cited]}}
**I.2** **call to one's house** or **to a repast, invite** (not in {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.231, 17.382, +others, NT.1Cor.10.27}}; later usually with a word added, κ. ἐπὶ δεῖπνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.16 (_passive_), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.30, etc.; ἐς ἔρανον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.37; ἐς θοίνην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1140; ὑπὸ σοῦ κεκλημένος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174d, etc.}}; κληθέντες πρός τινα **invited** to his house, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.196}}; ὁ κεκλημένος the **guest**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 2.26.}}
**I.3** **invoke**, Δία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.44, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.58, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 223; at sacrifices, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 482; μάρτυρας κ. θεούς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.141}}:—_middle_, τοὺς θεοὺς καλούμεθα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 201, compare 216; also μαρτύριά τε καὶ τεκμήρια καλεῖσθε [prev. author] “Eu.” 486}}; but ἀράς, ἅς σοι καλοῦμαι which I **call down** on thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1385}}:—_passive_, of the god, **to be invoked**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 417.}}
**I.4** as law-term, **summon**, of the judge, καλεῖν τινας εἰς τὸ δικαστήριον **cite** or **summon** before the court, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.211, etc.}}; simply καλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]212, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 851, etc.; ἐὰν μὲν καλέσῃ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.56}}; also ὁ ἄρχων τὴν δίκην καλεῖ **calls on** the case, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1441}}:—_passive_, ἡ πατροκτόνος δίκη κεκλῇτ᾽ ἂν αὐτῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 696}}; πρὶν τὴν ἐμὴν [δίκην] καλεῖσθαι before it **is called on**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 780; καλουμένης τῆς γραφῆς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.43}}; but,
**I.4.b** of the plaintiff in _middle_, καλεῖσθαί τινα **to sue** at law, **bring before the court**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1221, +others, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.63; κ. τινὰ ὕβρεως 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1046; κ. τινὰ πρὸς τὴν ἀρχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 914c}}; ὁ καλεσάμενος the **plaintiff,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PHal. 1.224 (3rd c.BC).}}
**I.5** with an abstract subject, **demand, require**, καλεῖ ἡ τάξις with _infinitive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“CPHerm.” 25ii7 (3rd c.AD).}}
**I.6** _metaphorically_ in _passive_, καλουμένης τῆς δυνάμεως πρὸς τὴν συναναληψίαν **called forth, summoned**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.29.}}
**II** **call by name, name**, ὃν Βριάρεων καλέουσι θεοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.403, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.273, etc.; κοτύλην δέ τέ μιν καλέουσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.306; ὥς σφας καλοῦμεν Εὐμενίδας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 486, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 86, etc.}}; ὄνομα καλεῖν τινα **call** him by a name, εἴπ᾽ ὄνομ᾽ ὅττι σε κεῖθι κάλεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.550, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 259, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 383b, etc.}} (in _passive_, οὔνομα καλέεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.173, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.56): without ὄνομα, τί νιν καλοῦσα τύχοιμ᾽ ἄ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1232; τοῦτο αὐτὴν κάλεον 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 66b; τούτοις τὸ ὄνομα ἱμάτια ἐκαλέσαμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 279e (_passive_, τύμβῳ δ᾽ ὄνομα σῷ κεκλήσεται}} **shall be given** to thy tomb, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1271)}}; κ. ὄνομα ἐπί τινι **give** a name **to** something, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 147d}}; but **call** (a man) a name **because of** some function, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Sph.” 218c; κ. τινὰ ἐπὶ τῷ ὀνόματι τοῦ πατρός NT.Luke.1.59; ἐπ᾽ ὀνόματος καλεῖν τινα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 35.4.11}}:—_passive_, **to be named** or **called**, Μυρμιδόνες δὲ καλεῦντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.684; εἰ τόδ᾽ αὐτῷ φίλον κεκλημένῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 161 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ καλούμενος the **socalled**, ἐν τῇ Θεράπνῃ καλεομένῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.61; ὁ κ. θάνατος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 86d}}; οἱ τῶν ὁμοτίμων κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.9}}; κεκλημένος τινός **called from** or **after** him, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.67; καλεῖσθαι ἐπί τινι LXX.Gen.48.6; κέκληνται δέ σφιν ἕδραι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.76.}}
**II.2** _passive_, **to be called**, almost ={εἰμί}, especially with words expressing kinship or status, ἐμὸς γαμβρὸς καλέεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.313, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 2 (_anapaest meter_); ἀφνειοὶ καλέονται 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.433}}; especially in _perfect_ _passive_ κέκλημαι, οὕνεκα σὴ παράκοιτις κέκλημαι because I **am** thy wife, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.61; φίλη κεκλήσῃ ἄκοιτις 3.138; αἲ γὰρ ἐμοὶ τοιόσδε πόσις κεκλημένος εἴη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.244; ἠγάγετ᾽ ἐς μέγα δῶμα φίλην κεκλῆσθαι ἄκοιτιν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 410; σὴ κεκλημένη.. ἦα “hymnus ad Apollinem” 324; μηδ᾽ ἔτι Τηλεμάχοιο πατὴρ κεκλημένος εἴην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.260; οὔτινος δοῦλοι κέκληνται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 242, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 366, etc.}}
**II.3** special constructions, a. Ἀλησίου ἔνθα κολώνη κέκληται where **is** the hill **called** the hill of Alesios, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.758; ἵνα κριοῦ καλέονται εὐναί 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.115; ἔνθα ἡ Τριπυργία καλεῖται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.10, etc.: -so in}}_active_, ἔνθα Ῥέας πόρον ἄνθρωποι καλέοισιν where **is** the ford men **call** the ford of Rhea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.41, compare κικλήσκω, κλῄζω, κλέω.}}
**II.3.b** folld by a dependent clause, ἐκάλεσσέ νιν ἰσώνυμον ἔμμεν **said that** his **name** should be the same, {{Refs|[prev. author] “O.” 9.63}}; καλεῖ με πλαστὸς ὡς εἴην πατρί, i.e. καλεῖ με πλαστόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 780}}; καλοῦμέν γε παραδιδόντα μὲν διδάσκειν **we say** that one who delivers teaches, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 198b, compare “Smp.” 205d; τὰς ἀμπέλους τραγᾶν καλοῦσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 546a3.}}

2566:
** καλός**, ή, όν,
_Aeolic dialect_ **κάλος** (see. below), α, ον, _Boeotian dialect_ **καλϝός** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 538 (6th c.BC)}}:—
**A** **beautiful**, of outward form, frequently of persons, κάλλιστος ἀνὴρ ὑπὸ Ἴλιον ἦλθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.673: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} usually in the phrase κ. τε μέγας τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.108, +others}}; μέγας καὶ κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.513; καλή τε μεγάλη τε 13.289, 15.418}}; καλὸς δέμας **beautiful** of form,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.307; κ. ἰδέᾳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10 (11). 103; εἶδος κάλλιστος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.1; κ. τὸ σῶμα [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.6.30; τὰς ὄψεις 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 195}}; Χορῷ καλή **beauteous** in the dance, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.180}}: with _infinitive_, καλλίονες καὶ μείζονες εἰσοράασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.396}}; ἐσορᾶν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.19}}: frequently of parts of the body, **fair, shapely**, κ. πρόσωπα, ὅμματα, παρήϊα, σφυρά, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.285, 23.66, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.208, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.147; Χρώς 5.354, +others}}; of clothes, εἵματα, φάρεα, Χιτών, Χλαῖνα, πέδιλα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.111, 24.277, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.43, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.365, 1.96; πέπλος κάλλιστος ποικίλμασιν ἠδὲ μέγιστος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.294}}; of arms and armour, κνημῖδες, ἀσπίς, σάκος, κόρυς, φάσγανα, ἔντεα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.331, 11.33, 22.314, 18.612, 15.713, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.18}}; of buildings, manufactured articles, etc., αὐλὴ κ. τε μεγάλη τε {{Refs|14.7}}; κ. δώματα, τεῖχος, πόλιες,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.387, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.447, 18.491; ἄμαξα, τράπεζα, θρόνος, 24.267, 11.629, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.131}}; also τέμενος, ἀγρός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.314, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.206; so after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, Λύδιον κ. ἔργον 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 19, etc.}}; ἐέρσα κ. ead.{{Refs|“Supp.” 25.12.}}
**A.2** in _Attic dialect_ added to a name in token of love or admiration, as Ἀρίσημος κ. {{Refs|“IG” 12.921, etc.}}; ἐν τοῖσι τοίχοις ἔγραφ᾽ Ἀθηναῖοι καλοί” {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 144, compare “V.” 98}}; Ἀλκιβιάδης ὁ καλός, Σαπφὼ ἡ καλή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.113b, “Phdr.” 235c.}}
**A.2.b** ἡ Καλή or Καλλίστη, as epithet, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 140 (Lyric poetry), 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.29.2, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4445 (from Beroea).}}
**A.2.c** Καλοί, οἱ, divinities worshipped in childbirth, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1445 (Messene, 2nd c.BC).}}
**A.3** τὸ καλόν **beauty**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 79, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 21 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.; τὰ καλά **the proprieties** or **elegancies** of life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, 207; ἁπάντων καλῶν ἄμμορος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.84; αἱ τέχναι ἃς πηγάς φασι τῶν κ. εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.13.}}
**A.II** with _reference_ to use, **good, of fine quality**, κ. λιμήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.263}}; Βορέῃ ἀνέμῳ.. καλῷ **fair**, {{Refs|14.253, 299}}; κ. ἀργύριον, opposed to κίβδηλον, **genuine** silver, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.1.9}}; opposed to ἀποτετριμμένον, **good** silver currency, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 21.33 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐλαῖαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.49.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; γῆ {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Luke.8.15; κ. οἶνος “PFay.” 133.8 (4th c.AD)}}; στρατόπεδον κάλλιστον {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.60; ἀνταπεδώκατε πονηρὰ ἀντὶ καλῶν LXX.Gen.44.4; κ. ἐς στρατιάν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.6; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 295c, “Grg.” 474d, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, λόφος κάλλιστος τρέχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.26}}; ἐν καλῷ [τόπῳ] in a **good** place, καθίζεσθαι, ὁρμεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 292, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.25; ἐν καλῷ μὲν τοῦ κόλπου καὶ τῶν πόλεων, ἐν κ. δὲ τοῦ τὴν Χώραν βλάπτειν, [prev. work] 6.2.9}}; ἐν καλῷ under **favourable circumstances**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.59.60}}; ἐν κ. (i.e. Χρόνῳ) in **good time**, in **season**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1106}}; ἐν οὐ κ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 579}}; ἐν καλῷ [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 384 (so καλόν ἐστι}} with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1155 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 278, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.2); ἐς καλόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 78, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89e, “Smp.” 174e}}; τί γὰρ ἐμοὶ ζῆν καλό; what is the **good** of life to me? {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.594}}; καλῇ πίστει, = Latin _bona fide,_ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PTeb. 418.14 (3rd c.AD).}}
**A.II.2** of sacrifices, **auspicious**, σφάγια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 379; οἰωνοί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1333; ἱερά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.92; τὸ τέλος κ. τῆς ἐξόδου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.9; κ. τὰ ἱερὰ ἦν αὐτῷ [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.2.3}}: with _infinitive_, ἰέναι.. κ. ἡμῖν τὰ ἱερὰ ἦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 2.2.3: Comedy texts, τὰ τῆς πυγῆς κ. (for τοῦ θεοῦ) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 868.}}
**A.III** in a moral sense, **beautiful, noble, honourable**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _neuter_, οὐ καλὸν ἔειπες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.166, compare 17.381; μεῖζον κλέος.. καὶ κάλλιον 18.255}}; frequently καλόν [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, κ. τοι σὺν ἐμοὶ τὸν κήδειν ὅς κ᾽ ἐμὲ κήδῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.615}}; οὐ γὰρ ἔμοιγε κ. (i.e. ἄρχειν){{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.440; οὐ κ. ἀτέμβειν οὐδὲ δίκαιον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.294}}; so in Trag., καλόν μοι τοῦτο ποιούσῃ θανεῖν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 72, etc.; μάθετε καλὸν ποιεῖν LXX.Isa.1.17}}: _comparative_, οὐ μέν τοι τόδε κάλλιον οὐδὲ ἔοικε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.159, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.52; after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus frequently of actions, etc., κάλων κἄσλων 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 2.4 (unless of persons here)}}; κ. ἔργματα **noble** deeds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).42, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 839, etc.}}; ἀναστροφὴ κ. {{NT|NT.1Pet.2.12}}: in _plural_, **excellences**, πλῆθος καλῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.45; πολλῶν καλῶν δεῖ τῷ καλόν τι μωμένῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 938}}; τὰ τοῦ παιδὸς κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.17.}}
**A.III.2** τὸ κ. **moral beauty, virtue, honour**, opposed to τὸ αἰσχρόν, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.1.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 183d, etc.; ὅττι καλόν, φίλον ἐστί, τὸ δ᾽ οὐ καλὸν οὐ φίλον ἐστίν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 17, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 881 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 216c; οὐ ταὐτὸν ἡγῇ σύ, ὡς ἔοικας, κ. τε καὶ ἀγαθὸν καὶ κακὸν καὶ αἰσχρόν [prev. author] “Grg.” 474d, compare “Smp.” 201e}}; τοὐμὸν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 300.}}
**A.III.3** of persons, in early writers coupled with ἀγαθός, see at {καλοκἀγαθός}; later κ. ποιμήν {{NT|NT.John.10.11}}; κ. στρατιώτης {{NT|NT.2Tim.2.3.}}
**A.IV** in _Attic dialect_ and Trag. frequently ironically, **fine, specious**, γέρας κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 209; κ. γὰρ οὑμὸς βίοτος ὥστε θαυμάσαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 393, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 652; κ. Χάρις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.65; κ. ὕβριν ὑβρισμένοι [prev. author] 23.121; καί σοι.. θωπεῦσαι καλόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1003; μετ᾽ ὀνομάτων καλῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.89.}}
**B** Degrees of _comparative_: _comparative_ καλλίων, ον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.52, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.396, etc.: _neuter_ κάλιον [ᾰ] 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 134}}: _superlative_ κάλλιστος, η, ον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.233, etc.}}; late καλλιώτερος or -ότερος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“POxy.” 1672.6 (1st c.AD), Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 966; also καλώτερος 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus “Epim.” 69.}}
**C** _adverb_:—Poets frequently use _neuter_ καλόν as _adverb_, κ. ἀείδειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.570, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.155; καλά 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.326}}; later τὸ κ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.3, 18, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 53, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.54.}}
**C.II** regular _adverb_ **καλῶς** (_Doric dialect_ καλώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 22)}}, **well, rightly**, οὐδ᾽ ἔτι κ. οἶκος ἐμὸς διόλωλε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.64}}; κ. ζῆν, τεθνηκέναι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 479, etc.}}; κ. φρονεῖν to be in one's **right** mind, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 836; οὐ κ. ταρβεῖς [prev. author] “Tr.” 457}}; κ. ἀγωνιεῖσθαι **fairly, on the merits of the case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.88}}; Χρήματα δατῆθθαι κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 4.39; κ. εἰρημένα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 576.6; κάλλιον λέγεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161b; κάλλιστ᾽ ἂν εἴποι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1172: frequently in phrase καλῶς καὶ εὖ, καλῶς τε καὶ εὖ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319e, “Prm.” 128b, etc.}}
**C.II.2** of good fortune, **well, happily**, κ. πράσσειν, ={εὖ π}., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 979, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 271; κ. καὶ εὖ πράττειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 172a}}; κ. ἔχειν to be **well**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 799, etc.; κ. ἔχει σοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 946, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 816}}; κ. ἔχει with _infinitive_, 'tis **well** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.1: with _genitive_, κ. ἔχειν τινός}} to be **well off in** **respect to** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 29; κ. παράπλου κεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.36; εἰ κ. σφίσιν ἔχοι [prev. author] 4.117; οὔτε τοῖς θεοῖς ἔφη κ. ἔχειν, εἰ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.3; καλλιόνως ἔχει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 169e, etc.; κάλλιστα ἕζει [prev. author] “Hp.Ma.” 295b.}}
**C.II.3** καλῶς, ={πάνυ}, **thoroughly, altogether**, τὸν κ. εὐδαίμονα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 317, = 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 934; κ. ἔξοιδα [prev. author] “OC” 269, compare “OT” 1008; κ. ὑπὸ τοῦ πυρὸς διεφθάρθαι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.108}}: _comparative_, κάλλιον εἰδέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 300d}}; κάλλιον ἐοικέναι to be **just** like, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Genit.” 8.}}
**C.II.4** κ. ἀκούειν to be **well** spoken of, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 285, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.177e.}}
**C.II.5** κ. ποιῶν **rightly, deservedly**, κ. ποιῶν ἀπόλλυται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 863, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.28, +others, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.232}}; in requests, κ. ποιήσεις πριάμενος, etc., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.143 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; also with _infinitive_, κ. π. γράψαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1203.7 (1st c.BC), etc.}}
**C.II.6** in answers, to approve the words of the former speaker, **well said**! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1216, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.15}}; also, to decline an offer courteously, **no, thank you**! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 888; κ. ἔχει 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 165, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 266}}; πάνυ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 512; ἀμέλει κ. [prev. work] 532: _superlative_, κάλλιστ᾽, ἐπαινῶ [prev. work] 508; ἔχει κάλλιστα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.3.}}
**C.II.7** ironically, **finely**, καλῶς ἐρήμης γ᾽ ἂν σὺ γῆς ἄρχοις μόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 739, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 588, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 344, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.69.}}
**C.II.8** κ. ὁ ἱερεύς **hurrah for** the priest! {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“SIG” 1109.14 (Athens, 2nd c.AD).}}
**C.II.9** repeated with the _adjective_, καλὴ καλῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 253, “Pax” 1330, “Ec.” 730; καλὸς κάλλιστά τε ῥέξαις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.94.}}
**C.II.10** _comparative_ καλλιόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Tht.” [same place], “Lg.” 660d}}: _superlative_ καλλίστως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PMag.Par.” 1.2443,2465, Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 310.}}
**D** for compounds, see at {καλλι-, καλο-}.
**E** Quantity: ᾱ in _Epic dialect_ and early {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus Poets (except “hymnus ad Venerem” 29, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 63, “Th.” 585)}}: ᾰ in Lyric poetry (except κᾱλῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.206) and Trag. (4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 314, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1381 are corrupt).--In Eleg., epigram, and Bucol. Poets ᾰ or ᾱ (the latter usually in thesi)}}; τὰ μὴ κᾰλὰ κᾱλὰ πέφανται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.19, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.115, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 55}}.--In _comparative_, ῐ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ῑ in Trag. and later.}}

2570:
** καλός**, ή, όν,
_Aeolic dialect_ **κάλος** (see. below), α, ον, _Boeotian dialect_ **καλϝός** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 538 (6th c.BC)}}:—
**A** **beautiful**, of outward form, frequently of persons, κάλλιστος ἀνὴρ ὑπὸ Ἴλιον ἦλθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.673: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} usually in the phrase κ. τε μέγας τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.108, +others}}; μέγας καὶ κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.513; καλή τε μεγάλη τε 13.289, 15.418}}; καλὸς δέμας **beautiful** of form,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.307; κ. ἰδέᾳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10 (11). 103; εἶδος κάλλιστος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.1; κ. τὸ σῶμα [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.6.30; τὰς ὄψεις 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 195}}; Χορῷ καλή **beauteous** in the dance, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.180}}: with _infinitive_, καλλίονες καὶ μείζονες εἰσοράασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.396}}; ἐσορᾶν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.19}}: frequently of parts of the body, **fair, shapely**, κ. πρόσωπα, ὅμματα, παρήϊα, σφυρά, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.285, 23.66, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.208, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.147; Χρώς 5.354, +others}}; of clothes, εἵματα, φάρεα, Χιτών, Χλαῖνα, πέδιλα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.111, 24.277, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.43, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.365, 1.96; πέπλος κάλλιστος ποικίλμασιν ἠδὲ μέγιστος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.294}}; of arms and armour, κνημῖδες, ἀσπίς, σάκος, κόρυς, φάσγανα, ἔντεα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.331, 11.33, 22.314, 18.612, 15.713, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.18}}; of buildings, manufactured articles, etc., αὐλὴ κ. τε μεγάλη τε {{Refs|14.7}}; κ. δώματα, τεῖχος, πόλιες,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.387, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.447, 18.491; ἄμαξα, τράπεζα, θρόνος, 24.267, 11.629, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.131}}; also τέμενος, ἀγρός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.314, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.206; so after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, Λύδιον κ. ἔργον 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 19, etc.}}; ἐέρσα κ. ead.{{Refs|“Supp.” 25.12.}}
**A.2** in _Attic dialect_ added to a name in token of love or admiration, as Ἀρίσημος κ. {{Refs|“IG” 12.921, etc.}}; ἐν τοῖσι τοίχοις ἔγραφ᾽ Ἀθηναῖοι καλοί” {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 144, compare “V.” 98}}; Ἀλκιβιάδης ὁ καλός, Σαπφὼ ἡ καλή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.113b, “Phdr.” 235c.}}
**A.2.b** ἡ Καλή or Καλλίστη, as epithet, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 140 (Lyric poetry), 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.29.2, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4445 (from Beroea).}}
**A.2.c** Καλοί, οἱ, divinities worshipped in childbirth, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1445 (Messene, 2nd c.BC).}}
**A.3** τὸ καλόν **beauty**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 79, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 21 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.; τὰ καλά **the proprieties** or **elegancies** of life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, 207; ἁπάντων καλῶν ἄμμορος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.84; αἱ τέχναι ἃς πηγάς φασι τῶν κ. εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.13.}}
**A.II** with _reference_ to use, **good, of fine quality**, κ. λιμήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.263}}; Βορέῃ ἀνέμῳ.. καλῷ **fair**, {{Refs|14.253, 299}}; κ. ἀργύριον, opposed to κίβδηλον, **genuine** silver, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.1.9}}; opposed to ἀποτετριμμένον, **good** silver currency, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 21.33 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐλαῖαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.49.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; γῆ {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Luke.8.15; κ. οἶνος “PFay.” 133.8 (4th c.AD)}}; στρατόπεδον κάλλιστον {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.60; ἀνταπεδώκατε πονηρὰ ἀντὶ καλῶν LXX.Gen.44.4; κ. ἐς στρατιάν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.6; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 295c, “Grg.” 474d, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, λόφος κάλλιστος τρέχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.26}}; ἐν καλῷ [τόπῳ] in a **good** place, καθίζεσθαι, ὁρμεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 292, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.25; ἐν καλῷ μὲν τοῦ κόλπου καὶ τῶν πόλεων, ἐν κ. δὲ τοῦ τὴν Χώραν βλάπτειν, [prev. work] 6.2.9}}; ἐν καλῷ under **favourable circumstances**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.59.60}}; ἐν κ. (i.e. Χρόνῳ) in **good time**, in **season**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1106}}; ἐν οὐ κ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 579}}; ἐν καλῷ [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 384 (so καλόν ἐστι}} with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1155 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 278, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.2); ἐς καλόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 78, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89e, “Smp.” 174e}}; τί γὰρ ἐμοὶ ζῆν καλό; what is the **good** of life to me? {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.594}}; καλῇ πίστει, = Latin _bona fide,_ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PTeb. 418.14 (3rd c.AD).}}
**A.II.2** of sacrifices, **auspicious**, σφάγια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 379; οἰωνοί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1333; ἱερά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.92; τὸ τέλος κ. τῆς ἐξόδου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.9; κ. τὰ ἱερὰ ἦν αὐτῷ [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.2.3}}: with _infinitive_, ἰέναι.. κ. ἡμῖν τὰ ἱερὰ ἦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 2.2.3: Comedy texts, τὰ τῆς πυγῆς κ. (for τοῦ θεοῦ) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 868.}}
**A.III** in a moral sense, **beautiful, noble, honourable**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _neuter_, οὐ καλὸν ἔειπες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.166, compare 17.381; μεῖζον κλέος.. καὶ κάλλιον 18.255}}; frequently καλόν [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, κ. τοι σὺν ἐμοὶ τὸν κήδειν ὅς κ᾽ ἐμὲ κήδῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.615}}; οὐ γὰρ ἔμοιγε κ. (i.e. ἄρχειν){{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.440; οὐ κ. ἀτέμβειν οὐδὲ δίκαιον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.294}}; so in Trag., καλόν μοι τοῦτο ποιούσῃ θανεῖν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 72, etc.; μάθετε καλὸν ποιεῖν LXX.Isa.1.17}}: _comparative_, οὐ μέν τοι τόδε κάλλιον οὐδὲ ἔοικε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.159, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.52; after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus frequently of actions, etc., κάλων κἄσλων 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 2.4 (unless of persons here)}}; κ. ἔργματα **noble** deeds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).42, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 839, etc.}}; ἀναστροφὴ κ. {{NT|NT.1Pet.2.12}}: in _plural_, **excellences**, πλῆθος καλῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.45; πολλῶν καλῶν δεῖ τῷ καλόν τι μωμένῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 938}}; τὰ τοῦ παιδὸς κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.17.}}
**A.III.2** τὸ κ. **moral beauty, virtue, honour**, opposed to τὸ αἰσχρόν, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.1.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 183d, etc.; ὅττι καλόν, φίλον ἐστί, τὸ δ᾽ οὐ καλὸν οὐ φίλον ἐστίν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 17, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 881 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 216c; οὐ ταὐτὸν ἡγῇ σύ, ὡς ἔοικας, κ. τε καὶ ἀγαθὸν καὶ κακὸν καὶ αἰσχρόν [prev. author] “Grg.” 474d, compare “Smp.” 201e}}; τοὐμὸν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 300.}}
**A.III.3** of persons, in early writers coupled with ἀγαθός, see at {καλοκἀγαθός}; later κ. ποιμήν {{NT|NT.John.10.11}}; κ. στρατιώτης {{NT|NT.2Tim.2.3.}}
**A.IV** in _Attic dialect_ and Trag. frequently ironically, **fine, specious**, γέρας κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 209; κ. γὰρ οὑμὸς βίοτος ὥστε θαυμάσαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 393, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 652; κ. Χάρις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.65; κ. ὕβριν ὑβρισμένοι [prev. author] 23.121; καί σοι.. θωπεῦσαι καλόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1003; μετ᾽ ὀνομάτων καλῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.89.}}
**B** Degrees of _comparative_: _comparative_ καλλίων, ον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.52, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.396, etc.: _neuter_ κάλιον [ᾰ] 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 134}}: _superlative_ κάλλιστος, η, ον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.233, etc.}}; late καλλιώτερος or -ότερος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“POxy.” 1672.6 (1st c.AD), Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 966; also καλώτερος 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus “Epim.” 69.}}
**C** _adverb_:—Poets frequently use _neuter_ καλόν as _adverb_, κ. ἀείδειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.570, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.155; καλά 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.326}}; later τὸ κ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.3, 18, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 53, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.54.}}
**C.II** regular _adverb_ **καλῶς** (_Doric dialect_ καλώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 22)}}, **well, rightly**, οὐδ᾽ ἔτι κ. οἶκος ἐμὸς διόλωλε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.64}}; κ. ζῆν, τεθνηκέναι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 479, etc.}}; κ. φρονεῖν to be in one's **right** mind, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 836; οὐ κ. ταρβεῖς [prev. author] “Tr.” 457}}; κ. ἀγωνιεῖσθαι **fairly, on the merits of the case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.88}}; Χρήματα δατῆθθαι κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 4.39; κ. εἰρημένα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 576.6; κάλλιον λέγεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161b; κάλλιστ᾽ ἂν εἴποι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1172: frequently in phrase καλῶς καὶ εὖ, καλῶς τε καὶ εὖ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319e, “Prm.” 128b, etc.}}
**C.II.2** of good fortune, **well, happily**, κ. πράσσειν, ={εὖ π}., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 979, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 271; κ. καὶ εὖ πράττειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 172a}}; κ. ἔχειν to be **well**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 799, etc.; κ. ἔχει σοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 946, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 816}}; κ. ἔχει with _infinitive_, 'tis **well** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.1: with _genitive_, κ. ἔχειν τινός}} to be **well off in** **respect to** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 29; κ. παράπλου κεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.36; εἰ κ. σφίσιν ἔχοι [prev. author] 4.117; οὔτε τοῖς θεοῖς ἔφη κ. ἔχειν, εἰ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.3; καλλιόνως ἔχει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 169e, etc.; κάλλιστα ἕζει [prev. author] “Hp.Ma.” 295b.}}
**C.II.3** καλῶς, ={πάνυ}, **thoroughly, altogether**, τὸν κ. εὐδαίμονα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 317, = 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 934; κ. ἔξοιδα [prev. author] “OC” 269, compare “OT” 1008; κ. ὑπὸ τοῦ πυρὸς διεφθάρθαι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.108}}: _comparative_, κάλλιον εἰδέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 300d}}; κάλλιον ἐοικέναι to be **just** like, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Genit.” 8.}}
**C.II.4** κ. ἀκούειν to be **well** spoken of, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 285, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.177e.}}
**C.II.5** κ. ποιῶν **rightly, deservedly**, κ. ποιῶν ἀπόλλυται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 863, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.28, +others, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.232}}; in requests, κ. ποιήσεις πριάμενος, etc., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.143 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; also with _infinitive_, κ. π. γράψαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1203.7 (1st c.BC), etc.}}
**C.II.6** in answers, to approve the words of the former speaker, **well said**! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1216, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.15}}; also, to decline an offer courteously, **no, thank you**! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 888; κ. ἔχει 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 165, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 266}}; πάνυ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 512; ἀμέλει κ. [prev. work] 532: _superlative_, κάλλιστ᾽, ἐπαινῶ [prev. work] 508; ἔχει κάλλιστα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.3.}}
**C.II.7** ironically, **finely**, καλῶς ἐρήμης γ᾽ ἂν σὺ γῆς ἄρχοις μόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 739, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 588, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 344, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.69.}}
**C.II.8** κ. ὁ ἱερεύς **hurrah for** the priest! {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“SIG” 1109.14 (Athens, 2nd c.AD).}}
**C.II.9** repeated with the _adjective_, καλὴ καλῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 253, “Pax” 1330, “Ec.” 730; καλὸς κάλλιστά τε ῥέξαις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.94.}}
**C.II.10** _comparative_ καλλιόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Tht.” [same place], “Lg.” 660d}}: _superlative_ καλλίστως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PMag.Par.” 1.2443,2465, Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 310.}}
**D** for compounds, see at {καλλι-, καλο-}.
**E** Quantity: ᾱ in _Epic dialect_ and early {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus Poets (except “hymnus ad Venerem” 29, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 63, “Th.” 585)}}: ᾰ in Lyric poetry (except κᾱλῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.206) and Trag. (4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 314, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1381 are corrupt).--In Eleg., epigram, and Bucol. Poets ᾰ or ᾱ (the latter usually in thesi)}}; τὰ μὴ κᾰλὰ κᾱλὰ πέφανται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.19, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.115, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 55}}.--In _comparative_, ῐ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ῑ in Trag. and later.}}

2571:
** κάλυμμα** [ᾰ], ατος, τό,
(κᾰλύπτω) **head-covering, hood, veil**, κ. κυάνεον dark **veil** worn in mourning, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.93, “hymnus ad Cererem” 42}}; Χρύσεον κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 16.38; ὁ Χρησμὸς οὐκέτ᾽ ἐκ καλυμμάτων ἔσται δεδορκὼς νεογάμου νύμφης δίκην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1178 (but _metaphorically_, δείξω τάδ᾽ ἐκ καλυμμάτων}} 'I will lift the **veil**', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1078)}}; λεπτῶν ὄμμα διὰ καλυμμάτων ἔχους᾽ {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 372, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 532, “fragment” 320.5, 4th c.BC: Dicaearchus Geographus 1.18; κάρα καλύμμασι κρυψάμενον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 245 (Lyric poetry); Χαλᾶτε πᾶν κάλυμμ᾽ ἀπ᾽ ὀφθαλμῶν [prev. author] “El.” 1468; Μωυσῆς ἐτίθει κ. ἐπὶ τὸ πρόσωπον NT.2Cor.3.13.}}
**2** **fishing-net** shaped like a sack, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 3.82}}; βουλευτοῖσιν ἐν καλύμμασιν, of the garment thrown by Clytaemnestra over her husband, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 494.}}
**3** **skull** (as the brain's covering), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 906.}}
**4** **grave,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 7.227 (3rd c.BC(?): Diotimus Epigrammaticus).}}
**5** in animals, **the covering** of the gills of fishes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 505a2}}; **operculum** of testaceans, {{Refs|[prev. work] 547b5}}; **eyelid**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.66.}}
**6** **covering of honeycomb**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 624b31.}}
**7** **shell** of fruit, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 269.}}
**8** **shutter**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.91.}}
**9** **sheathing-planks** for a roof, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1668.57}}; but, **slabs** for closing coffers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work]4.1484.57 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 11(2).144 A 42 (Delos, 4th c.BC).}}
**10** perhaps **paving-slab,** {{Refs|Milet. Ergebnisse 7.60.53.}}

2572:
** κᾰλύπτ-ω**,
_Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ κάλυπτον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.20}}: _future_ **-ψω**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1045:}}_aorist_ ἐκάλυψα, _Epic dialect_ κάλ- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.693:}} —_middle_, _future_ καλύψομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(ἐγ-) 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.12, (συγ-) 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.59 J}}.: _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ καλυψάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.141, +others}}:—_passive_, _future_ καλυφθήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.11.11, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.130J., 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 9.3, (δια-) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.13}}: _aorist_ ἐκαλύφθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.402, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 531}}: _aorist 2_ _participle_ καλῠφείς {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“CPR” 239.5 (3rd c.AD)}}: _perfect_ κεκάλυμμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.360, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.4, 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 26.3}}: _pluperfect_ κεκάλυπτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.549.--Rare in Prose, except in compounds (Cf. κέλυφος, καλύβη}}, Latin _oc-culo_, **celo.**)
**I** **cover**, frequently with _dative_ _instrumental_, παρδαλέῃ.. μετάφρενον εὐρὺ κάλυψε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.29; σάωσε δὲ νυκτὶ καλύψας 5.23 (but in 13.425, ἐρεβεννῇ νυκτὶ καλύψαι}} is **to kill**); simply, **cover**, μέλαν δέ ἑ κῦμα κάλυψεν {{Refs|23.693; ἐπισκύνιον κάτω ἕλκεται ὄσσε καλύπτων 17.136}}; [πέτρον] περὶ Χεὶρ ἐκάλυψεν his hand **covered, grasped** a stone, {{Refs|16.735}}; of death, τὼ.. τέλος θανάτοιο κάλυψεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5.553; τὸν δὲ σκότος ὄσσε κάλυψεν 4.461, 503, etc.; τὸν δὲ κατ᾽ ὀφθαλμῶν ἐρεβεννὴ νὺξ ἐκάλυψεν 13.580; τὼ δέ οἱ ὄσσε νὺξ ἐκάλυψε μέλαινα 14.439; so τὸν δ᾽ ἄχεος νεφέλη ἐκάλυψε 17.591; ἑ πένθος ὀφθαλμοὺς ἐκάλυψε 11.250: frequently in Lyric poetry and Trag., ὅταν θανάτοιο κυάνεον νέφος καλύψῃ 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.64}}; κ. Χθονὶ γυῖα, i.e. **to be buried**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.38}}; but Χθονί, τάφῳ κ., **bury**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 582 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 28}}; γῇ, Χέρσῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1633, “Hel.” 1066: _absolutely_, καὐτὴ καλύψω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1045}}: rare in Prose, μὴ καλύπτειν τὰ δολοσχερέα τοῖς εἱματίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 1218.7 (Ceos, 5th c.BC)}}; of armour, **protect**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 12.5}}:—_middle_, **cover** or **veil oneself**, ἀργεννῇσι καλυψαμένη ὀθόνῃσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.141; κρηδέμνῳ δ᾽ ἐφύπερθε καλύψατο 14.184}}; λευκοῖσιν φαρέεσσι καλυψαμένω (_feminine_ dual) Χρόα καλόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 198: _absolutely_, καλυψάμενος δ᾽ ἐνὶ νηῒ κείμην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.53}}:—_passive_, ἀσπίδι ταυρείῃ κεκαλυμμένος.. ὤμους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.360; ἐν Χλαίνῃ κεκ. 24.163; Χαλκῷ, ἠέρι, 13.192, 21.549; οἰὸς ἀώτῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.443; φρικὶ καλυφθείς, of the sea, 4.402}}: in Prose, τὸν νεκρὸν κεκαλυμμένον φερέτω σιγᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Michel” 995 C 32 (Delph., 5th-4th c.BC)}}; [βράγχια] καλυπτόμενα καλύμματι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 505a6; κεκαλυμμένος}}**veiled** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(2).514.10 (from Lycosura).}}
**I.2** **hide, conceal**, κεκαλυμμένοι ἵππῳ **concealed** in it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.503}}:—_active_, {{NT+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 52, etc.; ἔξω μέ που καλύψατε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1411, cf. NT.Luke.23.30; κρυφῇ κ. καρδίᾳ τι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1254}}; σιγῇ κ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 712: _metaphorically_, ἐκάλυψας τὰς ἁμαρτίας αὐτῶν LXX.Psa.84(85).2, cf. NT.Jam.5.20.}}
**I.3** **cover with dishonour, throw a cloud over**, σὺ μὴ κάλυπτε τὰς εὐδαίμονας ἔργοις Ἀθήνας ἀνοσίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 282.}}
**II** **put over as a covering**, πρόσθε δέ οἱ πέπλοιο πτύγμ᾽ ἐκάλυψεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.315}}; τόσσην οἱ ἄσιν καθύπερθε καλύψω I **will put** mud **over** him,{{Refs|21.321; ἀμφὶ Μενοιτιάδῃ σάκος εὐρὺ καλύψας 17.132; πρόσθεν δὲ σάκος στέρνοιο κάλυψε 22.313.}}

2573:
Included with: ** καλός**, ή, όν,
_Aeolic dialect_ **κάλος** (see. below), α, ον, _Boeotian dialect_ **καλϝός** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 538 (6th c.BC)}}:—
**A** **beautiful**, of outward form, frequently of persons, κάλλιστος ἀνὴρ ὑπὸ Ἴλιον ἦλθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.673: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} usually in the phrase κ. τε μέγας τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.108, +others}}; μέγας καὶ κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.513; καλή τε μεγάλη τε 13.289, 15.418}}; καλὸς δέμας **beautiful** of form,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.307; κ. ἰδέᾳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10 (11). 103; εἶδος κάλλιστος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.1; κ. τὸ σῶμα [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.6.30; τὰς ὄψεις 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 195}}; Χορῷ καλή **beauteous** in the dance, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.180}}: with _infinitive_, καλλίονες καὶ μείζονες εἰσοράασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.396}}; ἐσορᾶν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.19}}: frequently of parts of the body, **fair, shapely**, κ. πρόσωπα, ὅμματα, παρήϊα, σφυρά, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.285, 23.66, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.208, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.147; Χρώς 5.354, +others}}; of clothes, εἵματα, φάρεα, Χιτών, Χλαῖνα, πέδιλα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.111, 24.277, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.43, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.365, 1.96; πέπλος κάλλιστος ποικίλμασιν ἠδὲ μέγιστος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.294}}; of arms and armour, κνημῖδες, ἀσπίς, σάκος, κόρυς, φάσγανα, ἔντεα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.331, 11.33, 22.314, 18.612, 15.713, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.18}}; of buildings, manufactured articles, etc., αὐλὴ κ. τε μεγάλη τε {{Refs|14.7}}; κ. δώματα, τεῖχος, πόλιες,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.387, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.447, 18.491; ἄμαξα, τράπεζα, θρόνος, 24.267, 11.629, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.131}}; also τέμενος, ἀγρός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.314, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.206; so after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, Λύδιον κ. ἔργον 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 19, etc.}}; ἐέρσα κ. ead.{{Refs|“Supp.” 25.12.}}
**A.2** in _Attic dialect_ added to a name in token of love or admiration, as Ἀρίσημος κ. {{Refs|“IG” 12.921, etc.}}; ἐν τοῖσι τοίχοις ἔγραφ᾽ Ἀθηναῖοι καλοί” {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 144, compare “V.” 98}}; Ἀλκιβιάδης ὁ καλός, Σαπφὼ ἡ καλή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.113b, “Phdr.” 235c.}}
**A.2.b** ἡ Καλή or Καλλίστη, as epithet, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 140 (Lyric poetry), 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.29.2, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4445 (from Beroea).}}
**A.2.c** Καλοί, οἱ, divinities worshipped in childbirth, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1445 (Messene, 2nd c.BC).}}
**A.3** τὸ καλόν **beauty**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 79, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 21 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.; τὰ καλά **the proprieties** or **elegancies** of life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, 207; ἁπάντων καλῶν ἄμμορος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.84; αἱ τέχναι ἃς πηγάς φασι τῶν κ. εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.13.}}
**A.II** with _reference_ to use, **good, of fine quality**, κ. λιμήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.263}}; Βορέῃ ἀνέμῳ.. καλῷ **fair**, {{Refs|14.253, 299}}; κ. ἀργύριον, opposed to κίβδηλον, **genuine** silver, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.1.9}}; opposed to ἀποτετριμμένον, **good** silver currency, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 21.33 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐλαῖαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.49.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; γῆ {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Luke.8.15; κ. οἶνος “PFay.” 133.8 (4th c.AD)}}; στρατόπεδον κάλλιστον {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.60; ἀνταπεδώκατε πονηρὰ ἀντὶ καλῶν LXX.Gen.44.4; κ. ἐς στρατιάν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.6; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 295c, “Grg.” 474d, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, λόφος κάλλιστος τρέχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.26}}; ἐν καλῷ [τόπῳ] in a **good** place, καθίζεσθαι, ὁρμεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 292, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.25; ἐν καλῷ μὲν τοῦ κόλπου καὶ τῶν πόλεων, ἐν κ. δὲ τοῦ τὴν Χώραν βλάπτειν, [prev. work] 6.2.9}}; ἐν καλῷ under **favourable circumstances**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.59.60}}; ἐν κ. (i.e. Χρόνῳ) in **good time**, in **season**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1106}}; ἐν οὐ κ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 579}}; ἐν καλῷ [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 384 (so καλόν ἐστι}} with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1155 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 278, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.2); ἐς καλόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 78, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89e, “Smp.” 174e}}; τί γὰρ ἐμοὶ ζῆν καλό; what is the **good** of life to me? {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.594}}; καλῇ πίστει, = Latin _bona fide,_ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PTeb. 418.14 (3rd c.AD).}}
**A.II.2** of sacrifices, **auspicious**, σφάγια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 379; οἰωνοί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1333; ἱερά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.92; τὸ τέλος κ. τῆς ἐξόδου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.9; κ. τὰ ἱερὰ ἦν αὐτῷ [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.2.3}}: with _infinitive_, ἰέναι.. κ. ἡμῖν τὰ ἱερὰ ἦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 2.2.3: Comedy texts, τὰ τῆς πυγῆς κ. (for τοῦ θεοῦ) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 868.}}
**A.III** in a moral sense, **beautiful, noble, honourable**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _neuter_, οὐ καλὸν ἔειπες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.166, compare 17.381; μεῖζον κλέος.. καὶ κάλλιον 18.255}}; frequently καλόν [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, κ. τοι σὺν ἐμοὶ τὸν κήδειν ὅς κ᾽ ἐμὲ κήδῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.615}}; οὐ γὰρ ἔμοιγε κ. (i.e. ἄρχειν){{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.440; οὐ κ. ἀτέμβειν οὐδὲ δίκαιον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.294}}; so in Trag., καλόν μοι τοῦτο ποιούσῃ θανεῖν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 72, etc.; μάθετε καλὸν ποιεῖν LXX.Isa.1.17}}: _comparative_, οὐ μέν τοι τόδε κάλλιον οὐδὲ ἔοικε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.159, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.52; after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus frequently of actions, etc., κάλων κἄσλων 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 2.4 (unless of persons here)}}; κ. ἔργματα **noble** deeds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).42, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 839, etc.}}; ἀναστροφὴ κ. {{NT|NT.1Pet.2.12}}: in _plural_, **excellences**, πλῆθος καλῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.45; πολλῶν καλῶν δεῖ τῷ καλόν τι μωμένῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 938}}; τὰ τοῦ παιδὸς κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.17.}}
**A.III.2** τὸ κ. **moral beauty, virtue, honour**, opposed to τὸ αἰσχρόν, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.1.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 183d, etc.; ὅττι καλόν, φίλον ἐστί, τὸ δ᾽ οὐ καλὸν οὐ φίλον ἐστίν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 17, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 881 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 216c; οὐ ταὐτὸν ἡγῇ σύ, ὡς ἔοικας, κ. τε καὶ ἀγαθὸν καὶ κακὸν καὶ αἰσχρόν [prev. author] “Grg.” 474d, compare “Smp.” 201e}}; τοὐμὸν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 300.}}
**A.III.3** of persons, in early writers coupled with ἀγαθός, see at {καλοκἀγαθός}; later κ. ποιμήν {{NT|NT.John.10.11}}; κ. στρατιώτης {{NT|NT.2Tim.2.3.}}
**A.IV** in _Attic dialect_ and Trag. frequently ironically, **fine, specious**, γέρας κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 209; κ. γὰρ οὑμὸς βίοτος ὥστε θαυμάσαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 393, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 652; κ. Χάρις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.65; κ. ὕβριν ὑβρισμένοι [prev. author] 23.121; καί σοι.. θωπεῦσαι καλόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1003; μετ᾽ ὀνομάτων καλῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.89.}}
**B** Degrees of _comparative_: _comparative_ καλλίων, ον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.52, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.396, etc.: _neuter_ κάλιον [ᾰ] 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 134}}: _superlative_ κάλλιστος, η, ον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.233, etc.}}; late καλλιώτερος or -ότερος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“POxy.” 1672.6 (1st c.AD), Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 966; also καλώτερος 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus “Epim.” 69.}}
**C** _adverb_:—Poets frequently use _neuter_ καλόν as _adverb_, κ. ἀείδειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.570, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.155; καλά 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.326}}; later τὸ κ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.3, 18, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 53, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.54.}}
**C.II** regular _adverb_ **καλῶς** (_Doric dialect_ καλώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 22)}}, **well, rightly**, οὐδ᾽ ἔτι κ. οἶκος ἐμὸς διόλωλε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.64}}; κ. ζῆν, τεθνηκέναι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 479, etc.}}; κ. φρονεῖν to be in one's **right** mind, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 836; οὐ κ. ταρβεῖς [prev. author] “Tr.” 457}}; κ. ἀγωνιεῖσθαι **fairly, on the merits of the case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.88}}; Χρήματα δατῆθθαι κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 4.39; κ. εἰρημένα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 576.6; κάλλιον λέγεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161b; κάλλιστ᾽ ἂν εἴποι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1172: frequently in phrase καλῶς καὶ εὖ, καλῶς τε καὶ εὖ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319e, “Prm.” 128b, etc.}}
**C.II.2** of good fortune, **well, happily**, κ. πράσσειν, ={εὖ π}., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 979, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 271; κ. καὶ εὖ πράττειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 172a}}; κ. ἔχειν to be **well**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 799, etc.; κ. ἔχει σοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 946, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 816}}; κ. ἔχει with _infinitive_, 'tis **well** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.1: with _genitive_, κ. ἔχειν τινός}} to be **well off in** **respect to** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 29; κ. παράπλου κεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.36; εἰ κ. σφίσιν ἔχοι [prev. author] 4.117; οὔτε τοῖς θεοῖς ἔφη κ. ἔχειν, εἰ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.3; καλλιόνως ἔχει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 169e, etc.; κάλλιστα ἕζει [prev. author] “Hp.Ma.” 295b.}}
**C.II.3** καλῶς, ={πάνυ}, **thoroughly, altogether**, τὸν κ. εὐδαίμονα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 317, = 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 934; κ. ἔξοιδα [prev. author] “OC” 269, compare “OT” 1008; κ. ὑπὸ τοῦ πυρὸς διεφθάρθαι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.108}}: _comparative_, κάλλιον εἰδέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 300d}}; κάλλιον ἐοικέναι to be **just** like, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Genit.” 8.}}
**C.II.4** κ. ἀκούειν to be **well** spoken of, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 285, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.177e.}}
**C.II.5** κ. ποιῶν **rightly, deservedly**, κ. ποιῶν ἀπόλλυται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 863, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.28, +others, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.232}}; in requests, κ. ποιήσεις πριάμενος, etc., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.143 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; also with _infinitive_, κ. π. γράψαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1203.7 (1st c.BC), etc.}}
**C.II.6** in answers, to approve the words of the former speaker, **well said**! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1216, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.15}}; also, to decline an offer courteously, **no, thank you**! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 888; κ. ἔχει 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 165, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 266}}; πάνυ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 512; ἀμέλει κ. [prev. work] 532: _superlative_, κάλλιστ᾽, ἐπαινῶ [prev. work] 508; ἔχει κάλλιστα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.3.}}
**C.II.7** ironically, **finely**, καλῶς ἐρήμης γ᾽ ἂν σὺ γῆς ἄρχοις μόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 739, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 588, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 344, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.69.}}
**C.II.8** κ. ὁ ἱερεύς **hurrah for** the priest! {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“SIG” 1109.14 (Athens, 2nd c.AD).}}
**C.II.9** repeated with the _adjective_, καλὴ καλῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 253, “Pax” 1330, “Ec.” 730; καλὸς κάλλιστά τε ῥέξαις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.94.}}
**C.II.10** _comparative_ καλλιόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Tht.” [same place], “Lg.” 660d}}: _superlative_ καλλίστως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PMag.Par.” 1.2443,2465, Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 310.}}
**D** for compounds, see at {καλλι-, καλο-}.
**E** Quantity: ᾱ in _Epic dialect_ and early {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus Poets (except “hymnus ad Venerem” 29, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 63, “Th.” 585)}}: ᾰ in Lyric poetry (except κᾱλῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.206) and Trag. (4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 314, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1381 are corrupt).--In Eleg., epigram, and Bucol. Poets ᾰ or ᾱ (the latter usually in thesi)}}; τὰ μὴ κᾰλὰ κᾱλὰ πέφανται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.19, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.115, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 55}}.--In _comparative_, ῐ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ῑ in Trag. and later.}}

2577:
** κάμνω**,
_future_ κᾰμοῦμαι, καμῇ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 881, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1215}}; καμεῖται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.389, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 921e}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ **-έεσθαι**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.580:}}_aorist 2_ ἔκᾰμον, _Epic dialect_ κάμον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.187, +others}}; _infinitive_ καμεῖν, _Epic dialect_ _subjunctive_ _reduplicate_ κεκάμω, κεκάμῃσι, κεκάμωσιν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.168, 17.658, 7.5 (but 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus read κε κάμω, etc., probably rightly)}}: _perfect_ κέκμηκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.262, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ ἐκεκμήκεσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.98}}; _Epic dialect_ _participle_ κεκμηώς, κεκμηῶτι, κεκμηῶτα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.232, 6.261, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.31; κεκμηότας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.802}}; κεκμηῶτας is variant for{κεκμηκότας} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59}}:— _middle_, _aorist 2_ ἐκᾰμόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.130}}, _Epic dialect_ καμ- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.341.}}
**I** _transitive_, **work**, μίτρη, τὴν Χαλκῆες κάμον ἄνδρες **wrought** it, {{Refs|4.187, 216}}; ἐπεὶ πάνθ᾽ ὅπλα κάμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|18.614; σκῆπτρον.., τὸ μὲν Ἥφαιστος κάμε τεύχων 2.101, compare 8.195; κ. νῆας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.126; πέπλον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.338, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.105; ἵππον 11.523; λέχος 23.189}}; ἄστυ **build**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1322}}: also in _aorist_ _middle_, ἱρόν {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.718.}}
**I.2** _aorist_ _middle_, **win by toil**, τὰς (i.e. γυναῖκας) αὐτοὶ καμόμεσθα βίηφί τε δουρί τε μακρῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.341.}}
**I.3** _aorist_ _middle_, **labour, till**, οἵ κέ σφιν καὶ νῆσον.. ἐκάμοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.130; οἴκους 4th-3rd c.BC: Philetas 8.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **toil, labour**, τινι **for** one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.65; ὑπὲρ τῆς πόλεως 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.41}}: then, from the effect of continued work, **to be weary**, ἀνδρὶ δὲ κεκμηῶτι μένος μέγα οἶνος ἀέξει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.261, compare 11.802: with _accusative_ of the part, οὐδέ τι γυῖα.. κάμνει}} nor **is** he **weary in** limb, {{Refs|19.170}}, etc.; περὶ δ᾽ ἔγχεϊ Χεῖρα καμεῖται {{Refs|2.389; ὁ δ᾽ ἀριστερὸν ὦμον ἔκαμνεν 16.106: frequently with}}_participle_, κ. πολεμίζων, ἐλαύνοντες, ἐρεθίζων, **is weary** of fighting, rowing, etc., {{Refs|1.168, 7.5, 17.658}}, etc.; οὐ μέν θην κάμετον.. ὀλλῦσαι Τρῶας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.448; ἔκαμον δέ μοι ὄσσε πάντῃ παπταίνοντι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.232}}; but οὐδέ τι τόξον δὴν ἔκαμον τανύων I **did** not long **strain over** stringing the bow, i.e. did it without **effort**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.426, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.22: later frequently with _negative_, οὔτοι καμοῦμαι.. λέγουσα}} I **shall** never **be tired** of saying, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 881; μὴ κάμῃς λέγων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1143; οὐκ ἂν κάμοιμι τὰς κακὰς κτείνων [prev. author] “Or.” 1590; οὔποτε κάμοιμ᾽ ἂν ὀρχουμένη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 541 (Lyric poetry)}}; κ. εὐεργετῶν, ἐπαινῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 470c, “Lg.” 921e: with _dative_, κ. δαπάναις}} **to grow tired** in spending, spare expense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.90.}}
**II.2** **to be hard-pressed, worsted**, in battle or contest, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.78, 80}}; τὸ κάμνον στρατοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 709.}}
**II.3** **to be sick** or **suffering**, τί πάσχει; τί κάμνει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 708}}; οἱ κάμνοντες **the sick**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.197, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 282, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.64, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407c, NT.Jam.5.15, etc.}}; of a doctor's **patients**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 1, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.243, “SIG” 943.10 (from Cos)}}; καμοῦσα ἀπέθανε **having fallen sick**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.120: with _accusative_ _cognate_, κάμνειν νόσον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 990, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408e}}; [τὴν ποδάγραν] variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 604a23; τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.111}}; τὰ σώματα **to be ill** or **distempered** in body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 478a; ὠσίν τε κὤμμασιν 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.32; πάθᾳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.48; νοσήμασι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 603a30; ἀπὸ τοῦ τραύματος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 60; ὑπὸ νόσου 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.14.2.}}
**II.4** generally, **to be distressed, meet with disaster**, στρατοῦ καμόντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 670; τῷ πεποιημένῳ κ. μεγάλως 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.118, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 482 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1138, “HF” 293}}; οὐ καμῇ τοὐμὸν μέρος **wilt** not **have to complain**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1215; κ. ἔν τινι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 306, “IA” 966}}; of a ship, νεὼς καμούσης ποντίῳ πρὸς κύματι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 210: with _accusative_ _cognate_, οὐκ ἴσον καμὼν ἐμοὶ λύπης}} not **having borne** an equal share of grief, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 532.}}
**II.5** in _aorist_ _participle_, of the dead, i. e. either **outworn**, or **those whose work is done**, or **those who have met with disaster**, οἳ ὑπένερθε καμόντας ἀνθρώπους τίνυσθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.278, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.49; βροτῶν εἴδωλα καμόντων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.476}}; εἴδωλα κ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|. 24.14, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.72, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 231, etc.}}: also in _perfect_ _participle_ in Trag. and Prose, κεκμηκότες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 284, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 756, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 718a, 927b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1101a35}}; ἱερὰ τῶν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 96}}; also in the finite Verb, ὅπη ἄνθρωπος ἔκαμε {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.158 (from Cyrene)}}.--The _perfect_ is always _intransitive_ (Cf. Sanskrit _śamnīte_ 'work hard', 'serve zealously', **śamitár-** 'sacrificing priest', Gr. εἰρο-κόμος, κομέω, κομίζω.)

2578:
** κάμπτω**,
_future_ κάμψω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.118, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 91}}: _aorist 1_ ἔκαμψα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.453, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.51, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ καμφθήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 77.33, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 2.15}}: _aorist_ ἐκάμφθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 513, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58}}: _perfect_ _infinitive_ κεκάμφθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 67}}, _participle_ κεκαμμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1016a12, (ἐπι-, συγ-) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 3, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.2}}. (Cogn. with Lithuanian _ka[mtilde]p-as_ 'corner', **ku[mtilde]p-as** 'curved', and probably Latin _campus_):—**bend, curve**, ὄφρα ἴτυν κάμψῃ that he **may bend** it into a chariot-rail, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.486 (so _metaphorically_, κ. νέας ἁψῖδας ἐπῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 53): frequently in phrase, γόνυ κ}}. **bend** the knee so as to sit down and rest, φημί μιν ἀσπασίως γόνυ κάμψειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.118, compare 19.72; ὁ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἄμφω γούνατ᾽ ἔκαμψε Χεῖράς τε στιβαράς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.453}}; οὐ κάμπτων γόνυ, i.e. never resting, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 32}}; ἄσμενός τἂν.. κάμψειεν γόνυ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 398; ἵζω.. κάμψας γόνυ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1150; so κ. κῶλα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 19}}; then κάμπτειν alone, **sit down, rest**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 85, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1080 (Lyric poetry)}}; also γόνυ κ. **bend** the knee in worship, {{LXX|LXX.Isa.45.23, etc.}}:—_passive_, **bend oneself**, opposed to ἐκτείνεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 74b; ὥσπερ ξύλον καμπτόμενον εὐθύνουσιν [prev. author] “Prt.” 325d}}; ἡ κεκαμμένη (i.e. γραμμή) a **bent** line, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphorical” [prev. cited]}}
**II** **turn** or **guide** a horse or chariot **round** the turning-post {{Refs|compare (καμπτήρ 11), κάμψαι διαύλου θάτερον κῶλον πάλιν}} **to double** the post and return along the second half of the δίαυλος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 344; κ. δρόμον 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9.26}}; κάμπτοντος ἵππου as the horse **was turning**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 744; κ. περὶ νύσσαν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.120: _metaphorically_, κ. βίον}} **to make the last turn in the** **course** of life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 91; κ. βίου τέλος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 87, “El.” 956; ὅταν κάμψῃς καὶ τελευτήσῃς βίον [prev. author] “Hel.” 1666}}; ἑξηκοστὸν ἥλιον κ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 10.1}}; διὰ λόγου κάμψαι κακά **to end** evils by reasoning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 748.}}
**II.2** of seamen, **double** a headland, Ἡρακλέας στήλας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.42}}; τὸ ἀκρωτήριον, τὴν ἄκρην, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.43, 7.122; ὡς δὲ τὴν ἄκραν κάμπτοντας ἡμᾶς εἶδον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 15, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschrio Lyricus 8.3}}; Μαλέαν κ. poetical cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.20, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.64, etc.; κ. περὶ ἄκραν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 96}}; κ. κόλπον **wind round** the bay, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.58.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, πάλιν κ. **turn** back, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1225, “Rh.” 234 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐγγὺς τῶν ἐμῶν κάμπτεις φρενῶν (κάμπτῃ codices) thou **comest** near my meaning, {{Refs|[prev. author] “IT” 815.}}
**III** in Music, κάμπτων με καὶ στρέφων ὅλην διέφθορεν (i.e. Phrynis) with his **turns** and twists, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 145.15; κ. καμπήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 969; κ. ᾠδάς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.39.}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, κάμπτειν τινά **bow down, humble**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.51; ὁ Χρόνος μ᾽ ἔκαμψε 4th c.BC: Crates Thebanus Poeta Philosophus 17}}:—_passive_, **to be bent** or **bowed down**, πημοναῖσι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 239, 308, compare 513}}; κάμπτομαι I **submit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 320b, etc.; κάμπτεσθαι καὶ ἕλκεσθαι πρὸς φιλοσοφίαν [prev. author] “R.” 494e}}; πολλὰ κάμπτονται καὶ συγκλῶνται are **warped**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 173b}}: _absolutely_, **to be moved to pity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58 (in full κ. εἰς ἔλεον 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 59.85).}}

2579:
** κἄν**
(not κᾄν), by crasis,
**I** for καὶ ἄν.., variant in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 357, frequently in _Attic dialect_: not often when καί}} is simply _copulative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 79a, “Grg.” 514d}}; but frequently when καί is _intensive_, ὅ γε κ. μέγα δοίη **even** a great thing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus [same place]; κακὸν δὲ κ. ἐν ἡμέρᾳ γνοίης μιᾷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 615, compare 591, “Aj.” 45, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1130, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.61, etc.}}; sometimes repeated after or before a Verb with ἄν, ἐπεὶ κ. σὺ.., εἴ τίς σε διδάξειεν.., βελτίων ἂν γένοιο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 318b, compare “R.” 515e}}; frequently in the phrase κ. εἰ, where καί properly belongs to εἰ, **even if**, and ἄν to the Verb that follows in apodosi, νῦν δέ μοι δοκεῖ, κ. ἀσέβειαν εἰ καταγιγνώσκοι, τὰ προσήκοντα ποιεῖν (for καὶ εἰ καταγιγνώσκοι, ποιεῖν ἄν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.51: hence}}
**I.2** even when the Verb in apodosi was of a tense that could not be joined with ἄν, κ. εἰ πολλαὶ [αἱ ἀρεταί].. εἰσιν, ἕν γέ τι εἶδος ταὐτὸν ἅπασαι ἔχουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 72c; κ. εἰ μή τῳ δοκεῖ [prev. author] “R.” 473a, 579d, compare 408b, “Phd.” 71b, “Sph.” 247e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 136a31, +others}}
**I.3** in later Gr. without εἰ, simply as a stronger form of καί, **even**, εἴσελθε κ. νῦν {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 342; κ. νεκρῷ Χάρισαι τὰ σὰ Χείλεα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.41, compare 35 (variant) (and so with εἷς, μία, ἕν, κ. μίαν ἡμέραν δόντες αὐτοῖς variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.19; εἰ κ. ἕν τι φαίνοιτο 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.195, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.29); ἐὰν ἅψωμαι κ. τῶν ἱματίων NT.Mark.5.28}}; κ. νῦν now **at any rate,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 2151.7 (3rd c.AD)}}; κἂν ὧς **even** so, **nevertheless**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work]123.7 (3rd-4th c.AD)}}; οἷς οὐδὲ κ. ὄνος ὑπῆρξε πώποτε {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 20 codices, compare “DDeor.” 5.2, etc.}}
**II** for καὶ ἄν (ἐάν), **even if**, with the same moods as ἐάν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 15, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319c, etc.: frequently used elliptically, ἄνδρα Χρὴ δοκεῖν πεσεῖν ἂν κ. (i.e. πέσῃ) ἀπὸ σμικροῦ κακοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1078, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 92, “Ach.” 1021}}, and so probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1483: later followed by _indicative_, κἂν γὰρ οὕτω φαμέν 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 70.22.}}
**II.2** κἄν.., κἄν.., **whether.., or**.., κἂν μεγάλην πόλιν οἰκῶσι κἂν μικράν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.15.}}

2583:
** κᾰνών**, όνος, ὁ,
**straight rod, bar**, especially to keep a thing straight:
**1** in _plural_, **staves** which preserved the shape of the shield, [ἀσπίδα] δύω κανόνεσσ᾽ ἀραρυῖαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.407, compare 8.193, 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 21.257a.}}
**2** **weaver's rod**, to which alternate threads of the warp were attached, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.761, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 822 (_anapaest meter_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.156b, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 37.631.}}
**3** **ruddled line** used by masons or carpenters, πύργους.. ὀρθοῖσιν ἔθεμεν κανόσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 6; βάθρα φοίνικι κανόνι.. ἡρμοσμένα [prev. author] “HF” 945; also κ. λίθινος}}**rule, straight-edge,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.313.113, 373.217, +others, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 56b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 10.2, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.120 (from Callicter)}}; ὥστε τέκτονος παρὰ στάθμην ἰόντος ὀρθοῦται κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 474.5; κανόνα προσφέρειν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.199; ποιῶν ὀρθὰ πάντα πρὸς κανόνα “IG” 7.3073.108 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC)}}; κανόνεσσι.. μετρήσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.724, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1004}}; μολίβδινος κ., i.e. a flexible **rule** that cannot be depended on for straight measurement, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1137b31 (unless ={κῦμα}); κανόνα ποιῆσαι στρεβλόν [prev. author] “Rh.” 1354a26.}}]
**3.b** **ruler,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 6.63.2 (6th c.AD: Damocharis Epigrammaticus).}}
**3.c** _metaphorically_, κανόνες καὶ πήχεις ἐπῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 799; λαμπρὰ μὲν ἀκτὶς ἡλίου, κ. σαφής 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 650.}}
**4** **beam** or **tongue of the balance**, στῆσαι ἐκ κανόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.334, cf. Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 811.}}
**5** **curtain-rod**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Chares Historicus “fragment” 4J.}}
**6** in _plural_, **reeds** of a wind-organ, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.365 (4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator Imp.).}}
**7** **bed-post**, {{LXX|LXX.Jdt.13.6.}}
**8** in _plural_, **poles** from which the **ancilia** were suspended when carried, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.71.}}
**9** _plural_, **bars** of a window, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 5.547.9 (3rd c.BC).}}
**10** in Music, **monochord**, κατατομὴ κανόνος, title of work by {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.100K., 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Harmonica - Opera Mathematica” 1.8, 2.12; ὀκτάχορδος, πεντεκαιδεκάχορδος κ., [prev. work] 2.2, 3.1 at Title Page}}
**11** **cross-bar of** κιθάρα, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus}}**inHarm.**p.207.
**II** _metaphorically_, **rule, standard**, κανόνι τοῦ καλοῦ μαθών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 602; γνώμης πονηροῖς κανόσιν ἀναμετρούμενος τὸ σῶφρον [prev. author] “El.” 52; κανόνα προσάγειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist. Conscr.” 5}}; of the law, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 9}}; ὁ σπουδαῖος.. ὥσπερ κ. καὶ μέτρον αὐτῶν (i.e. καλῶν καὶ ἡδέων) ὤν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1113a33, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.4.5; τὴν ἐλευθερίαν καὶ τὸ μηδέν᾽ ἔχειν δεσπότην αὑτῶν, ἃ τοῖς προτέροις Ἕλλησιν ὅροι τῶν ἀγαθῶν ἦσαν καὶ κανόνες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.296; ὡς κανόνι τῷ πάθει πᾶν ἀγαθὸν κρίνοντες 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.63U.}}; ὁ Ἐπικούρου κ. his treatise on Logic, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 34, 4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 2.15}}; ὁ τῆς φιλοσοφίας κ. {{LXX|LXX.4Mac.7.21: Κανόνες, οἱ}}, title of treatise by Democritus; of a philosophic **principle**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 312.}}
**II.2** in Art, **model, standard**, ὁ κ., a statue by Polyclitus which furnished a model of proportions, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 34.55}}; also his treatise on the same, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.122 (adnot.)}}; also in Literature, Ἡρόδοτος τῆς Ἰάδος ἄριστος κ., Θουκυδίδης δὲ τῆς Ἀτθίδος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 3.}}
**II.2.c** of a person, **severe critic**, κ. **scriptorum**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Familiares” 16.17.1.}}
**II.3** Grammars, **general rule,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|AB 1180, 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus}}**inTheod.**2 p.xxi; **paradigm**, οἱ κ. τῶν ὀνομάτων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 141.25.}}
**II.3.b** metrical **scheme** showing all possible forms of a verse, {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 14.1, +others}}
**II.4** in Astronomy and Chronology, **table** of dates, κανόνες Χρονικοί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 27}}; _singular_, κανών, ὁ, **system of chronology**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.74.}}
**II.4.b** astrological **table**, κανόνων καὶ εἰσόδων πήξεις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 108.19.}}
**II.5** **limit, boundary**, explanation as τὸ μέτρον τοῦ πηδήματος, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.151.}}
**II.5.b** '**province', sphere of action**, {{NT|NT.2Cor.10.15.}}
**II.6** **assessment** for taxation, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.99.5 (4th c.AD)}}, etc.; οἱ δεσποτικοὶ κ. the Imperial **taxes**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. work] 234.9 (4th c.AD)}}; ἰδιωτικὸς κ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 2124.10 (4th c.AD).}}
**II.7** **tariff,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Stud.Pal. 20.143.5 (5th-6th c.AD).}}

2585:
** κᾰπηλ-εύω**,
**to be a retail-dealer, drive a petty trade**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.155, 2.35, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.1, Nymphodorus Historicus 21, “IG” 11(2).161 A 16 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), “BGU” 1024 vii 23 (4th c.AD)}}; δι᾽ ἀψύχου βορᾶς σίτοις καπήλευ᾽ **drive a trade, chaffer** with your vegetable food, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 953.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **sell by retail**, τὸν ἕρπιν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 51.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, κ. τὰ πρήγματα, of Darius, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.89}}; κ. τὰ μαθήματα **sell** learning **by retail, hawk** it **about**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313d; κ. τὸν λόγον τοῦ θεοῦ NT.2Cor.2.17}}; so ἔοικεν οὐ καπηλεύσειν μάχην will not **peddle in** war, i. e. fight half-heartedly, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 545; κ. τῇ Χάριτι τὴν ἀμοιβήν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 39}}; κ. τὴν πολιτείαν **traffic in** grants of citizenship, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 60.17}}; κ. τῆς ὥρας ἄνθος or τὴν ὥραν, of prostitutes, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.394, 576}}; εἰρήνην πρὸς Ῥωμαίους Χρυσίου κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.7.9}}; τύχη καπηλεύουσα.. τὸν βίον **playing tricks with** life, **corrupting** it, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.180 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus).}}

2586:
** καπνός**, ὁ,
**smoke**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.317, etc.; κνισάεντι καπνῷ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).66; καπνῷ πυρός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 497}}; **spray**, καπνοῦ καὶ κύματος ἐκτὸς ἔεργε νῆα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.219 (hence _metaphorically_, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.47): proverbial, καπνοῦ σκιά}}, of things worth nothing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 399, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 946; τἄλλ᾽ ἐγὼ καπνοῦ σκιᾶς οὐκ ἂν πριαίμην [prev. author] “Ant.” 1170; also περὶ καπνοῦ στενολεσχεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 320; κ. καὶ φλυαρία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 581d: and in _plural_, γραμμάτων καπνοί}} learned **trifles**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 954; καπνοὺς.. καὶ σκιάς 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 51}}; nickname of a man, {{Refs|[prev. author] 122: _metaphorically_ also of envy, ὕδωρ καπνῷ φέρειν}} to throw water **on the smoking embers**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.24: proverbial, ἐς αὐτὸ τὸ πῦρ ἐκ τοῦ καπνοῦ βιαζόμενος}} 'out of the frying-pan into the fire', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 4, +others}}
**II** **fumitory, Fumaria officinalis**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Anonymus Londinensis 36.58, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.109.}}

2588:
** καρδί-α**, ἡ,
_Ionic dialect_ **καρδίη**, _Epic dialect_ **κρᾰδίη** (καρδίη in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in καρδίῃ ἄλληκτον πολεμίζειν ἠδὲ μάχεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.452, +others, καρδία}} always in Trag., except in some dact. and _anapaest meter_ verses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 881, “Th.” 781, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 99, “Hipp.” 1274)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **κάρζα** {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 407.21}} (but καρδία {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.6); 5th c.AD: Cypria}} **κορζία** (Paph.), {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (perhaps κόρζα)}}:—**heart**, ἐν δέ τέ οἱ κραδίη μεγάλα στέρνοισι πατάσσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.282; κραδίη δέ μοι ἔξω στηθέων ἐκθρῴσκει, of one panic-stricken, 10.94}}; πηδᾷ ἡ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1391 (Lyric poetry)}}: especially as the seat of feeling and passion, as rage or anger, οἰδάνεται κραδίη Χόλῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.646; τέτλαθι δή, κραδίη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.18, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 837}}; καρδίης πλέως full of **heart**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 58.4}}; of fear or courage, κυνὸς ὄμματ᾽ ἔχων, κραδίην δ᾽ ἐλάφοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.225}}; [σφηκῶν] κραδίην καὶ θυμὸν ἔχοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|16.266; ἐν μέν οἱ κραδίῃ θάρσος βάλε 21.547, etc.; ὀρχεῖται καρδία φόβῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 166; θερμὴν ἐπὶ ψυχροῖσι κ. ἔχεις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 88}}; τὸν νέον τίνα οἴει κ. ἴσχει; what do you think are his **feelings?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 492c}}; of sorrow or joy, ἐν κραδίῃ μέγα πένθος ἄεξε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.489; κ. καὶ θυμὸς ἰάνθη 4.548; ἄχος κραδίην καὶ θυμὸν ἵκανεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.171, compare 10.10, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.85, etc.; καρδίην ἰαίνεται 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 36}}; κελαινόχρως.. πάλλεταί μου κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 785; ὦ τάλαινα κ. ψυχή τ᾽ ἐμή 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 466}}; of love, Sapph.[same place], etc.; ἐκ τῆς κ. φιλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 86}}; φιλέειν ἀπὸ κ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.4 (but ἐρεῖν τἀπὸ κ}}. to speak **freely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 475)}}; λαλῆσαι ἐπὶ καρδίαν τινός speak **kindly** to.., {{LXX|LXX.Judg.19.3.}}
**2** **inclination, desire, purpose**, ἔμ᾽ ὀτρύνει κραδίη καὶ θυμός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.220}}; πρόφρων κ. ἐν πάντεσσι πόνοισι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 244; καρδίας δ᾽ ἐξίσταμαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1105.}}
**3** **mind**, ὡς ἄνοον κραδίην ἔχες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.441; κραδίη πόρφυρε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.572; κραδίη προτιόσσετ᾽ ὄλεθρον 5.389; εἰ θεάσῃ τοῖς τῆς καρδίας ὀφθαλμοῖς “Corpus Hermeticum” 4.11}}, compare 7.2; διαλογισμοὶ ἀναβαίνουσι ἐν τῇ κ. {{NT|NT.Luke.24.38.}}
**II** **cardiac orifice of the stomach**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.72, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.338, +others}}
**III** **heart in wood, pith**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.14.1}}; ={ἐντεριώνη},{{Refs|[prev. work] 1.2.6; ἀρτεμισίας μονοκλώνου καρδίας ζ “PMag.Berol.” 1.245, compare “PMag. Leid.V.” 13.24; λαβὼν βάϊν Χλωρὰν καὶ τῆς κ. κρατήσας σχίσον εἰς δύο “PMag.Leid.W.” 6.51.}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, κ. θαλάσσης **depths** of the sea, {{LXX|LXX.Eze.27.4.}}
**V** Κ. Λέοντος, name of the star {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Regulus”, 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 3.5. (I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **[kcirc]ṛd-**, cf. Latin _cor(d)-_, Lithuanian _širdis_ 'heart', etc.)

2590:
** καρπός**, ὁ,
**A** **fruit**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus (only in _sing._)}}, usually of **the fruits of the earth, corn**, ἀρούρης κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.142; κ. δ᾽ ἔφερε ζείδωρος ἄρουρα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 117; κ. Δήμητρος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193, etc.; Δηοῦς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 515}}; κ. ἀρούρης, also of wine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.246}}; ἀμπέλινος κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.212}}; so κ. alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1119 (codices and Scholia)}}; but of corn, opposed to Βάκχιον νᾶμα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ec.” 14}}; καρποῦ ξυγκομιδή **harvest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.15}}; κ. λωτοῖο, κρανείης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.94, 10.242}}; μελιηδέα κ., of grapes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.568; κ. ἐλαίας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.35}}; τὸν ἐπέτειον κ. the **crops** of the year, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 470b}}: generally, **produce**, κ. ὑγρός, of honey, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.20}}; also κ. εὐανθὴς μήλων, of wool, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 2.22: _plural_, καρπῶν ἐστερήθητε διξῶν}} robbed of two years' **produce**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.142; καρπῶν ἀτελεῖς [prev. author] 6.46}}; κ. ὑγροὶ καὶ ξηροί **produce** of trees and fields, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 5.20}}; ξύλινοι, σιτικοὶ κ., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.4.2}}; of **fruits** offered in sacrifice, {{Refs|“BMus.Inscription” 975.7 (from Amathus), compare κάρπωσις 11}}; also of **taxes paid in kind**, opposed to Χρυσικά, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.47.5 (3rd c.BC), +others}}
**A.2** **seed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 16.12}}; defined as **seed with seed-vessel**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.2.1.}}
**A.3** of children, Δῖοι κ. **offspring** of Zeus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 922 (Lyric poetry).}}
**A.II** **returns, profits**, οἱ κ. οἱ ἐκ τῶν ἀγελῶν γενόμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.2}}; τῶν ἀνηλωμένων.. τοὺς κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.29.}}
**A.III** of actions, **fruit, profit**, εἰ κ. ἔσται θεσφάτοισι Λοξίου if his oracles shall bear **fruit**, i.e. be fulfilled, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 618}}; γλώσσης ματαίας κ., i. e. curses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eu.” 831 codices; ὁμιλίας κακῆς κάκιον οὐδέν, κ. οὐ κομιστέος [prev. author] “Th.” 600; οὐκ ἐξάγουσι καρπὸν οἱ ψευδεῖς λόγοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 834, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260d: frequently in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, κ. ἐπέων οὐ κατέφθινε}}, i. e. poesy, {{Refs|“I.” 8(7).50}}; κ. φρενῶν wisdom, {{Refs|“P.” 2.74}}; κ. φρενός, of his own ode, {{Refs|“O.” 7.8}}; ἥβας κ., of the **bloom** of youth,{{Refs|[prev. work] 6.58, “P.” 9.109}}; later, **reward, profit**, ἐπιτηδευμάτων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 27}}; ὅπου ὁ κίνδυνος μέγας, καὶ ὁ κ. {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 27; κ. νίκης 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.3.6: frequently in “NT”, κ. εἰρηνικὸς δικαιοσύνης NT.Heb.12.11, etc.}} (Cf. Latin _carpo_, Engl. **harvest.**)

2594:
** καρτερ-έω**,
**to be steadfast, patient**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1274, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 112, etc.; ῥᾷον παραινεῖν ἢ παθόντα καρτερεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1078, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.64; κ. μάχῃ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 837; κ. ἐλπίδι τινός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44: frequently with a _preposition_, κ. πρός τι}} **to hold up** against a thing, e.g. πρὸς ἡδονάς τε καὶ λύπας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556c; πρὸς λιμὸν καὶ ῥῖγος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.13; ἐπὶ τοῖς παροῦσι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.48, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 194a}}; κ. ἐν ταῖς ἡδοναῖς **to be patient** or **temperate** in.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 635c; ἐν πολέμῳ [prev. author] “La.” 193a}}; κ. ἀπὸ τοῦ ὕπνου **refrain** therefrom, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 13.13}}: with _participle_, **persevere in** doing, οἱ δ᾽ ἐκαρτέρουν πρὸς κῦμα λακτίζοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1395; κ. ἀναλίσκων ἀργύριον φρονίμως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 192e; ἀκούων 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.241; κ. ἐν ἐπιτηδεύμασιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.32}}; also τὰ δείν᾽ ἐκαρτέρουν **was** strangely **obdurate** or **obstinate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 650}}: in later Prose meaning little more than **wait**, καρτέρει καὶ θεώρει **wait** and see, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.2Mac.7.17; οὐ κ. μέχρι θαλάμων ἐλθεῖν 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.291.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _of things_, **bear patiently, endure**, τὰ δ᾽ ἀδύναθ᾽ ἡμῖν καρτερεῖν οὐ ῥᾴδιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1370; κ. θεοῦ δόσιν [prev. author] Alcaeus1071; τῷ σώματι τὰ συντυγχάνοντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.7; τὸν τῶν ὑπεροπτικῶν ὄγκον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.30; πολλὴν κακοπάθειαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1278b27:}} —_passive_, κεκαρτέρηται τἀμά my **time for patience is over**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1457.--In 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, οὐ καρτεριάδδει· οὐ φρόνιμος εἶ, should probably be οὐ καρτερίδδει (_Laconian dialect_ for καρτερίζει).}}

2595:
** κάρφος**, εος, τό,
**any small dry body**, especially **dry stalk**, as of the **dry sticks** of cinnamon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.111}}; of **rice-straw**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 4.3.32, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 33}}: generally, in _plural_, **dry twigs, chips, straws, bits of wool**, such as birds make their nests of, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 643, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 32, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 612b23, “Anthologia Graeca” 10.14 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus): collectivelyin _sing._, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 24, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 560b8, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.187c}}: in _singular_, **chip** of wood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 249}}; **toothpick**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.22: proverbial, κινοῦσα μηδὲ κ}}. 'not stirring an inch', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 474, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.67; οὐδὲ κ. ἐβλάβη “epigram Gr.” 980.9 (from Philae)}}; ἀπὸ τῆς κύλικος κάρφος τῷ μικρῷ δακτύλῳ ἀφαιρετεῖν Ion Hist.1.
**II** = Latin _festuca_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.550b.}}
**III** **a small piece of wood** on which the watchword was written, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.36.3.}}
**IV** in _plural_, **ripe fruit**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 230, 491, “Th.” 893, 941.}}
**V** ={τῆλις}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.102. (σκάρφος is variant (perhaps right)}} in A.[same place], Plb.[same place]: perhaps _cognate_ with Engl. **sharp.**)

2596:
** κατά** [κᾰτᾰ],
_poetry_ καταί _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 309.28, found in Compds., as καταιβάτης}}: _preposition_ with _genitive_ or _accusative_:— **downwards.**
**A** WITH GEN.,
**A.I** denoting motion from above, **down from**, βῆ δὲ κατ᾽ Οὐλύμποιο καρήνων, κατ᾽ Ἰδαίων ὀρέων, βαλέειν κ. πέτρης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.187, 16.677, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.399; κατ᾽ οὐρανοῦ εἰλήλουθας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.128}}; καθ᾽ ἵππων ἀΐξαντε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 232; δάκρυα.. κ. βλεφάρων Χαμάδις ῥέε 17.438; ἵεις σαυτὸν κ. τοῦ τείχους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 355; ἁλόμενοι κ. τῆς πέτρας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.17; κ. τῶν πετρῶν ὦσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229c; κ. κρημνῶν ῥιφέντες [prev. author] “Lg.” 944a:}} — for κατ᾽ ἄκρης see.{ἄκρα}: Μοῖσα κ. στόματος Χέε νέκταρ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.82 (but perhaps in sense 11.1).}}
**A.II** denoting **downward motion**,
**A.II.1** **down upon** or **over**, κ. Χθονὸς ὄμματα πήξας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.217}}; of the dying, κατὰ.. ὀφθαλμῶν κέχυτ᾽ ἀχλύς a cloud settled **upon** the eyes, {{Refs|5.696, compare 20.321; τὸν δὲ κατ᾽ ὀφθαλμῶν.. νὺξ ἐκάλυψεν 13.580}}; φᾶρος κὰκ κεφαλῆς εἴρυσσε **down over.. **, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.85}}; [κόπρος] κ. σπείους κέχυτο.. πολλή {{Refs|9.330}}; ὕδωρ κ. Χειρός, see at {Χεί; μύρον κ. τῆς κεφαλῆς καταχέαντες} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398a; νάρκη μου κ. τῆς Χειρὸς καταχεῖται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 713; κ. τῆς τραπέζης καταπάσας τέφραν [prev. author] “Nu.” 177}}; ξαίνειν κ. τοῦ νώτου πολλὰς [πληγάς] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.197; ἐσκεδασμένοι κ. τῆς Χώρας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.17.10; οἱ κ. νώτου πονοῦντες [prev. author] 3.19.7; ῥόπαλον ἤλασα κὰκ κεφαλῆς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.256}}; κ. κόρρης παίειν, ={ἐπὶ κόρρης}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 12, +others}}
**A.II.1.b** Geometry texts, **along, upon**, πίπτειν κατ᾽ [εὐθείας] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1 “Def.” 2}} αἱ γωνίαι κ. κύκλων περιφερειῶν ἐνεχθήσονται will move **on.. **, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work]1.23, +others,compare 3rd c.BC: Aristarchus Samius Astronomus 1.}}
**A.II.2** **down into**, νέκταρ στάξε κ. ῥινῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.39}}; of a dart, κ. γαίης ᾤχετο {{Refs|13.504, etc.; ἔθηκε κατ᾽ ὄχθης μείλινον ἔγχος 21.172; ψυχὴ κ. Χθονὸς ᾤχετο 23.100}}; κ. γᾶς **under**ground, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.59}}; κατ᾽ ὕδατος **under** water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.149}}; [ποταμὸς] δὺς κ. τῆς γῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phd.” 113c, compare “Ti.” 25d; κ. γῆς σύμεναι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1007 (_anapaest meter_)}}; κ. Χθονὸς κρύψαι to bury. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 24}}; ὁ κ. γῆς one dead and buried, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.5; οἱ κ. Χθονὸς θεοί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 689, etc.; θεοὶ[οἱ] κ. γᾶς [prev. author] “Ch.” 475 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; so κ. θαλάσσης ἀφανίζεσθαι, καταδεδυκέναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6, 235}}; also βᾶτε κατ᾽ ἀντιθύρων go **down by** or **through.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1433.}}
**A.II.3** later, **towards** a point, τοξεύειν κ. σκοποῦ to shoot **at**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.7.8; κατ᾽ ἰχνῶν τινος ὁδεύειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 9.}}
**A.II.4** of vows or oaths, **by**, καθ᾽ ἡμῶν ὀμνύναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.26, compare 54.38; ἐπιορκήσασα κ. τῶν παίδων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.13}}; especially of the victims, etc., **over** which the oath is taken, ὀμνυόντων τὸν ἐπιχώριον ὅρκον καθ᾽ ἱερῶν τελείων Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.47, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 29.5, “Foed.Delph.Pell.” 1 A 9, etc.; κ. τῶν νικητηρίων εὐξάμενοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 1.16}}; also κατ᾽ ἐξωλείας ὀμνύναι to imprecate destruction on oneself, {{Refs|[prev. author] 21.119; κατ᾽ ἐξ. ἐπιορκεῖν [prev. author] 57.22.}}
**A.II.4.b** to make a vow **towards**, i.e. make a vow of offering.., κ. Χιλίων εὐχὴν ποιήσασθαι Χιμάρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 660.}}
**A.II.5** in hostile sense, **against**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 221, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 304, etc.; κ. πάντων φύεσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.19}}; especially of judges giving sentence **against** a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 198, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 449, etc.; ψεύδεσθαι κατά τινος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.7; λέγειν κατά τινος κακά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 65, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.2, etc.}}; of speeches, [λόγος] κ. Μειδίου, etc. (opposed to πρὸς Λεπτίνην, in reply to L.); δῶρα εἰληφέναι κατά τινος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.6, compare 18.}}
**A.II.6** of Time,**for**, μισθοῦν κ. εἴκοσι ἐτῶν {{Refs|“IG” 12.94.37}}; κ. βίου **for** life, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.50; κὰπ παντὸς Χρόνοι “IG” 9(2).517.20 (from Larissa) (but κ. παντὸς τοῦ Χρόνου σκέψασθε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.72 falls under 7)}}; κ. παντὸς τοῦ αἰῶνος ἀείμνηστον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 7.}}
**A.II.7** **in respect of, concerning**, μὴ κατ᾽ ἀνθρώπων σκόπει μόνον τοῦτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 70d; κ. τῶν ἄλλων τεχνῶν τοιαῦτα εὑρήσομεν [prev. author] “Sph.” 253b}}; οἱ κ. Δημοσθένους ἔπαινοι praises **bestowed on** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.50}}; ἐρεῖν or λέγειν κατά τινος to say **of** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 37b, “Prt.” 323b, etc.; εἰ κ. θηλείας φαίης 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 198.10; εἴπερ ἕν γέ τι ζητεῖς κ. πάντων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 73d, compare 74b; ὅπερ εἴρηται καθόλου κ. πασῶν τῶν πολιτειῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1307b2}}; frequently in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, κατά τινος λέγεσθαι or κατηγορεῖσθαι}} to be predicated **of.., **{{Refs|“Int.” 16b10, “Cat.” 1b10, etc.}}; καταφῆσαί (or ἀποφῆσαί) τι κατά τινος to affirm (or deny) **of.., Metaphorical**{{Refs|1007b21}}; so κ. τινὸς ὑπάρχειν {{Refs|“Int.” 16b13: and in}} _adverb_ καθόλου (which see).
**B** WITH Acc.,
**B.I** of motion **downwards**, κ. ῥόον **down** stream, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.254, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.33}}; opposed to ἀνὰ τὸν ποταμόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96}}; κ. τὸν ποταμόν, κ. τὸ ὑδάτιον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.194, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229a}}; κατ᾽ οὖρον ἰέναι, ῥεῖν, **down** (i.e. **with**) the wind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 690, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 468}}; κ. πνεῦμα, κατ᾽ ἄνεμον ἵστασθαι **to leeward**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 535a19, 560b13, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.153.}}
**B.I.2** with or without _signification_ of motion, **on, over, throughout** a space, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, καθ᾽ Ἑλλάδα καὶ μέσον Ἄργος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.344}}; κατ᾽ Ἀχαΐδα, κ. Τροίην, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.770, 9.329; κατ᾽ ἠερόεντα κέλευθα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.64}}; κ. πόντον, κῦμα, ὕλην, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.276, 6.136, 3.151; κ. πτόλιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.383}}; κ. ἄστυ, οἶκον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.286, 6.56; κ. ὅμιλον, στρατόν, 3.36, 1.229}}; κ. κλισίας τε νέας τε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 487; πόλεμον κάτα δακρυόεντα 17.512; κ. ὑσμίνην, μόθον, κλόνον, 5.84, 18.159, 16.331; τὸ ὕδωρ κ. τοὺς ταφροὺς ἐχώρει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.16, etc.}} (in later Gr.of motion **to** a place, κ. τὴν Ἰταλίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus 3.1); καθ᾽ Ἑλλάδα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 578; κ. πτόλιν [prev. author] “Th.” 6; αἱ σκηναὶ αἱ κ. τὴν ἀγοράν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.169; τὰ κατ᾽ ἀγροὺς Διονύσια 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.157, etc.; κ. τὸ προάστιον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.54; τύμβον κατ᾽ αὐτόν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 528, compare “Supp.” 869 (Lyric poetry)}}: Geometry texts, **at** a point, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.1, +others}}; τέμνειν [σφαῖραν] κ. κύκλον **in** a circle, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “ψαμμίτης” 1.17}}; also, **in the region of**, οἱ κ. τὸν ἥλιον γινόμενοι ἀστέρες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 12.7: frequently in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in describing the place of a wound, βαλεῖν κ. στῆθος, γαστέρα, etc., 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.108, 16.465, +others; νύξε κ. δεξιὸν ὦμον 5.46; οὔτασε κατ᾽ ἰσχίον 11.339; so βαλεῖν κατ᾽ ἀσπίδα, κ. ζωστῆρα, 5.537, 615}}; βέλος κ. καίριον ἦλθεν struck **upon** a vital part, variant in {{Refs|11.439: _metaphorically_, ἄχος κ. φρένα τύψε 19.125: generally, κ. φρένα καὶ κατὰ θυμόν}} **in** heart and soul, {{Refs|4.163, +others}}
**B.I.3** **opposite, over against**, κ. Σινώπην πόλιν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.76, compare 2.148, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.30, etc.; ἀνὴρ κατ᾽ ἄνδρα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 505; μολὼν.. μοι κ. στόμα [prev. author] “Ch.” 573; κατ᾽ ὀφθαλμούς τινος LXX.2Ki.12.11; οἱ μὲν Ἀθηναῖοι κ. Λακεδαιμονίους ἐγένοντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.2.18; κατ᾽ Ἀχαιοὺς ἀντετάχθησαν [prev. passage]; ἐν συμποσίῳ.., περίμενε, μέχρις ἂν γένηται κατὰ σέ 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 15, compare 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.108.}}
**B.II** **distributively**, of a whole divided into parts, κρῖν᾽ ἄνδρας κ. φῦλα, κ. φρήτρας **by** tribes, **by** clans, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.362}}; κ. σφέας μαχέονται **by** themselves, **separately**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 366, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.64; ἐσκήνουν κ. τάξεις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.25; αὐτὴ καθ᾽ αὑτήν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1013}}; κ. κώμας κατοικημένοι **in separate** villages, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96}}; κατ᾽ ἑωυτοὺς ἕκαστοι ἐτράποντο each **to his own** home, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.15}}; κ. πόλεις ἀποπλεῦσαι, διαλυθῆναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.89, 3.1: στρατιὰ κ. ἕνδεκα μέρη κεκοσμημένη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247a; later οἱ κατ᾽ ἄνδρα λόγοι “PLond.” 2.259.72 (1st c.AD),compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 32.6, etc.; ἡ κατ᾽ οἰκίαν ἀπογραφή “PLond.” 3.904.20 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; κατ᾽ ἔπος word **by** word, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 802}}; κατ᾽ ὄνομα **individually**, {{NT|NT.3John.15, etc.}}; παῖδα κ. κρήνην **at each** fount a boy, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 37.13, compare “POxy” 2108.9 (3rd c.AD).}}
**B.II.2** of Time, καθ᾽ ἡμέραν, κατ᾽ ἦμαρ, day **by** day, daily, see at {ἡμέρα} 111; καθ᾽ ἑνιαυτόν, κατ᾽ ἔτος, {{NT+1st c.AD+|“Test.Epict.” 6.24, NT.Luke.2.41, etc.; κ. μῆνα “POxy.” 275.18 (1st c.AD).}}
**B.II.3** of Numbers, **by** so many **at a time**, καθ᾽ ἕνα one **at a time, individually**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.104 (later τὸ καθ᾽ ἕν}}**detailed list,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 47.34 (2nd c.BC), etc.)}}; κ. μίαν τε καὶ δύο **by** ones and twos, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.113; δύο μνέαι τεταγμέναι κατ᾽ ἄνδρα αἰχμάλωτον ἕκαστον [prev. author] 6.79; ἐκ τῶν συμμάχων ἐξελέγετο κατ᾽ ὀλίγους [prev. author] 8.113; κ. τὰς πέντε καὶ εἴκοσι μνᾶς πεντακοσίας δραχμὰς εἰσφέρειν to pay 500}} drachmae **on every** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|25 minae, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.7; κ. διακοσίας καὶ τριακοσίας ὁμοῦ τι τάλαντον διακεχρημένον}}**in separate sums of** 200{{Refs|and 300 drachmae, [prev. author] 27.11}}; of ships, κ. μίαν (i.e. ναῦν) **in column**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90; κ. μίαν ναῦν ἐπιτάττειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.26.12, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84: Geometry texts, μετρεῖν, μετρεισθαι κατά..}}, measure, be measured a certain number of times, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7 “Def.” 8,9, +others}}; μετρεῖν κ. τὰς ἐν τῷ Β μονάδας **as many times as** there are units in B, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.16.}}
**B.III** of direction **towards** an object or purpose, πλεῖν κ. πρῆξιν **on** a business, **for** or **after** a matter, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.72, 9.253}}; πλάζεσθαι κ. ληΐδα to rove **in search of** booty, {{Refs|3.106}}; κ. ληΐην ἐκπλῶσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.152; ἔβη κ. δαῖτα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.424; ἐπιδημεῖν κατ᾽ ἐμπορίαν “IG” 22.141.32, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 11.1}}; κ. Χρέος τινὸς ἐλθεῖν come to seek his help, consult him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.479, etc.; ἵεται κ. τὴν φωνήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.70}}; κ. θέαν ἥκειν to have come **for the purpose of** seeing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31; κ. πλοῦν ἤδη ὤν [prev. author] 7.31; καθ᾽ ἁρπαγὴν ἐσκεδασμένοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.2}}; κ. τ; **for** what purpose? why? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 239.}}
**B.III.2** of pursuit, κ. πόδας τινὸς ἐλαύνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.89}}; simply κ. τινά **after** him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.84; ἰέναι κ. τοὺς ἄλλους [prev. author] 9.53}}; κατ᾽ ἴχνος **on** the track, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 32, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 695 (Lyric poetry); ὥσπερ κατ᾽ ἴχνη κ. τὰ νῦν εἰρημένα ζῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 115b.}}
**B.III.3** Geometry texts, in adverbial phrases, κ. κάθετον **in the same** vertical line, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “τετραγωνισμὸς παραβολῆς” 6}}; κατ᾽ εὐθεῖάν τινι **in the same** straight line with.., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 58.7.}}
**B.IV** of fitness or conformity, **in accordance with**, κ. θυμόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.136}}; καθ᾽ ἡμέτερον νόον **after** our liking,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|9.108; κ. νόον πρήξωμεν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.97}}; κ. μοῖραν **as is** meet and right, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.286; κατ᾽ αἶσαν, κ. κόσμον, 10.445, 472}}; κ. νόμον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 417; κὰν νόμον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.78; κ. τοὺς νόμους “IG” 22.1227.15}}; αἰτίαν καθ᾽ ἥντινα **for** what cause, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 228}}; κατ᾽ ἔχθραν, κ. φθόνον, **for** (i.e. **because of**) hatred, envy, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 336, “Eu.” 686}}; καθ᾽ ἡδονήν τι δρᾶν, ποιεῖν, do **as** one pleases, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37, 53; κ. τὸ ἔχθος τὸ Θεσσαλῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.30, compare 9.38}}; κ. φιλίαν, κατ᾽ ἔχθος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.60, 103, etc.}}; κατ᾽ ἄλλο μὲν οὐδέν, ὅτι δέ.. **for** no other **reason** but that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229d}}; κ. δύναμιν **to the best of** one's power, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142, etc. (κὰδ δ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 336)}}; κ. τρόπον διοικεῖν arrange **suitably**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.6, +others}}; κατ᾽ εὐνοίην **with** goodwill, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.108; κ. τὰ παρηγγελμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.8, etc.}}; in quotations, **according to**, κατ᾽ Αἰσχύλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 134; κ. Πίνδαρον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 227b, etc.}}
**B.IV.2** **in relation to, concerning**, τὰ κατ᾽ ἀνθρώπους ={τὰ ἀνθρώπινα}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 930, 310; τὰ κ. τὸν Τέλλον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31; τὰ κ. τὴν Κύρου τελευτήν [prev. work] 214}}; τὰ κ. πόλεμον **military** matters, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.181}}; αἱ κ. τὴν πόλιν οἰκονομίαι (opposed to αἱ πολεμικαὶ πράξεις) the management of **public** affairs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.97; τὰ κ. τὰς θυσίας “SIG” 506.7 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}; so τὸ κατ᾽ ὑμέας **as far as concerns** you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.158}}; τὸ κατ᾽ ἐμέ **as far as** I **am concerned**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.247}}; κ. τοῦτο **in** this **respect**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.3, etc.}}; κ. ταὐτά **in** the same **way**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.20}}; καθ᾽ ὅτι **so far as**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.82, etc.}}
**B.IV.3** in Comparisons, **corresponding with, after the fashion of**, κρομύοιο λοπὸν κ. **like** the coat of an onion, uncertain in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.233; μέλος κ. Φοίνισσαν ἐμπολὰν πέμπεται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.67}}; κ. Μιθραδάτην **answering to the description of** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.121}}; τὴν ἰδέαν κ. πνιγέα **like** an oven in appearance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1001}}; κηδεῦσαι καθ᾽ ἑαυτόν to marry **in** one's own **rank** of life, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 890; οὐ κατ᾽ ἄνθρωπον φρονεῖν [prev. author] “Th.” 425; λέγω κατ᾽ ἄνδρα, μὴ θεόν, σέβειν ἐμέ [prev. author] “Ag.” 925}}; οὐ κατὰ σέ none **of** your **sort**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Chionides Comicus 1 (but ἵνα προσείπω σε κατὰ σέ}} to address you **in** your own **style**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467c); τὸ κατ᾽ ἐμὲ καὶ οὐ κατ᾽ ἐμέ 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.28.5; οὐ κ. τὰς Μειδίου λῃτουργίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.169; ἡ βασιλεία κ. τὴν ἀριστοκρατίαν ἐστί 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1310b3}}: frequently after a _comparative_, μέζων ἢ κατ᾽ ἀνθρώπων φύσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.38, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20e, etc.}}; μείζω ἢ κ. δάκρυα too great for tears, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75}}; ἤθεα βαθύτερα ἢ κ. Θρήϊκας morerefined than **was common among** the Thracians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.95.}}
**B.V** **by the favour of** a god, etc., κ. δαίμονα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.28, compare “P.” 8.68; κ. θεῖον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 147 codices (κ. θεὸν Cobet)}}; κ. τύχην τινά {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.24.}}
**B.VI** of round numbers (see. below v11.2), **nearly, about**, κ. Χίλια ἑξακόσια ἔτεα 1600 years **more or less**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.145, compare 6.44, +others}}; κατ᾽ οὐδέν **next to** nothing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 302b.}}
**B.VII** of Time, **during** or **in the course of** a period, κ. τὸν πόλεμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.137}}; καθ᾽ ἡμέραν, κατ᾽ ἦμαρ, **by** day, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 818, “Ag.” 668; κατ᾽ εὐφρόνην [prev. author] “Pers.” 221}}; κ. Χειμῶνα, κ. θερείαν, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PLille” 1r14 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 27.60 (2nd c.BC).}}
**B.VII.2** **about**, κ. τὸν αὐτὸν τοῦτον Χρόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.131, etc.; κ. τοὺς θανάτους τῶν βασιλέων [prev. author] 6.58}}; especially with names of persons, κ. Ἄμασιν βασιλεύοντα **about the time of** Amasis, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.134; κ. τὸν κ. Κροῖσον Χρόνον [prev. author] 1.67}}; οἱ κατ᾽ ἐκεῖνον (i.e. τὸν Ἀλκιβιάδην) ὑμέτεροι πρόγονοι [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.146 (variant{κατ᾽ ἐκ. τὸν Χρόνον}); κ. τοὺς Ἡρακλείδας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 10.8}}]; οἱ καθ᾽ ἑαυτοὺς ἄνθρωποι their contemporaries, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 3.5.10.}}
**B.VII.3** καθ᾽ ἕτος **this** year, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 284.24 (Erythrae, 4th c.BC), “OGI” 458.64 1st c.BC-1st c.AD, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3641b5,38 (from Lampsacus).}}
**B.VIII** periphrastically with abstract _substantive_, κατ᾽ ἡσυχίην, κ. τάχος, ={ἡσύχως, ταχέως}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, 7.178}}; κ. κράτος **by** force, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.19, etc.}}; κ. μέρος **partially**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456a16}}; **individually, severally**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157b, “Lg.” 835a}}; κ. φύσιν **naturally**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 428e}}; κ. τὴν τέχνην **skilfully**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 20.7}}; οὔτ᾽ ἐμοὶ λέγειν καθ᾽ ἡδονήν [ἐστι] it is not **pleasant** for me to tell you, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 263.}}
**C** Position: κατά may follow both its cases, and is then written with _anastrophe_ κάτα, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.221, etc.}}; so also in tmesi, when it follows its Verb, {{Refs|17.91.}}
**D** _absolutely_ as _adverb_. in all the above senses, especially like{κάτω}, **downwards, from above, down**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}
**E** κατά in COMPOSITION (joined with other words),
**E.I** **downwards, down**, as in καταβαίνω, καταβάλλω, κατάκειμαι, καταπέμπω, καταπίπτω, καταπλέω 1.
**E.II** **in answer to, in accordance with**, as in κατᾴδω (**occino**), καταινέω, καταθύμιος.
**E.III** **against**, in hostile sense {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.5), as in καταγιγνώσκω, κατακρίνω, καταψηφίζομαι: more rarely with a}} _substantive_, as καταδίκη.
**E.IV** **back, back again**, as in κάτειμι, καταπορεύομαι, καταπλέω 11.
**E.V** frequently only to strengthen the notion of the simple word, as in κατακόπτω, κατακτείνω, καταφαγεῖν, etc.; also with _Substantives_ and _adjectives_, as in κατάδηλος, κάτοξος.
**E.VI** sometimes to give a _transitive_ force to an _intransitive_ Verb, our **be-**, as in καταθρηνέω **be**wail.
**E.VII** implying **waste** or **consumption**, as in καταλειτουργέω, καθιπποτροφέω, καταζευγοτροφέω: and generally in a **disparaging** sense, as in καταγιγνώσκω 1.
**F** κατά as a _preposition_ was shortened in some dialects, especially in _Epic dialect_, into κάγ, κάδ, κάκ, κάμ, κάν, κάπ, κάρ, κάτ, before γ, δ, κ, μ, ν, π (or φ), ῥ, τ (or θ), respectively; see these forms in their own places. Mss. and the older Edd. join the _preposition_ with the following word, as καγγόνυ, καδδέ, κακκεφαλῆς, καππεδίον, καπφάλαρα, καρρόον, καττάδε, καττόν, etc. In _compound_ Verbs, κατά sometimes changes into καβ, καλ, καρ, κατ, before β, λ, ρ, θ, respectively, as κάββαλε, κάλλιπε, καρρέζουσα, κάτθαν; and before στ, σχ, the second syllable sometimes disappears, as in καστορνῦσα, κάσχεθε, as also in the _Doric dialect_ forms καβαίνων, κάπετον.
** καθεῖς**,
for καθ᾽ εἷς, **one by one, one after antoher**, {{LXX+NT+2nd c.AD+|LXX.3Mac.5.34; εἷς καθεῖς NT.Mark.14.19, etc.: formed backwards from the _neuter_ ἓν καθέν, noted by 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 9.}}

2597:
** καταβαίνω**,
_Doric dialect_ **κᾰβαίνω** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 38}}: _future_ **-βήσομαι**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 750, etc.:}}_perfect_ -βέβηκ; _Boeotian dialect_ _participle_ καταβεβάων {{Refs|“IG” 7.3055}}: _aorist_ κατέβην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.541, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.43, etc.}}; _poetry_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ κατέβαν {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.329, κατέβησαν LXX.2Ki.23.13; _imperative_ κατάβηθι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 873}}, _Laconian dialect_ κάβασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, κατάβᾱ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 979, “Ra.” 35}}; _Epic dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ _subjunctive_ καταβήομεν (variant{-βείομεν}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.97}}; late _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _optative_ κατάβοι {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.2Ki.1.21 (codex B), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Conon Historicus 45.2J.}}; _poetry_ _participle_ καββάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.51}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ καταβήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.19}}:—_middle_, _Epic dialect_ _aorist 1_ κατεβήσετο [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.288, 13.17, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.337, +others (with variant{κατεβήσατο})}}]; _imperative_ καταβήσεο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.109}}:—**go** or **come down from**.., with _genitive_, πόλιος κ. {{Refs|24.329}}; οὐρανόθεν κ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. 11.184; Παρνασοῦ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.43, etc.}}: also with Preps., ἐξ ὄρεος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.17; ἐς πεδίον 3.252, etc.: also with _accusative_ loci, θάλαμον κατεβήσετο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.337}}; κ. Ἀΐδαν, Ἀΐδα δόμον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 822 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 913 (Lyric poetry); but κατέβην δόμον Ἄϊδος εἴσω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.252}} (later _absolutely_, **die**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 38.16)}}; ἔσω κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 750: also with _accusative_ in quite different senses, κατέβαιν᾽ ὑπερώϊα}} she **came down from** the upper floor, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.206, 23.85}}; κλίμακα κατεβήσετο **came down** the ladder,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.330 (κ. κατὰ τῆς κλίμακος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.9)}}; ξεστὸν ἐφόλκαιον καταβάς **having got down by** the lading-plank, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.350: _absolutely_, καταβαίνειν δ᾽ οὐ σχολή}} **come downstairs**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 409}}; especially
**1** **dismount from** a chariot or from horseback, δίφρου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.109; ἐκ τῆς ἁρμαμάξης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.76; ἀφ᾽ ἁρμάτων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.51; ἀπὸ τοῦ ἵππου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.6}}; but κ. ἀπὸ τῶν ἵππων **give up** riding, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.24, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 49.1:}} —hence in _passive_, ἵππος καταβαίνεται the horse **is dismounted from**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 11.7.}}
**2** **go down from the inland parts to the sea**, especially from central Asia {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (ἀναβαίνω 11.3), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94, etc.}}; also from Athens, κ. ἐς Πειραιᾶ, ἐς λιμένα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 327a, “Tht.” 142a.}}
**3** **go down into the scene of contest**, γυμνὸν ἐπὶ στάδιον κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.49}}; κ. ἐπ᾽ αὐτὸ τοῦτο (i.e. τὸ ἀεθλεύειν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.22}}: _absolutely_, = Latin _in certamen descendere_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.42, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 504 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.27}}; compare καταβατέο; μέτρῳ καταβαίνειν 'seek no more contests' (μέτρῳ by litotes for μή), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.78}}; μεθ᾽ ὅπλων κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 834c.}}
**4** of an orator, **come down from the tribune**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.92, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.23, etc.; rarely in full, κ. ἀπὸ τοῦ βήματος [prev. work]113; so κατάβα.--καταβήσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 979}}; later, also κ. ἀπὸ τοῦ λόγου, ἀπὸ τῶν ἰαμβείων, **to cease** from.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 35, “Nec.” 1.}}
**5** less frequently of things, πρὶν.. καταβήμεναι ἐκ Διὸς οὖρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.19}}; of tears, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 111 (eleg.)}}; of streams, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 118d}}; of the womb, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 582b24}}; πόσσω κατέβα τοι ἀφ᾽ ἱστ; at what price did [the robe] **come down** from the loom? {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.35}}; of the heavenly bodies, **set**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 31.3.}}
**II** _metaphorically_,
**II.1** **attain**, πόμπιμον κατέβαινε νόστου τέλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.25; κ. ἐπὶ τελευτήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 511b}}: _absolutely_, **attain one's end**, ἐν φάει κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.38}}; simply, **come** to, **arrive** at in course of speaking, κατέβαινε ἐς λιτάς he **ended** with prayer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116: usually with}}_participle_, κατέβαινε αὖτις παραιτεόμενος{{Refs|[prev. work] 90, compare 118, 9.94}}; καταβάς, of a writer, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.454B.}}
**II.2** κ. εἰς.. **conform to**, εἰς τοὺς Χρόνους κ. τούτους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1335a11.}}
**II.3** **condescend**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 1.2 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**II.4** **fall in value,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 1223.33 (4th c.AD),compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.51.}}
**II.5** λέγεται μηδὲν αὐτοῖς τούτων καταβαίνειν, of abusive language, does not **affect** them or **get home**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.242.}}

2598:
** καταβάλλω**,
_future_ -βᾰλῶ: _aorist_ κατέβᾰλον; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ κάββαλε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.172, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 189, etc.; _imperative_ καββαλόντων “Foed.Delph.Pell.” 1 B 14}}:—**throw down, overthrow**, κατὰ πρηνὲς βαλέειν Πριάμοιο μέλαθρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.414; ἐς μέσσον κ. τι 15.357; ἐνὶ πόντῳ 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 189; ἐπ᾽ ἀκτῆς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.125 (tmesis); ἐπὶ Χθονί 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 462, etc.}}; κ. [τινὰ] ἐνθάδε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.172}}; κ. τὰ οἰκήματα, τὰ ἀγάλματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.17, 8.109; τεῖχος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.24; κ. τινὰ ἀπὸ τοῦ ἵππου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.41; ἀπ᾽ ἐλπίδος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 15e}}; κ. ἐς τὸ μηδέν **to bring down** to nothing, opposed to ἐξᾶραι ὑψοῦ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.79}}; κάββαλλε τὸν Χείμωνα **confound, defy** the storm, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 34.3.}}
**2** κ. ἑαυτόν **throw** oneself **down** to sleep, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 38.}}
**3** **strike down** with a weapon, **slay**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.692 (tmesis), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64, etc.}}; by a blow, κ. πατάξας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.71}}; especially of slaying victims, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1603, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.20; κ. θῦμα δαίμοσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1246.}}
**3.b** _passive_, **to be stricken**, νόσῳ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1121.9 (3rd c.AD).}}
**4** **throw** into prison, κ. τινὰ ἐς ἐρκτήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.146}}: generally, **throw, bring** into a certain state, κ. [τινὰ] ἐς ξυμφοράς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 606, 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 58}}; εἰς ἀπορίαν, εἰς ἀπιστίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 15e, “Phd.” 88c, etc.}}
**5** **overthrow, refute**, οἱ -βάλλοντες (i.e. λόγοι), title of work by Protagoras: κ. τινά {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 125; δόξαν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 6.20.}}
**6** **abuse, bully**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.164S.}}
**7** **cast down** or **away, cast off, reject**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.24}}: _metaphorically_, **forget**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 111}}; κ. εἴς τι **throw away** upon a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 960e:}} —_passive_, οἱ καταβεβλημένοι **despicable fellows**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.8}}; compare καταβεβλημένως.
**II** **let fall, drop**, ἀπὸ ἕο κάββαλεν υἱόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.343; κάββαλε νεβρόν, of an eagle, 8.249}}; of a fawning dog, οὔατα κάββαλεν ἄμφω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.302}}; ἴουλον ἀπὸ κροτάφων κ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.85}}; of sails, καθ᾽ ἱστία λευκὰ βαλόντες {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 671; τἀκάτια 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 10}}; κατ᾽ ὀφθαλμοὺς βαλεῖ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 574}}; τὰς ὀφρῦς κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 167}}; κ. τὰ κέρατα **droop** their feelers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 590b26}}: in Politics, **abandon** a measure, καταβάλλοντ᾽ ἐᾶν ἐν ὑπωμοσίᾳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.103.}}
**II.2** **lay down, set down**, κρεῖον μέγα κάββαλεν ἐν πυρὸς αὐγῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.206, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 165, “V.” 727, etc.}}
**II.3** **lay down, lay in** stores, κ. σιτία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.25:}} —_passive_, κὰτ ἄσπιδες βεβλήμεναι **stored up**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 15.5.}}
**II.4** **pay down, yield, bring in**, ἡ λίμνη καταβάλλει ἐπ᾽ ἡμέρην ἑκάστην τάλαντον ἐκ τῶν ἰχθύων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.149; τὰς ἐπικαρπίας τῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.92, cf. Legal ib. 93.}}
**II.4.b** **pay**, τἀργύριον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.27; τριώβολον 5th-6th c.BC: Amipsias Comicus 13; ἀρραβῶνα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 743, compare “PRev.Laws” 48.10 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; τιμήν τινι ὑπέρ τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 932d, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 25; τέλη “GDI” 5018a17 (from Gortyn), “PHib.” 29.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; λύτρα {{Refs|“GDI” 5151.8 (Cretan dialect, found at Delphi)}}; καταβαλών σοι δραχμὴν τῶν βοτρύων for them, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Her.Praef.” 1}}; κ. ζημίαν **pay up, discharge** a fine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.83, compare 59.27}}:—later in _middle_, μισθὸν καταβαλέσθαι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.12.}}
**II.5** **put in, deposit**, in _passive_, εἰ ἡ μαρτυρία κατεβάλλετο ἐνταῦθα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.46}}:—but usually in _middle_, **deposit**, γράμματα εἰς κιβωτόν {{Refs|“BCH” 25.100 (from Tlos), compare “IG” 12(1).3.15 (from Rhodes)}}; ψευδεῖς γραφὰς εἰς τὰ δημόσια γράμματα Docum. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.55; λόγους “IG” 7.2850 (from Haliartus)}}; δόγμα {{Refs|“GDI” 5182.10 (Cretan dialect, found at Teos).}}
**II.6** **throw down** seed, **sow**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 37}}, compare καταβλητέο; κ. τὸ σπέρμα, of the male, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 908.1}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Placita Philosophorum” 5.7.4, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.33, 1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 4.14: _metaphorically_, σπέρμα κ. τοιούτων πραγμάτων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.154}}; κ. φάτιν ὡς.. **spread abroad** a rumour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.122, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 758 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.7** **lay down as a foundation**, mostly in _middle_, τὴν τῆς ναυπηγίας ἀρχὴν καταβαλλόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 803a: especially _metaphorically_, -βαλλομένα μέγαν οἶκτον}} **beginning** a lament {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare(below 8), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 164 (Lyric poetry); Ἀρίστιππος τὴν Κυρηναϊκὴν φιλοσοφίαν κατεβάλετο 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.3.22; καταβαλέσθαι τοὐπτάνιον 3rd c.BC(?): Sosipater Comicus 1.39; ἐξ ἀρχῆς καινὴν νομοθεσίαν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.20; τὴν Στωϊκῶν αἵρεσιν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.329a}}: hence generally, **to be the author of, commit to writing**, ἱστορικὰς καταβαλόμενοι πραγματείας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.1}}; λόγον Darius cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 9.13; φλυαρίας 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.476}}:— _passive_, ὅταν δὲ κρηπὶς μὴ καταβληθῇ.. ὀρθῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1261: frequently _metaphorically_, δεδημοσιωμένα που καταβέβληται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 232d; πολλοὶ λόγοι πρὸς αὐτὰ -βέβληνται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1096a10}}; καταβεβλημέναι μαθήσεις **fundamental, established**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1337b21}}; τὰ κ. παιδεύματα{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 1338a36, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.27S.}}
**II.8** with _infinitive_, γάμον καταβάλλομ᾽ ἀείδειν I **begin** my song of, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 196.}}
**III** _passive_, **lie down**, εἰς εὐνάν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.11.}}
**III.2** like{καταβαίνω} {{Refs|II.1}}**arrive** at in a course of lectures, εἰς Γοργίαν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “Vita Isidori” 54.}}
**B** _intransitive_, **fall**, εἰς φθόνον καὶ ἀπορίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 344c.}}
